window.REV_FLIP=window.REV_FLIP||{};window.REV_FLIP["docker"]=[{"id":"docker-f0001","card":"q-docker-001-docker-vs-a-virtual-machine","sec":"docker-s002","q":"What is Docker vs a VM?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Docker</strong>: lightweight containers that share the host OS kernel. Fast.</li>\n  <li><strong>VM</strong>: full OS emulation. Heavy and slower.</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0002","card":"q-docker-001-docker-vs-a-virtual-machine","sec":"docker-s002","q":"What are the advantages of Docker over virtualization?","a":"<p>Lightweight, fast, efficient, portable, scalable.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0003","card":"q-docker-001-docker-vs-a-virtual-machine","sec":"docker-s002","q":"What is containerization, Docker's take?","a":"<p>The app and its dependencies packaged in one unit. Docker uses namespaces, cgroups and a union file system.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0004","card":"q-docker-001-docker-vs-a-virtual-machine","sec":"docker-s002","q":"What problem does Docker solve?","a":"<p>Consistency across dev, test and prod. No \"works on my machine\" issues.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0005","card":"q-docker-001-docker-vs-a-virtual-machine","sec":"docker-s002","q":"Why does a Docker container start in about a second while a VM takes much longer?","a":"<p>A container is an isolated process on the host kernel. There is no guest OS to boot, no hypervisor-emulated hardware and no separate kernel to initialize. A VM boots a full OS on virtual hardware first, so it needs more time, memory and disk.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0006","card":"q-docker-001-docker-vs-a-virtual-machine","sec":"docker-s002","q":"What do you give up in isolation when you choose containers over VMs?","a":"<p>Containers share the host kernel, so a kernel vulnerability or a container breakout can affect the host and every other container. A VM has its own kernel behind a hypervisor, which is a stronger boundary. For hostile multi-tenant workloads, run containers inside VMs, use a sandboxed runtime (gVisor, Kata) or use separate VMs per tenant.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0007","card":"q-docker-001-docker-vs-a-virtual-machine","sec":"docker-s002","q":"Which Linux kernel features make containers possible?","a":"<p>Namespaces give each container its own view of PIDs, network, mounts, hostname, IPC and users. Cgroups limit and account CPU, memory, I/O and process counts. A union/overlay file system provides the layered root file system. Docker packages these into one workflow.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0008","card":"q-docker-001-docker-vs-a-virtual-machine","sec":"docker-s002","q":"Can you run a Windows container on a Linux host, or a Linux container directly on macOS?","a":"<p>No, a container uses the host kernel, so the container OS must match the host kernel family. Docker Desktop on macOS and Windows runs a lightweight Linux VM and starts Linux containers inside it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0009","card":"q-docker-001-docker-vs-a-virtual-machine","sec":"docker-s002","q":"When would you still pick a VM over a container?","a":"<p>When you need a different kernel or OS than the host, strong isolation between untrusted tenants, a full OS with its own services and kernel modules, or when you run legacy software that expects a full machine. Many teams run containers on top of VMs (for example Docker on EC2) to get both.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0010","card":"q-docker-001-docker-vs-a-virtual-machine","sec":"docker-s002","q":"A teammate says \"a container is just a lightweight VM\". What is wrong with that?","a":"<p>A container has no kernel and no virtual hardware. It is a normal process (or process tree) on the host that is isolated by namespaces and limited by cgroups. You can see its processes with <code>ps</code> on the host.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0011","card":"q-docker-002-docker-architecture","sec":"docker-s003","q":"What are the key Docker architecture components?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Daemon</strong>: manages Docker objects.</li>\n  <li><strong>Client</strong>: the CLI.</li>\n  <li><strong>Images</strong>: templates.</li>\n  <li><strong>Containers</strong>: running instances.</li>\n  <li><strong>Registry</strong>: image store.</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0012","card":"q-docker-002-docker-architecture","sec":"docker-s003","q":"Container vs image?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Container</strong>: a running instance.</li>\n  <li><strong>Image</strong>: a static blueprint.</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0013","card":"q-docker-002-docker-architecture","sec":"docker-s003","q":"Image layer vs container layer?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Image</strong>: a read-only stack of layers.</li>\n  <li><strong>Container</strong>: a writable top layer.</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0014","card":"q-docker-002-docker-architecture","sec":"docker-s003","q":"What is the default storage driver and why does it matter?","a":"<p><strong>overlay2</strong> (the Linux default). It affects performance, storage and compatibility.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0015","card":"q-docker-002-docker-architecture","sec":"docker-s003","q":"How does the Docker CLI talk to the Docker daemon?","a":"<p>The CLI sends REST API calls to the daemon (<code>dockerd</code>). By default this goes over the Unix socket <code>/var/run/docker.sock</code>. It can also use TCP, ideally with TLS, or SSH. The CLI itself does no real work, so the same client can control a remote daemon with <code>DOCKER_HOST</code> or <code>docker context</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0016","card":"q-docker-002-docker-architecture","sec":"docker-s003","q":"In Docker, why is mounting <code>/var/run/docker.sock</code> into a container considered dangerous?","a":"<p>Anyone who can reach the socket can ask the daemon to start containers, including privileged ones that mount the host root file system. That is effectively root on the host. Avoid it, or use a socket proxy with limited API access, or rootless mode.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0017","card":"q-docker-002-docker-architecture","sec":"docker-s003","q":"What happens step by step when you run <code>docker run nginx</code> and the image is not on the machine?","a":"<p>The CLI calls the daemon. The daemon looks for the image locally, pulls its layers from the registry (Docker Hub by default) if missing, creates a container with a writable layer, network and mounts, then starts it through containerd and runc, which set up namespaces and cgroups and run the process.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0018","card":"q-docker-002-docker-architecture","sec":"docker-s003","q":"What are containerd and runc in the Docker stack?","a":"<p><code>containerd</code> is the high-level runtime that manages images, container lifecycle and snapshots for <code>dockerd</code>. <code>runc</code> is the low-level OCI runtime that actually creates the container process using namespaces and cgroups. Kubernetes can use containerd directly without the Docker daemon.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0019","card":"q-docker-002-docker-architecture","sec":"docker-s003","q":"In Docker, why are image layers read-only and shared between containers?","a":"<p>Sharing means ten containers from one image store the base layers once, which saves disk and speeds up pulls and starts. Each container gets only its own thin writable layer. When a container changes a file from a lower layer, the file is copied up into the writable layer first (copy-on-write).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0020","card":"q-docker-002-docker-architecture","sec":"docker-s003","q":"Why is <code>myapp:1.0</code> not a safe way to pin an exact image in Docker?","a":"<p>A tag is a mutable label that can be moved to a different image. The image ID or the registry digest (<code>myapp@sha256:...</code>) identifies the exact content. Pin by digest when you need reproducible deployments.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0021","card":"q-docker-002-docker-architecture","sec":"docker-s003","q":"In Docker, what is the difference between an image and a container?","a":"<p>An image is a read-only, layered template that holds the file system and default metadata (command, env, ports). A container is a running or stopped instance of an image, with its own thin writable layer, process, network and mounts. One image can start many containers, much like a class and its objects.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0022","card":"q-docker-002-docker-architecture","sec":"docker-s003","q":"What is the difference between <code>docker save</code>/<code>docker load</code> and <code>docker export</code>/<code>docker import</code>?","a":"<p><code>docker save</code> writes one or more images to a tar archive with all layers, tags and history, and <code>docker load</code> restores them, which suits moving images to an air-gapped host. <code>docker export</code> dumps a container's flattened file system to a tar without layers, history, metadata (CMD, ENV) or volumes, and <code>docker import</code> turns that tar into a new single-layer image.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0023","card":"q-docker-002-docker-architecture","sec":"docker-s003","q":"Why is building images with a Dockerfile preferred over <code>docker commit</code> in Docker?","a":"<p><code>docker commit</code> snapshots a container's changes into an image, so the steps are not recorded, cannot be reviewed or reproduced, and hidden changes such as caches come along. A Dockerfile is versioned text, rebuilds the same image on any machine and benefits from layer caching. <code>commit</code> is acceptable only for quick debugging snapshots.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0024","card":"q-docker-003-run-vs-start-vs-exec","sec":"docker-s004","q":"What is the difference between <code>docker run</code>, <code>start</code> and <code>exec</code>?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>run</strong>: creates a new container and starts it.</li>\n  <li><strong>start</strong>: restarts a stopped container.</li>\n  <li><strong>exec</strong>: runs a command inside a running container.</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0025","card":"q-docker-003-run-vs-start-vs-exec","sec":"docker-s004","q":"In Docker, you run <code>docker run -d --name web nginx</code> twice. What happens and how do you avoid leftovers in throwaway jobs?","a":"<p>The second call fails with a name conflict because <code>run</code> always creates a new container and the name <code>web</code> is taken. Without <code>--name</code> you would get a second container with a random name. For one-off jobs use <code>--rm</code> so the container is deleted when it exits.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0026","card":"q-docker-003-run-vs-start-vs-exec","sec":"docker-s004","q":"In Docker, can you use <code>docker exec</code> on a stopped container, and what happens to a shell you started with it when the container stops?","a":"<p>No, <code>exec</code> needs a running container. A process started with <code>exec</code> is an extra process in the container's namespaces. It does not change the main process, and it ends when the container stops.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0027","card":"q-docker-003-run-vs-start-vs-exec","sec":"docker-s004","q":"In Docker, what is the difference between <code>docker run -it ubuntu bash</code> and <code>docker exec -it web bash</code>?","a":"<p><code>run</code> creates a new container from the <code>ubuntu</code> image and runs <code>bash</code> as its main process. <code>exec</code> starts a new <code>bash</code> inside the already running container <code>web</code>, so you see its live files and processes. Use <code>exec</code> to debug a running container.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0028","card":"q-docker-003-run-vs-start-vs-exec","sec":"docker-s004","q":"In Docker, what does <code>docker create</code> do compared with <code>docker run</code>?","a":"<p><code>docker create</code> prepares the container (layers, config, network settings) but does not start it. <code>docker run</code> is create plus start (plus attach when not detached). Use <code>create</code> when you want to configure first and start later with <code>docker start</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0029","card":"q-docker-004-checking-the-docker-version","sec":"docker-s005","q":"How do you check the Docker version?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker --version\ndocker version  # Full client/server info</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0030","card":"q-docker-004-checking-the-docker-version","sec":"docker-s005","q":"In Docker, <code>docker version</code> prints a Client section but fails on the Server section with \"Cannot connect to the Docker daemon\". What do you check?","a":"<p>The daemon is not running or the client cannot reach it. Check <code>systemctl status docker</code>, that the socket <code>/var/run/docker.sock</code> exists, that your user is in the <code>docker</code> group (or use sudo), and that <code>DOCKER_HOST</code> or the active <code>docker context</code> does not point to another host.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0031","card":"q-docker-004-checking-the-docker-version","sec":"docker-s005","q":"In Docker, why does <code>docker version</code> show separate Client and Server versions, and why does that matter?","a":"<p>The CLI and the daemon are separate programs and can run different versions, even on different machines. API version mismatch can cause errors such as \"client version is too new\". Use <code>docker info</code> for more details like the storage driver, cgroup version and number of containers.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0032","card":"q-docker-005-building-and-listing-images","sec":"docker-s006","q":"What is the role of a Dockerfile?","a":"<p>A script that builds images. It defines the base, dependencies and runtime.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0033","card":"q-docker-005-building-and-listing-images","sec":"docker-s006","q":"How do you create an image from a Dockerfile?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker build -t myapp:1.0 .</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0034","card":"q-docker-005-building-and-listing-images","sec":"docker-s006","q":"What happens in <code>docker build</code>?","a":"<p>It reads the Dockerfile, builds layers, caches them and tags the image.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0035","card":"q-docker-005-building-and-listing-images","sec":"docker-s006","q":"How do you list all images?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker images\ndocker image ls</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0036","card":"q-docker-005-building-and-listing-images","sec":"docker-s006","q":"How do you tag an image, and why?","a":"<p>For versioning and clarity.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker tag myapp myapp:1.0</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0037","card":"q-docker-005-building-and-listing-images","sec":"docker-s006","q":"How do you remove unused images?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker image rm &lt;id&gt;\ndocker image prune -a</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0038","card":"q-docker-005-building-and-listing-images","sec":"docker-s006","q":"In <code>docker build -t myapp:1.0 .</code>, what is the final <code>.</code> and why does it matter?","a":"<p>It is the build context: the directory whose contents are sent to the daemon. <code>COPY</code> and <code>ADD</code> can only use files inside it. A large context slows every build, which is why <code>.dockerignore</code> matters. Use <code>-f</code> to choose a Dockerfile that is not named <code>Dockerfile</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0039","card":"q-docker-005-building-and-listing-images","sec":"docker-s006","q":"How does Docker's build cache decide whether to reuse a layer?","a":"<p>Each instruction produces a layer. Docker reuses a cached layer when the instruction text and its inputs (for <code>COPY</code>/<code>ADD</code>, the file checksums) are unchanged and all previous layers were cached. Once one layer changes, every later layer is rebuilt.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0040","card":"q-docker-005-building-and-listing-images","sec":"docker-s006","q":"How would you order a Dockerfile so dependency installs are cached when only source code changes?","a":"<p>Copy only the dependency manifest first and install, then copy the rest of the source. A source change only invalidates the last <code>COPY</code>, so the slow install layer stays cached. Copying everything first would reinstall dependencies on every code change.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Dockerfile</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>FROM python:3.12-slim\nWORKDIR /app\nCOPY requirements.txt .\nRUN pip install --no-cache-dir -r requirements.txt\nCOPY . .</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"docker-f0041","card":"q-docker-005-building-and-listing-images","sec":"docker-s006","q":"Why is it a bad practice to deploy images tagged <code>latest</code>?","a":"<p><code>latest</code> is just a default tag that moves. Two hosts can pull different content under the same tag, rollbacks are unclear, and you cannot tell what is running. Tag with a version or commit SHA and deploy that, or pin by digest.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0042","card":"q-docker-005-building-and-listing-images","sec":"docker-s006","q":"In Docker, what are dangling images and how does <code>docker image prune</code> differ from <code>docker image prune -a</code>?","a":"<p>Dangling images are untagged layers (shown as <code>&lt;none&gt;</code>), usually left when a tag moves to a newer build. <code>docker image prune</code> removes only dangling images. With <code>-a</code> it removes every image not used by at least one container, including tagged ones.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0043","card":"q-docker-005-building-and-listing-images","sec":"docker-s006","q":"In Docker Engine 23.0 and later, which builder does <code>docker build</code> use by default?","a":"<p>BuildKit. It builds independent stages in parallel, has better caching, and supports features such as <code>--mount=type=cache</code> and <code>--mount=type=secret</code>. Older engines used the legacy builder unless you set <code>DOCKER_BUILDKIT=1</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0044","card":"q-docker-005-building-and-listing-images","sec":"docker-s006","q":"In Docker, can one image have several tags?","a":"<p>Yes. Tags are only names pointing to the same image ID. For example <code>docker tag myapp:1.0 myapp:latest</code> adds a second name without copying data. Pushing sends the tagged name you specify.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0045","card":"q-docker-006-dockerfile-instructions","sec":"docker-s007","q":"What are the common Dockerfile instructions?","a":"<p><code>FROM</code>, <code>RUN</code>, <code>CMD</code>, <code>ENTRYPOINT</code>, <code>COPY</code>, <code>ADD</code>, <code>WORKDIR</code>, <code>EXPOSE</code>, <code>ENV</code>.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0046","card":"q-docker-006-dockerfile-instructions","sec":"docker-s007","q":"CMD vs ENTRYPOINT?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>CMD</strong>: default arguments, overridable.</li>\n  <li><strong>ENTRYPOINT</strong>: the fixed executable.</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0047","card":"q-docker-006-dockerfile-instructions","sec":"docker-s007","q":"What is the purpose of <code>.dockerignore</code>?","a":"<p>It excludes files from the build context. Builds are faster and cleaner.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0048","card":"q-docker-006-dockerfile-instructions","sec":"docker-s007","q":"In a Dockerfile, when would you use <code>ADD</code> instead of <code>COPY</code>?","a":"<p>Almost never. <code>COPY</code> just copies files from the build context. <code>ADD</code> also extracts local tar archives and can download URLs, which makes it less predictable. Prefer <code>COPY</code>, and use <code>ADD</code> only for automatic tar extraction.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0049","card":"q-docker-006-dockerfile-instructions","sec":"docker-s007","q":"In a Dockerfile, what is the difference between the shell form <code>CMD python app.py</code> and the exec form <code>CMD [\"python\", \"app.py\"]</code>?","a":"<p>Shell form runs the command through <code>/bin/sh -c</code>, so the shell becomes PID 1 and may not forward signals, which breaks graceful shutdown on <code>docker stop</code>. Exec form runs the program directly as PID 1, so it receives SIGTERM. Use exec form for the main process.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0050","card":"q-docker-006-dockerfile-instructions","sec":"docker-s007","q":"A Dockerfile ends with these two instructions. What command runs with <code>docker run img</code>, and what does <code>docker run img --port 9000</code> do?","a":"<p><code>docker run img</code> runs <code>python app.py --port 8000</code>. <code>docker run img --port 9000</code> replaces only the <code>CMD</code>, so it runs <code>python app.py --port 9000</code>. To replace the entrypoint itself you need <code>--entrypoint</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Dockerfile</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>ENTRYPOINT [&quot;python&quot;, &quot;app.py&quot;]\nCMD [&quot;--port&quot;, &quot;8000&quot;]</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"docker-f0051","card":"q-docker-006-dockerfile-instructions","sec":"docker-s007","q":"What happens in a Dockerfile if you write several <code>CMD</code> instructions?","a":"<p>Only the last <code>CMD</code> takes effect. The earlier ones are ignored. The same is true for <code>ENTRYPOINT</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0052","card":"q-docker-006-dockerfile-instructions","sec":"docker-s007","q":"In a Dockerfile, what is the difference between <code>ENV</code> and <code>ARG</code>?","a":"<p><code>ARG</code> exists only at build time and is set with <code>--build-arg</code>. <code>ENV</code> is stored in the image and is visible to the running container.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0053","card":"q-docker-006-dockerfile-instructions","sec":"docker-s007","q":"Why use <code>WORKDIR /app</code> instead of <code>RUN cd /app</code> in a Dockerfile?","a":"<p>Each <code>RUN</code> starts a fresh shell, so a <code>cd</code> does not carry over to the next instruction. <code>WORKDIR</code> sets the directory for all following <code>RUN</code>, <code>CMD</code>, <code>COPY</code> and <code>ENTRYPOINT</code> instructions and creates it if missing.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0054","card":"q-docker-006-dockerfile-instructions","sec":"docker-s007","q":"Why should <code>apt-get update</code> and <code>apt-get install</code> be in the same <code>RUN</code> instruction in a Dockerfile?","a":"<p>If <code>update</code> is its own cached layer, a later change to the install line reuses a stale package index and can fail or install old versions. Combining them keeps the index fresh with the install, and you can clean the apt lists in the same layer.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0055","card":"q-docker-006-dockerfile-instructions","sec":"docker-s007","q":"What does <code>FROM scratch</code> mean in a Dockerfile?","a":"<p>It starts from an empty image with no OS files. It is used for statically linked binaries (for example Go) to get very small images. There is no shell, so you cannot <code>docker exec</code> into it with <code>sh</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0056","card":"q-docker-006-dockerfile-instructions","sec":"docker-s007","q":"In a Dockerfile, what is the difference between <code>RUN</code>, <code>CMD</code> and <code>ENTRYPOINT</code>?","a":"<p><code>RUN</code> executes at build time and its result is saved as an image layer (installing packages). <code>CMD</code> sets the default command or arguments used at run time and is easily replaced by arguments to <code>docker run</code>. <code>ENTRYPOINT</code> sets the fixed executable of the container, so <code>docker run</code> arguments are appended to it. Neither <code>CMD</code> nor <code>ENTRYPOINT</code> runs during the build.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0057","card":"q-docker-006-dockerfile-instructions","sec":"docker-s007","q":"In a Dockerfile, what does the <code>VOLUME</code> instruction do?","a":"<p>It declares that the path holds data that should live outside the container's writable layer. If you do not mount something there, Docker creates an anonymous volume for each container, which keeps the data after the container is removed but is hard to find. Anything the Dockerfile writes to that path after <code>VOLUME</code> is discarded. For clear ownership, mount a named volume at run time.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Dockerfile</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>FROM mysql:8\nVOLUME /var/lib/mysql</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"docker-f0058","card":"q-docker-007-reducing-image-size","sec":"docker-s008","q":"How do you reduce image size?","a":"<p>Use a small base (for example alpine), chain <code>RUN</code> commands, clean up after installs, and use multi-stage builds.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0059","card":"q-docker-007-reducing-image-size","sec":"docker-s008","q":"What is a multi-stage build and why use it?","a":"<p>It separates build from runtime, so the final image is smaller.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Dockerfile</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>FROM golang AS builder\nRUN go build -o app\nFROM alpine\nCOPY --from=builder /app .</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0060","card":"q-docker-007-reducing-image-size","sec":"docker-s008","q":"How do you reduce Docker image size in practice (on-call view)?","a":"<p>Use lightweight images, clean up after installs, minimize layers, and copy only the built output from a builder stage.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Dockerfile</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>FROM alpine:latest\nRUN apt-get update &amp;&amp; apt-get install -y &lt;package&gt; &amp;&amp; apt-get clean &amp;&amp; rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*\nFROM node:16 AS builder\nWORKDIR /app\nCOPY package.json .\nRUN npm install\nFROM node:16-slim\nCOPY --from=builder /app /app\nRUN rm -rf /app/unneeded_files</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0061","card":"q-docker-007-reducing-image-size","sec":"docker-s008","q":"In a Dockerfile, why does a separate <code>RUN rm -rf /tmp/build</code> not shrink the image if the files were created in an earlier <code>RUN</code>?","a":"<p>Layers are immutable. The files stay in the earlier layer, and the later layer only hides them. To really save space, create and delete the files in the same <code>RUN</code>, or use a multi-stage build and copy only the result.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0062","card":"q-docker-007-reducing-image-size","sec":"docker-s008","q":"In a Dockerfile multi-stage build, what does <code>AS builder</code> do here, and what ends up in the final image?","a":"<p><code>AS builder</code> names the first stage so a later stage can refer to it. The final image is the last <code>FROM</code> (alpine) plus whatever <code>COPY --from=builder</code> brings over. The Go toolchain and source stay in the discarded builder stage, so the final image is small.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Dockerfile</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>FROM golang AS builder\nRUN go build -o app\nFROM alpine\nCOPY --from=builder /app .</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"docker-f0063","card":"q-docker-007-reducing-image-size","sec":"docker-s008","q":"What is a risk of using <code>alpine</code> as a base image to reduce size?","a":"<p>Alpine uses musl libc instead of glibc. Prebuilt binaries or Python wheels built for glibc may fail or need compiling, and some behavior (DNS, locale, performance) differs. A <code>-slim</code> Debian image is a common compromise.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0064","card":"q-docker-007-reducing-image-size","sec":"docker-s008","q":"In Docker, what are distroless images and what is the trade-off?","a":"<p>They contain only the app and its runtime dependencies, with no shell or package manager. This reduces size and attack surface. The trade-off is that debugging is harder because there is no shell for <code>docker exec</code>. You can use debug variants or an ephemeral debug container.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0065","card":"q-docker-007-reducing-image-size","sec":"docker-s008","q":"How do you find which layers make a Docker image big?","a":"<p>Run <code>docker history &lt;image&gt;</code> to list each layer with its size and the instruction that created it. Tools such as <code>dive</code> show the content of each layer. Then fix the large layers, for example by cleaning caches in the same <code>RUN</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0066","card":"q-docker-007-reducing-image-size","sec":"docker-s008","q":"In Docker, why does image size matter beyond disk usage?","a":"<p>Smaller images pull and start faster during deployments and autoscaling, cost less in registry storage and transfer, and have fewer packages that can contain vulnerabilities.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0067","card":"q-docker-008-multi-architecture-images","sec":"docker-s009","q":"How do you build multi-arch images?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker buildx build --platform linux/amd64,linux/arm64</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0068","card":"q-docker-008-multi-architecture-images","sec":"docker-s009","q":"Why do you need multi-architecture images, and what error shows up when you do not have one?","a":"<p>Hosts run different CPU architectures (amd64 servers, ARM Graviton or Apple Silicon). An image built for one fails on the other with <code>exec format error</code>, or Docker warns about a platform mismatch. A multi-arch image lets the same tag work on both.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0069","card":"q-docker-008-multi-architecture-images","sec":"docker-s009","q":"In Docker, how does a single tag like <code>myapp:1.0</code> serve different CPU architectures?","a":"<p>The tag points to a manifest list (an OCI image index) that lists one image per platform. When you pull, the client sends its platform and the registry returns the matching image.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0070","card":"q-docker-008-multi-architecture-images","sec":"docker-s009","q":"What is the trade-off between building a Docker image for another architecture with QEMU emulation and with a native builder?","a":"<p>With <code>docker buildx</code> you can emulate foreign architectures through QEMU, which is easy but slow. Native builders for each architecture, or cross-compiling in the Dockerfile, are faster. Many teams use CI runners of each architecture and merge them into one manifest list.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0071","card":"q-docker-008-multi-architecture-images","sec":"docker-s009","q":"With the classic Docker image store, why does <code>docker buildx build --platform linux/amd64,linux/arm64</code> need <code>--push</code> instead of loading the result locally?","a":"<p>The classic local image store holds a single-platform image per tag and cannot hold a multi-platform manifest list. So you push the result straight to a registry. The newer containerd image store can keep multi-platform images locally.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0072","card":"q-docker-009-hands-on-build-a-custom-image","sec":"docker-s010","q":"How do you create a container with a custom image from a Dockerfile?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Dockerfile</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code># Dockerfile\nFROM nginx:latest\nCOPY index.html /usr/share/nginx/html/</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker build -t my-nginx .\ndocker run -d -p 80:80 my-nginx</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0073","card":"q-docker-009-hands-on-build-a-custom-image","sec":"docker-s010","q":"This Dockerfile only copies one file. Which process starts when you run a container from it?","a":"<p>The <code>ENTRYPOINT</code>/<code>CMD</code> inherited from the <code>nginx</code> base image, which runs <code>nginx -g 'daemon off;'</code>. A child image inherits all metadata from its base unless it overrides it. <code>nginx</code> runs in the foreground, which keeps the container alive.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Dockerfile</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>FROM nginx:latest\nCOPY index.html /usr/share/nginx/html/</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"docker-f0074","card":"q-docker-009-hands-on-build-a-custom-image","sec":"docker-s010","q":"Why must the main process of a Docker container run in the foreground?","a":"<p>A container runs only as long as its main process (PID 1). If the process daemonizes and the launcher exits, Docker sees the process end and stops the container. This is why nginx is started with <code>daemon off</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0075","card":"q-docker-009-hands-on-build-a-custom-image","sec":"docker-s010","q":"In Docker, when you update <code>index.html</code> on the host, how does the change reach a container started from an image built with <code>COPY</code>?","a":"<p>It does not. <code>COPY</code> bakes a snapshot into the image. You must rebuild the image and recreate the container. For development you can bind mount the file or directory instead, so edits show up instantly.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0076","card":"q-docker-010-starting-and-naming-containers","sec":"docker-s011","q":"How do you start a container detached?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker run -d nginx</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0077","card":"q-docker-010-starting-and-naming-containers","sec":"docker-s011","q":"How do you run a container with a specific name?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker run --name myapp nginx</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0078","card":"q-docker-010-starting-and-naming-containers","sec":"docker-s011","q":"How do you create a container running Nginx and expose it?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker run -d -p 80:80 nginx</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0079","card":"q-docker-010-starting-and-naming-containers","sec":"docker-s011","q":"How do you expose it on port 8080?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker run -d -p 8080:80 nginx</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0080","card":"q-docker-010-starting-and-naming-containers","sec":"docker-s011","q":"How do you set an environment variable?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker run -d -e MY_VAR=&quot;hello&quot; nginx</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0081","card":"q-docker-010-starting-and-naming-containers","sec":"docker-s011","q":"In Docker, what does <code>-d</code> do and what happens to a container that was started without it?","a":"<p><code>-d</code> runs the container in the background and prints its ID. Without it the container's output is attached to your terminal, and pressing Ctrl+C sends a signal to the container's main process. In both cases the container stops when its main process exits.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0082","card":"q-docker-010-starting-and-naming-containers","sec":"docker-s011","q":"In <code>docker run -p 8080:80 nginx</code>, which number is the host port?","a":"<p>The first one. The format is <code>host:container</code>, so port 8080 on the host forwards to port 80 in the container. You get the error \"port is already allocated\" if something on the host already uses 8080.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0083","card":"q-docker-010-starting-and-naming-containers","sec":"docker-s011","q":"In Docker, what happens if you do not pass <code>--name</code>, and what if the name already exists?","a":"<p>Docker assigns a random name such as <code>quirky_curie</code>. Names are unique per host, so reusing a name fails with a conflict until you remove the old container or choose another name.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0084","card":"q-docker-010-starting-and-naming-containers","sec":"docker-s011","q":"In Docker, what do the flags in <code>docker run -it --rm ubuntu bash</code> mean?","a":"<p><code>-i</code> keeps stdin open and <code>-t</code> allocates a pseudo-terminal, so you get an interactive shell. <code>--rm</code> deletes the container when it exits. Together they are the usual way to try something in a throwaway container.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0085","card":"q-docker-010-starting-and-naming-containers","sec":"docker-s011","q":"In Docker, a container from <code>ubuntu</code> started with <code>docker run -d ubuntu</code> shows <code>Exited (0)</code> right away. Why?","a":"<p>The image's default command is <code>bash</code>, which exits at once when there is no terminal or stdin. A container lives only as long as its main process. Give it a long-running command or use <code>-it</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0086","card":"q-docker-010-starting-and-naming-containers","sec":"docker-s011","q":"In Docker, how do you publish a port only on the host's loopback interface?","a":"<p>Prefix the host IP: <code>docker run -p 127.0.0.1:8080:80 nginx</code>. Without the prefix the port binds to <code>0.0.0.0</code> and is reachable from other machines.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0087","card":"q-docker-010-starting-and-naming-containers","sec":"docker-s011","q":"In Docker, what is the difference between <code>-e VAR=value</code> and <code>--env-file</code>?","a":"<p><code>-e</code> sets one variable on the command line. <code>--env-file file</code> loads many <code>KEY=value</code> lines from a file, which keeps long values out of shell history. Both are plain runtime config, not a place for secrets.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0088","card":"q-docker-011-logs-inspect-and-attach","sec":"docker-s012","q":"How do you view container logs?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker logs &lt;id&gt;\ndocker logs -f &lt;id&gt;  # Real-time logs</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0089","card":"q-docker-011-logs-inspect-and-attach","sec":"docker-s012","q":"How do you inspect a running container?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker inspect &lt;id&gt;</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0090","card":"q-docker-011-logs-inspect-and-attach","sec":"docker-s012","q":"How do you attach to a container terminal?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker attach &lt;id&gt;\ndocker exec -it &lt;id&gt; bash</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0091","card":"q-docker-011-logs-inspect-and-attach","sec":"docker-s012","q":"In Docker, what is the difference between <code>docker attach</code> and <code>docker exec -it</code>?","a":"<p><code>attach</code> connects your terminal to the main process's stdin/stdout, so Ctrl+C can stop the container, and you detach with Ctrl+P then Ctrl+Q. <code>exec -it</code> starts a new process, such as a shell, in the container, and exiting it does not affect the main process.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0092","card":"q-docker-011-logs-inspect-and-attach","sec":"docker-s012","q":"How do you show only recent log lines, or logs since a time, in Docker?","a":"<p>Use <code>docker logs --tail 100 &lt;id&gt;</code> for the last lines and <code>--since 10m</code> (or a timestamp) for a time window. Combine with <code>-f</code> to follow, and <code>-t</code> to add timestamps.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0093","card":"q-docker-011-logs-inspect-and-attach","sec":"docker-s012","q":"In Docker, how do you read a single field from <code>docker inspect</code>, for example a container's IP address?","a":"<p>Use a Go template with <code>--format</code>, for example <code>docker inspect --format '{{range .NetworkSettings.Networks}}{{.IPAddress}}{{end}}' &lt;id&gt;</code>. This avoids parsing the large JSON output with grep.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0094","card":"q-docker-011-logs-inspect-and-attach","sec":"docker-s012","q":"What breaks if you leave Docker's default <code>json-file</code> logging driver unconfigured on a busy host?","a":"<p>By default the log files grow without limit and can fill the disk. Set <code>max-size</code> and <code>max-file</code> in <code>daemon.json</code> or per container with <code>--log-opt</code>, or use the <code>local</code> driver or a remote driver.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0095","card":"q-docker-012-stopping-and-removing-containers","sec":"docker-s013","q":"stop vs kill?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>stop</strong>: graceful (SIGTERM).</li>\n  <li><strong>kill</strong>: instant (SIGKILL).</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0096","card":"q-docker-012-stopping-and-removing-containers","sec":"docker-s013","q":"<code>rm</code> vs <code>rm -f</code>?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>rm</strong>: removes stopped containers only.</li>\n  <li><strong>rm -f</strong>: stops and removes.</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0097","card":"q-docker-012-stopping-and-removing-containers","sec":"docker-s013","q":"What happens to data when a container stops?","a":"<p>In-memory data is lost. The file system persists until the container is deleted. Use volumes for durable data.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0098","card":"q-docker-012-stopping-and-removing-containers","sec":"docker-s013","q":"In Docker, what exactly happens when you run <code>docker stop</code>?","a":"<p>Docker sends SIGTERM to the main process, waits for a grace period (10 seconds by default, <code>-t</code> changes it), then sends SIGKILL. An app that handles SIGTERM can finish requests and close connections cleanly.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0099","card":"q-docker-012-stopping-and-removing-containers","sec":"docker-s013","q":"What do exit codes 137 and 143 usually mean for a Docker container?","a":"<p>137 is 128 + 9, so the process was killed with SIGKILL (a forced kill after the stop timeout, <code>docker kill</code>, or an out-of-memory kill). 143 is 128 + 15, so it ended on SIGTERM.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0100","card":"q-docker-012-stopping-and-removing-containers","sec":"docker-s013","q":"In Docker, why do anonymous volumes pile up on a host and how do you remove them with a container?","a":"<p>Images that declare <code>VOLUME</code> create an anonymous volume per container, and plain <code>docker rm</code> leaves it behind. Use <code>docker rm -v</code> to remove the container's anonymous volumes, or <code>docker volume prune</code> later.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0101","card":"q-docker-012-stopping-and-removing-containers","sec":"docker-s013","q":"How do you remove all stopped containers in Docker?","a":"<p>Use <code>docker container prune</code>. It asks for confirmation and removes only stopped containers. Running containers are not touched.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0102","card":"q-docker-012-stopping-and-removing-containers","sec":"docker-s013","q":"How does <code>docker kill</code> differ from <code>docker stop</code>, and how do you change the stop signal or timeout in Docker?","a":"<p><code>docker kill</code> sends SIGKILL right away, or another signal with <code>-s</code>. For apps that expect a different signal, set <code>STOPSIGNAL SIGQUIT</code> in the Dockerfile or <code>--stop-signal</code> at run time. For slow shutdowns, use <code>--stop-timeout</code> or <code>docker stop -t 30</code> (<code>stop_grace_period</code> in Compose).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0103","card":"q-docker-013-resource-limits-and-restart-policy","sec":"docker-s014","q":"How do you limit CPU and memory?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker run --cpus=&quot;1&quot; -m 512m nginx\ndocker run -d --cpus=&quot;0.5&quot; nginx</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0104","card":"q-docker-013-resource-limits-and-restart-policy","sec":"docker-s014","q":"What is the purpose of <code>--restart</code>?","a":"<p>It sets an auto-restart policy, for example <code>always</code> or <code>on-failure:5</code>.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0105","card":"q-docker-013-resource-limits-and-restart-policy","sec":"docker-s014","q":"In Docker, what is the difference between <code>--cpus</code> and <code>--cpu-shares</code>?","a":"<p><code>--cpus=0.5</code> is a hard cap: the container can use at most half a CPU core's time. <code>--cpu-shares</code> is a relative weight that only matters when CPUs are contended. An idle host lets the container use as much CPU as it wants.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0106","card":"q-docker-013-resource-limits-and-restart-policy","sec":"docker-s014","q":"What happens in Docker when a container uses more memory than <code>-m</code> allows, compared with using more CPU than allowed?","a":"<p>Exceeding memory makes the kernel OOM killer kill a process in the container, usually with exit code 137 (<code>OOMKilled</code> true in <code>docker inspect</code>). Exceeding CPU is not fatal: the container is throttled and just runs slower.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0107","card":"q-docker-013-resource-limits-and-restart-policy","sec":"docker-s014","q":"What does <code>--memory-swap</code> do in Docker?","a":"<p>It sets the total of memory plus swap the container may use. Setting it equal to <code>-m</code> disables swap for the container. Without it the container may use swap up to the same amount as the memory limit, if the host has swap.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0108","card":"q-docker-013-resource-limits-and-restart-policy","sec":"docker-s014","q":"What are the Docker restart policies and how do they differ?","a":"<p><code>no</code> (default) never restarts. <code>on-failure[:N]</code> restarts only on non-zero exit, up to N times. <code>always</code> restarts whatever the exit reason, and also starts the container when the daemon restarts. <code>unless-stopped</code> is like <code>always</code> but does not restart a container you stopped manually after a daemon restart.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0109","card":"q-docker-013-resource-limits-and-restart-policy","sec":"docker-s014","q":"Why is <code>--restart always</code> not a replacement for an orchestrator?","a":"<p>It works on a single host only and has no health awareness, no placement, no rolling updates and no scaling. A container that crashes immediately will restart in a loop with growing back-off delay. Use Swarm, ECS or Kubernetes for production services.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0110","card":"q-docker-013-resource-limits-and-restart-policy","sec":"docker-s014","q":"In Docker, can you change limits of a running container?","a":"<p>Yes, for many of them: <code>docker update --cpus 0.5 --memory 512m --restart unless-stopped &lt;id&gt;</code>. If you also set memory, raise <code>--memory-swap</code> accordingly or the command may fail.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0111","card":"q-docker-013-resource-limits-and-restart-policy","sec":"docker-s014","q":"In Docker, what do <code>--pids-limit</code> and <code>--ulimit</code> protect against?","a":"<p><code>--pids-limit 200</code> caps the number of processes and threads in a container (a cgroup limit), which stops a fork bomb from exhausting the host. <code>--ulimit nofile=1024:2048</code> sets per-process limits such as open files. Together with memory and CPU limits they stop one container from starving others.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0112","card":"q-docker-014-troubleshooting-a-failing-container","sec":"docker-s015","q":"How do you debug a failing container?","a":"<p>Check logs, inspect it, and run it interactively.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0113","card":"q-docker-014-troubleshooting-a-failing-container","sec":"docker-s015","q":"How do you troubleshoot a container that keeps exiting?","a":"<p>Check logs and the exit code, run it interactively, and check the <code>CMD</code>.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0114","card":"q-docker-014-troubleshooting-a-failing-container","sec":"docker-s015","q":"A container overuses memory. What do you do?","a":"<p>Check <code>docker stats</code>, cap it with <code>-m</code>, and debug the app.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0115","card":"q-docker-014-troubleshooting-a-failing-container","sec":"docker-s015","q":"A failed image build. How do you fix it?","a":"<p>Check the logs, test each step, and rebuild with <code>--no-cache</code>.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0116","card":"q-docker-014-troubleshooting-a-failing-container","sec":"docker-s015","q":"A Docker container exits seconds after starting. What steps do you follow?","a":"<p>Run <code>docker ps -a</code> to see the exit code. Read <code>docker logs &lt;id&gt;</code>. Check <code>docker inspect</code> for <code>State.ExitCode</code>, <code>State.Error</code> and <code>OOMKilled</code>. Then reproduce interactively, for example <code>docker run -it --entrypoint sh &lt;image&gt;</code>, to run the command by hand. Typical causes are a missing command or file, bad config or env, a crash on startup, or an out-of-memory kill.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0117","card":"q-docker-014-troubleshooting-a-failing-container","sec":"docker-s015","q":"What do common Docker exit codes 125, 126, 127, 137 and 139 mean?","a":"<p>125 means the Docker daemon or <code>docker run</code> itself failed. 126 means the command exists but cannot be executed (permissions). 127 means the command was not found. 137 is SIGKILL (often OOM). 139 is SIGSEGV, a segmentation fault in the app.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0118","card":"q-docker-014-troubleshooting-a-failing-container","sec":"docker-s015","q":"A Docker container with <code>--restart always</code> keeps restarting. How do you see why?","a":"<p><code>docker ps</code> shows <code>Restarting</code>. Read <code>docker logs --tail 50 &lt;id&gt;</code> and the exit code in <code>docker inspect</code>. To debug without the loop, start a copy with a shell as the entrypoint, or disable the policy with <code>docker update --restart no &lt;id&gt;</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0119","card":"q-docker-014-troubleshooting-a-failing-container","sec":"docker-s015","q":"How do you debug a <code>RUN</code> step that fails during <code>docker build</code>?","a":"<p>Re-run with <code>--progress=plain</code> (BuildKit) to see the full output, and try <code>--no-cache</code> if the cache may be stale. Start a container from the last good layer or run the failing command by hand in a base container to experiment.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0120","card":"q-docker-014-troubleshooting-a-failing-container","sec":"docker-s015","q":"What does <code>docker events</code> help with when troubleshooting?","a":"<p>It streams daemon events such as container create, start, die (with exit code), OOM, health status change and network connect. It shows what happened and when, which is useful for restart loops and short-lived containers.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0121","card":"q-docker-015-network-types-and-the-default-bridge","sec":"docker-s016","q":"What are the types of Docker networks?","a":"<p><strong>Bridge</strong> (default), <strong>Host</strong> (no isolation), <strong>Overlay</strong> (multi-host), <strong>None</strong>, <strong>Macvlan</strong>.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0122","card":"q-docker-015-network-types-and-the-default-bridge","sec":"docker-s016","q":"What is the default bridge network?","a":"<p>It uses private IPs and NAT to the host, so port mapping is needed.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0123","card":"q-docker-015-network-types-and-the-default-bridge","sec":"docker-s016","q":"Host vs bridge network?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Host</strong>: uses the host's network stack, no isolation.</li>\n  <li><strong>Bridge</strong>: isolated and NAT'd.</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0124","card":"q-docker-015-network-types-and-the-default-bridge","sec":"docker-s016","q":"When do you use an overlay network?","a":"<p>For multi-host communication in Swarm, for example distributed apps.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0125","card":"q-docker-015-network-types-and-the-default-bridge","sec":"docker-s016","q":"How does a container on the default Docker bridge network reach the internet?","a":"<p>The bridge <code>docker0</code> (default subnet 172.17.0.0/16) gives the container a private IP. Outbound traffic is NAT-ed (masqueraded) through the host with iptables rules, so the container appears with the host's IP.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0126","card":"q-docker-015-network-types-and-the-default-bridge","sec":"docker-s016","q":"In Docker, what does <code>--network host</code> change, and what is the cost?","a":"<p>The container shares the host's network stack, so there is no NAT and no port mapping (<code>-p</code> is ignored). It is faster and can see host interfaces. The cost is no network isolation and port clashes with host services.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0127","card":"q-docker-015-network-types-and-the-default-bridge","sec":"docker-s016","q":"In Docker, when would you use the <code>none</code> network?","a":"<p>When a container needs no network, for example a batch job that processes local data. It gets only the loopback interface. It reduces attack surface and prevents data from leaving over the network.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0128","card":"q-docker-015-network-types-and-the-default-bridge","sec":"docker-s016","q":"In Docker, what is macvlan and when do you use it?","a":"<p>Macvlan gives each container its own MAC and IP on the physical LAN, so it looks like a separate machine. It suits legacy apps that need to be directly reachable on the LAN without port mapping. It needs a network that allows multiple MACs per port.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0129","card":"q-docker-015-network-types-and-the-default-bridge","sec":"docker-s016","q":"How does a Docker overlay network connect containers on different hosts?","a":"<p>It builds a virtual layer-2 network over the hosts' IP network using VXLAN encapsulation (UDP 4789). Swarm manages membership. Traffic between hosts can be encrypted with <code>--opt encrypted</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0130","card":"q-docker-015-network-types-and-the-default-bridge","sec":"docker-s016","q":"Two containers run on the default Docker bridge. Can they reach each other, and how?","a":"<p>Yes, by IP address, since they share the bridge. There is no automatic name resolution on the default bridge, so use a user-defined network if you want to reach them by name.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0131","card":"q-docker-016-custom-networks-dns-and-communication","sec":"docker-s017","q":"How do you create a custom network?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker network create mynet</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0132","card":"q-docker-016-custom-networks-dns-and-communication","sec":"docker-s017","q":"How do containers communicate?","a":"<p>On the same network, by IP or by name (custom networks). External access needs port mapping.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0133","card":"q-docker-016-custom-networks-dns-and-communication","sec":"docker-s017","q":"How does Docker DNS resolution work?","a":"<p>Custom networks resolve container names automatically. The default bridge does not.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0134","card":"q-docker-016-custom-networks-dns-and-communication","sec":"docker-s017","q":"What is <code>--link</code> and why is it deprecated?","a":"<p>It links containers on the default bridge. It is outdated; use custom networks.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0135","card":"q-docker-016-custom-networks-dns-and-communication","sec":"docker-s017","q":"Why should you use a user-defined bridge network instead of the default bridge in Docker?","a":"<p>User-defined networks have built-in DNS, so containers find each other by name or alias. They provide better isolation, because only containers on the same network can talk to each other. You can also connect and disconnect running containers, and each network can have its own subnet and options.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0136","card":"q-docker-016-custom-networks-dns-and-communication","sec":"docker-s017","q":"What is the address of Docker's embedded DNS server inside a container on a user-defined network?","a":"<p><code>127.0.0.11</code>. It resolves container names, service names and network aliases, and forwards other names to the host's configured resolvers.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0137","card":"q-docker-016-custom-networks-dns-and-communication","sec":"docker-s017","q":"In Docker, a web container cannot reach a database container using the hostname <code>db</code>. What do you check?","a":"<p>Both must be on the same user-defined network (the default bridge has no DNS). Check <code>docker network inspect &lt;net&gt;</code> for both containers, the container or alias name, and that the database listens on the container port and not only on localhost. Test with <code>docker exec web getent hosts db</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0138","card":"q-docker-016-custom-networks-dns-and-communication","sec":"docker-s017","q":"In Docker, why does an app inside a container fail when it connects to a database at <code>localhost:5432</code> while the database runs in another container?","a":"<p><code>localhost</code> inside a container is the container itself. Use the other container's name on a shared network (for example <code>db:5432</code>) or the host's address.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0139","card":"q-docker-016-custom-networks-dns-and-communication","sec":"docker-s017","q":"How do you reach a service on the host machine from inside a Docker container?","a":"<p>Use <code>host.docker.internal</code>, which Docker Desktop provides. On Linux (Docker 20.10 and later) add <code>--add-host=host.docker.internal:host-gateway</code>. Otherwise use the bridge gateway IP. The host service must listen on an interface that is not loopback-only.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0140","card":"q-docker-016-custom-networks-dns-and-communication","sec":"docker-s017","q":"How can one Docker container be on two networks, and why would you do that?","a":"<p>Use <code>docker network connect &lt;net&gt; &lt;container&gt;</code> or list several networks in Compose. A common design is an API container on both a frontend and a backend network, while the database sits only on the backend network, so the web tier cannot reach it directly.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0141","card":"q-docker-017-exposing-ports","sec":"docker-s018","q":"How do you expose a port?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker run -p 8080:80 nginx</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0142","card":"q-docker-017-exposing-ports","sec":"docker-s018","q":"EXPOSE vs <code>-p</code>?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>EXPOSE</strong>: documents intent.</li>\n  <li><strong>-p</strong>: actually binds the port.</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0143","card":"q-docker-017-exposing-ports","sec":"docker-s018","q":"In Docker, if two containers are on the same user-defined network, do you need <code>-p</code> for them to talk?","a":"<p>No. Containers on the same network can reach each other's container ports directly. <code>-p</code> is needed only to reach a container from outside the Docker network, such as from the host or other machines.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0144","card":"q-docker-017-exposing-ports","sec":"docker-s018","q":"What does <code>docker run -P</code> do?","a":"<p>It publishes all ports declared with <code>EXPOSE</code> in the image to random high ports on the host. Use <code>docker port &lt;container&gt;</code> to see the mappings. The lowercase <code>-p</code> maps ports you choose.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0145","card":"q-docker-017-exposing-ports","sec":"docker-s018","q":"In Docker, how do you publish a UDP port?","a":"<p>Add the protocol: <code>-p 5353:5353/udp</code>. Without it only TCP is published.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0146","card":"q-docker-017-exposing-ports","sec":"docker-s018","q":"In Docker on Linux, why can a published port be reachable even though <code>ufw</code> blocks it?","a":"<p>Docker adds its own iptables NAT and forward rules ahead of ufw's rules, so published ports bypass ufw. To restrict access, bind to <code>127.0.0.1</code>, or put rules in the <code>DOCKER-USER</code> chain, or do not publish the port.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0147","card":"q-docker-017-exposing-ports","sec":"docker-s018","q":"In Docker, how do you run three copies of a web container on one host that all listen on port 80?","a":"<p>A host port can be bound only once, so fixed <code>-p 80:80</code> fails on the second container. Publish different host ports (or a random one) and put a reverse proxy or load balancer in front.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0148","card":"q-docker-018-hands-on-networking-recipes","sec":"docker-s019","q":"How do you create a Docker network for two containers?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker network create my-network\ndocker run -d --name container1 --network my-network nginx\ndocker run -d --name container2 --network my-network busybox</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0149","card":"q-docker-018-hands-on-networking-recipes","sec":"docker-s019","q":"How do you attach a container to a custom network?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker network create custom-network\ndocker run -d --network custom-network nginx</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0150","card":"q-docker-018-hands-on-networking-recipes","sec":"docker-s019","q":"How do you link a container to another container?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker run -d --name db mysql:5.7\ndocker run -d --link db:database nginx</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0151","card":"q-docker-018-hands-on-networking-recipes","sec":"docker-s019","q":"From a long-running busybox container on the same user-defined network, how do you call the nginx container named <code>container1</code>?","a":"<p>By name over Docker DNS, for example <code>docker exec container2 wget -qO- http://container1</code>. No <code>-p</code> is needed because both are on the same network.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0152","card":"q-docker-018-hands-on-networking-recipes","sec":"docker-s019","q":"This recipe starts a MySQL container and links another one. What does <code>db:database</code> do, and what is missing for the MySQL container to start?","a":"<p><code>--link db:database</code> makes <code>db</code> reachable under the alias <code>database</code> (a hosts entry) and injects environment variables. It is a legacy feature. The <code>mysql</code> image refuses to start without a root password setting such as <code>MYSQL_ROOT_PASSWORD</code>. Also, MySQL 5.7 reached end of life in 2023, so use a supported version.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker run -d --name db mysql:5.7\ndocker run -d --link db:database nginx</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"docker-f0153","card":"q-docker-018-hands-on-networking-recipes","sec":"docker-s019","q":"How do you replace <code>--link</code> in modern Docker?","a":"<p>Put both containers on a user-defined network. They resolve each other by container name, and you can add <code>--network-alias</code> for extra names.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0154","card":"q-docker-019-volumes-vs-bind-mounts","sec":"docker-s020","q":"Volumes vs bind mounts?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Volumes</strong>: Docker-managed, portable.</li>\n  <li><strong>Binds</strong>: a host path, precise.</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0155","card":"q-docker-019-volumes-vs-bind-mounts","sec":"docker-s020","q":"What happens to a volume when the container is deleted?","a":"<p>It stays. Volumes live in <code>/var/lib/docker/volumes</code>.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0156","card":"q-docker-019-volumes-vs-bind-mounts","sec":"docker-s020","q":"How do you get persistent storage?","a":"<p>Volumes (<code>-v myvol:/data</code>) or binds (<code>-v /host:/data</code>).</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0157","card":"q-docker-019-volumes-vs-bind-mounts","sec":"docker-s020","q":"<code>--mount</code> vs <code>--volume</code>?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>--mount</strong>: precise, modern.</li>\n  <li><strong>-v</strong>: simple, older.</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0158","card":"q-docker-019-volumes-vs-bind-mounts","sec":"docker-s020","q":"When would you choose a bind mount over a named volume in Docker?","a":"<p>For development, such as mounting source code so edits show up without rebuilding, or when you must give the container a specific host file (config, socket). For database files and other production data, prefer named volumes because Docker manages them and they do not depend on the host layout.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0159","card":"q-docker-019-volumes-vs-bind-mounts","sec":"docker-s020","q":"What happens in Docker if the host path of a <code>-v /host/dir:/data</code> bind mount does not exist, compared with <code>--mount type=bind</code>?","a":"<p><code>-v</code> creates the host directory (owned by root) silently. <code>--mount</code> fails with an error, which catches typos. This is a reason to prefer <code>--mount</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0160","card":"q-docker-019-volumes-vs-bind-mounts","sec":"docker-s020","q":"What happens if you mount a volume or bind mount onto a directory that already has files in the image?","a":"<p>A bind mount hides the image's files at that path. A new empty named volume is first populated with the image's files at that path, then keeps its own data afterward. Because of this, mounting an empty host directory over an app directory can make the app disappear.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0161","card":"q-docker-019-volumes-vs-bind-mounts","sec":"docker-s020","q":"How do you make a Docker mount read-only and why?","a":"<p>Add <code>:ro</code>, as in <code>-v config:/etc/app:ro</code>, or <code>readonly</code> in <code>--mount</code>. It stops the container from changing the data, which limits the damage of a compromised or buggy app.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0162","card":"q-docker-019-volumes-vs-bind-mounts","sec":"docker-s020","q":"In Docker, what is the difference between a named volume and an anonymous volume?","a":"<p>A named volume has a name you choose and is easy to reuse and back up. An anonymous volume gets a random ID, for example from a <code>VOLUME</code> instruction or <code>-v /data</code>, and is hard to find later, so it tends to be left behind.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0163","card":"q-docker-020-managing-volumes","sec":"docker-s021","q":"How do you create and manage volumes?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker volume create myvol\ndocker volume ls\ndocker volume rm myvol\ndocker volume prune</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0164","card":"q-docker-020-managing-volumes","sec":"docker-s021","q":"How do you mount a local directory?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker run -v /host:/container nginx\ndocker run -d -v /local/path:/usr/share/nginx/html nginx</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0165","card":"q-docker-020-managing-volumes","sec":"docker-s021","q":"How do you create a volume and attach it to a container?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker volume create my-volume\ndocker run -d -v my-volume:/data nginx</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0166","card":"q-docker-020-managing-volumes","sec":"docker-s021","q":"In Docker, what does <code>-v my-volume:/data</code> do if <code>my-volume</code> does not exist yet?","a":"<p>Docker creates the named volume automatically. A source starting with <code>/</code> is treated as a host path (bind mount). A source without a slash is treated as a volume name.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0167","card":"q-docker-020-managing-volumes","sec":"docker-s021","q":"In Docker, what does <code>docker volume prune</code> delete?","a":"<p>It removes volumes not used by any container. In Docker Engine 23.0 and later it prunes only anonymous volumes by default and needs <code>--all</code> (<code>-a</code>) to include unused named volumes. In older versions it removed all unused volumes, so check the version before running it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0168","card":"q-docker-020-managing-volumes","sec":"docker-s021","q":"Why does <code>docker volume rm myvol</code> fail, and how do you fix it?","a":"<p>The volume is still referenced by a container, even a stopped one. Find it with <code>docker ps -a --filter volume=myvol</code>, remove that container, then remove the volume.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0169","card":"q-docker-020-managing-volumes","sec":"docker-s021","q":"Where does Docker store a named volume's data on a Linux host, and how do you find it?","a":"<p>Under <code>/var/lib/docker/volumes/&lt;name&gt;/_data</code>. <code>docker volume inspect &lt;name&gt;</code> shows the exact <code>Mountpoint</code>. You should not edit this directory by hand on production systems.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0170","card":"q-docker-020-managing-volumes","sec":"docker-s021","q":"How do you share data between two Docker containers, and what is the risk?","a":"<p>Mount the same named volume in both, or use <code>--volumes-from &lt;container&gt;</code>. The risk is concurrent writes: Docker adds no locking, so the apps must coordinate access.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0171","card":"q-docker-021-tmpfs-storage-drivers-and-backup","sec":"docker-s022","q":"What is a tmpfs mount and when do you use it?","a":"<p>RAM-based storage for temporary data.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker run --tmpfs /tmp</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0172","card":"q-docker-021-tmpfs-storage-drivers-and-backup","sec":"docker-s022","q":"How do you pick a storage driver?","a":"<p><strong>overlay2</strong> (fast, default) or <strong>devicemapper</strong> (enterprise). Match the OS and workload.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0173","card":"q-docker-021-tmpfs-storage-drivers-and-backup","sec":"docker-s022","q":"How do you back up volume data?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker run -v myvol:/data busybox tar cvf backup.tar /data</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0174","card":"q-docker-021-tmpfs-storage-drivers-and-backup","sec":"docker-s022","q":"Why use a tmpfs mount in Docker, and what are its limits?","a":"<p>It stores data in host memory only. Use it for scratch data or secrets that must never be written to disk. Data is lost when the container stops, and it uses RAM, so set a size, for example <code>--tmpfs /tmp:rw,size=64m</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0175","card":"q-docker-021-tmpfs-storage-drivers-and-backup","sec":"docker-s022","q":"How can <code>--read-only</code> combine with tmpfs for container hardening?","a":"<p><code>docker run --read-only --tmpfs /tmp nginx</code> makes the root file system read-only, so malware or bugs cannot modify it, while still letting the app write temporary files to <code>/tmp</code> in memory.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0176","card":"q-docker-021-tmpfs-storage-drivers-and-backup","sec":"docker-s022","q":"How does the <code>overlay2</code> storage driver build a container's file system?","a":"<p>It stacks the read-only image layers (lowerdirs) and a writable container layer (upperdir) into one merged view. Reads fall through to lower layers. A write to an existing file copies it up to the upper layer first (copy-on-write). Deleting a file adds a marker that hides it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0177","card":"q-docker-021-tmpfs-storage-drivers-and-backup","sec":"docker-s022","q":"Why should write-heavy data such as a database not live in the container's writable layer?","a":"<p>Copy-on-write through the storage driver adds overhead, and the data is lost when the container is removed. Volumes bypass the storage driver and persist independently.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0178","card":"q-docker-021-tmpfs-storage-drivers-and-backup","sec":"docker-s022","q":"In Docker, what is wrong with this volume backup command and how do you fix it?","a":"<p><code>backup.tar</code> is written inside the temporary container's own file system, which disappears when the container is removed. Mount a host directory too: <code>docker run --rm -v myvol:/data -v \"$PWD\":/backup busybox tar cvf /backup/backup.tar /data</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker run -v myvol:/data busybox tar cvf backup.tar /data</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"docker-f0179","card":"q-docker-021-tmpfs-storage-drivers-and-backup","sec":"docker-s022","q":"Is copying a live database's volume files a good backup in Docker?","a":"<p>Not reliably. Files may be mid-write and inconsistent. Use the database's own tools (<code>pg_dump</code>, <code>mysqldump</code>) or stop the container or take a consistent snapshot first.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0180","card":"q-docker-021-tmpfs-storage-drivers-and-backup","sec":"docker-s022","q":"How does an image's layer storage in Docker differ between <code>overlay2</code> and older drivers such as <code>aufs</code>, <code>devicemapper</code> and <code>vfs</code>?","a":"<p><code>overlay2</code> is the default and recommended driver on modern Linux. It is in the mainline kernel and is fast and efficient with inodes. <code>aufs</code> is not in the mainline kernel, so it is no longer recommended. <code>devicemapper</code> (especially loop-lvm mode) is slow and deprecated. <code>vfs</code> has no copy-on-write and copies the whole layer for each container, so it is very slow and used mainly for testing or special cases.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0181","card":"q-docker-022-compose-basics","sec":"docker-s023","q":"Docker Compose vs Docker?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Compose</strong>: multi-container orchestration.</li>\n  <li><strong>Docker</strong>: single-container runtime.</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0182","card":"q-docker-022-compose-basics","sec":"docker-s023","q":"What is the structure of <code>docker-compose.yml</code>?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>version: &quot;3.8&quot;\nservices:\n    web:\n        image: nginx</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0183","card":"q-docker-022-compose-basics","sec":"docker-s023","q":"How do you create a compose file with two services?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code># docker-compose.yml\nversion: &quot;3&quot;\nservices:\n  web:\n     image: nginx\n     ports:\n        - &quot;80:80&quot;\n  app:\n     image: busybox\n     command: sleep 3600</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0184","card":"q-docker-022-compose-basics","sec":"docker-s023","q":"What problem does Docker Compose solve compared with running several <code>docker run</code> commands?","a":"<p>It describes a multi-container app (services, networks, volumes, env, ports) in one YAML file that you version and start with one command. This makes setups repeatable, removes long command lines and gives you a shared default network.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0185","card":"q-docker-022-compose-basics","sec":"docker-s023","q":"In Docker Compose V2 and the Compose Specification, what happens to the top-level <code>version:</code> key?","a":"<p>It is obsolete. Compose V2 ignores it and prints a warning. The file is validated against the current specification instead.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0186","card":"q-docker-022-compose-basics","sec":"docker-s023","q":"What is the difference between <code>docker-compose</code> and <code>docker compose</code>?","a":"<p><code>docker-compose</code> is the old standalone Python tool (V1, end of life in 2023). <code>docker compose</code> is Compose V2, a Go plugin of the Docker CLI. The commands are almost the same, so new work should use <code>docker compose</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0187","card":"q-docker-022-compose-basics","sec":"docker-s023","q":"In Docker Compose, how do services reach each other and what are the project name and default network?","a":"<p>Compose creates a default network named <code>&lt;project&gt;_default</code>, where the project name comes from the directory unless you set <code>-p</code> or <code>name:</code>. Each service is reachable by its service name through Docker DNS.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0188","card":"q-docker-022-compose-basics","sec":"docker-s023","q":"Why should port mappings in a Compose file be quoted strings such as <code>\"80:80\"</code>?","a":"<p>Unquoted values like <code>22:22</code> can be parsed by YAML as a base-60 number. Quoting always yields a string, as Docker expects.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0189","card":"q-docker-022-compose-basics","sec":"docker-s023","q":"When is Compose enough, and when do you move to Kubernetes or Swarm?","a":"<p>Compose fits local development, tests and single-host deployments. It does not provide multi-host scheduling, self-healing across nodes, rolling updates or autoscaling. For those needs use an orchestrator.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0190","card":"q-docker-022-compose-basics","sec":"docker-s023","q":"In Docker Compose, what are <code>profiles</code> used for?","a":"<p>A service with <code>profiles</code> starts only when its profile is enabled, for example <code>docker compose --profile debug up</code> or <code>COMPOSE_PROFILES=debug</code>. Services without <code>profiles</code> always start. Use it for optional services such as debug tools, test runners or seed jobs in one Compose file.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>services:\n  web:\n    image: nginx\n  debug-tools:\n    image: busybox\n    profiles: [&quot;debug&quot;]</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"docker-f0191","card":"q-docker-022-compose-basics","sec":"docker-s023","q":"In a Docker Compose service, what is the difference between <code>build:</code> and <code>image:</code>, and what happens when both are set?","a":"<p><code>image:</code> pulls a ready image from a registry. <code>build:</code> builds the image from a Dockerfile in the given context. With both, Compose builds the image and tags it with the <code>image:</code> name, which is useful when you want to push it afterwards. <code>docker compose up --build</code> forces a rebuild.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0192","card":"q-docker-022-compose-basics","sec":"docker-s023","q":"How do you pass a secret to a service in Docker Compose without using an environment variable?","a":"<p>Define it under the top-level <code>secrets:</code> key (from a <code>file:</code> or an <code>environment:</code> source) and grant it to the service with <code>secrets:</code>. Compose mounts it read-only as a file at <code>/run/secrets/db_password</code>, so it does not appear in <code>docker inspect</code> environment output. Only services that list the secret can read it.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>services:\n  app:\n    image: myapp\n    secrets:\n      - db_password\nsecrets:\n  db_password:\n    file: ./db_password.txt</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"docker-f0193","card":"q-docker-023-running-compose-services","sec":"docker-s024","q":"How do you start and stop Compose services?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker-compose up\ndocker-compose stop\ndocker-compose down</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0194","card":"q-docker-023-running-compose-services","sec":"docker-s024","q":"<code>up</code> vs <code>up -d</code>?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>up</strong>: foreground.</li>\n  <li><strong>up -d</strong>: detached.</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0195","card":"q-docker-023-running-compose-services","sec":"docker-s024","q":"How do you scale a service?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker-compose up -d --scale web=3</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0196","card":"q-docker-023-running-compose-services","sec":"docker-s024","q":"What does <code>docker-compose down</code> do?","a":"<p>It stops and removes containers and networks. Add <code>--volumes</code> for a full cleanup.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0197","card":"q-docker-023-running-compose-services","sec":"docker-s024","q":"In Docker Compose, if the image already exists, does <code>docker compose up</code> rebuild it after you change source files?","a":"<p>No. It reuses the existing image. Use <code>docker compose up --build</code> or <code>docker compose build</code> to rebuild when you change a service with a <code>build:</code> section.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0198","card":"q-docker-023-running-compose-services","sec":"docker-s024","q":"What is the difference between <code>docker compose stop</code> and <code>docker compose down</code>?","a":"<p><code>stop</code> stops the containers but keeps them (and networks and volumes), so <code>start</code> brings them back. <code>down</code> removes the containers and the network. Volumes are kept unless you add <code>-v</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0199","card":"q-docker-023-running-compose-services","sec":"docker-s024","q":"In Docker Compose, what is the difference between <code>docker compose run</code> and <code>docker compose exec</code>?","a":"<p><code>run</code> starts a new one-off container for a service, for example for migrations or tests. <code>exec</code> runs a command in a container that is already running. <code>run</code> ignores published ports by default.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0200","card":"q-docker-023-running-compose-services","sec":"docker-s024","q":"When you rerun <code>docker compose up -d</code> after editing one service's config, what is recreated?","a":"<p>Compose compares the configuration and recreates only services whose config or image changed. Others keep running. <code>--force-recreate</code> recreates all of them.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0201","card":"q-docker-023-running-compose-services","sec":"docker-s024","q":"How do you see logs and status of a Compose app?","a":"<p><code>docker compose ps</code> lists services and state. <code>docker compose logs -f [service]</code> follows the merged logs with service prefixes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0202","card":"q-docker-024-environment-ordering-and-overrides","sec":"docker-s025","q":"How do you define environment variables?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>environment:\n    - KEY=value</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0203","card":"q-docker-024-environment-ordering-and-overrides","sec":"docker-s025","q":"What is the purpose of <code>depends_on</code>?","a":"<p>It sets start order, for example <code>depends_on: [db]</code>.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0204","card":"q-docker-024-environment-ordering-and-overrides","sec":"docker-s025","q":"How do you override Compose settings?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker-compose -f base.yml -f override.yml up</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0205","card":"q-docker-024-environment-ordering-and-overrides","sec":"docker-s025","q":"How do you link services in Compose?","a":"<p>They are auto-linked via DNS on the default network.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0206","card":"q-docker-024-environment-ordering-and-overrides","sec":"docker-s025","q":"Does <code>depends_on</code> make Docker Compose wait until a database is ready to accept connections?","a":"<p>No. By default it only controls start order, which means the database container is started, not that it is ready. Add a healthcheck and use <code>depends_on: db: condition: service_healthy</code>, or let the app retry its connection.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0207","card":"q-docker-024-environment-ordering-and-overrides","sec":"docker-s025","q":"In Docker Compose, what is the difference between <code>environment</code>, <code>env_file</code> and the <code>.env</code> file?","a":"<p><code>environment</code> sets variables in the container. <code>env_file</code> loads a file of variables into the container. A <code>.env</code> file next to the Compose file is read by Compose itself to substitute <code>${VAR}</code> in the YAML. Values in <code>environment</code> override <code>env_file</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0208","card":"q-docker-024-environment-ordering-and-overrides","sec":"docker-s025","q":"How do Docker Compose override files work?","a":"<p>A file named <code>docker-compose.override.yml</code> is merged automatically over <code>docker-compose.yml</code>, or you pass files with <code>-f</code>. Later files override scalar values and merge maps. Lists such as <code>ports</code> are appended. A common use is a base file plus dev and prod overrides.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0209","card":"q-docker-024-environment-ordering-and-overrides","sec":"docker-s025","q":"How do you check the final configuration Docker Compose will use after merging files and substituting variables?","a":"<p>Run <code>docker compose config</code>. It prints the resolved file, which also validates the syntax.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0210","card":"q-docker-025-swarm-concepts","sec":"docker-s026","q":"Swarm vs Kubernetes?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Swarm</strong>: simple, Docker-native.</li>\n  <li><strong>K8s</strong>: complex, feature-rich.</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0211","card":"q-docker-025-swarm-concepts","sec":"docker-s026","q":"Manager vs worker node?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Manager</strong>: runs the cluster.</li>\n  <li><strong>Worker</strong>: runs tasks.</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0212","card":"q-docker-025-swarm-concepts","sec":"docker-s026","q":"What is the purpose of <code>docker service</code>?","a":"<p>It manages Swarm services: create, list, update.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0213","card":"q-docker-025-swarm-concepts","sec":"docker-s026","q":"How does Swarm load balance?","a":"<p>A built-in routing mesh spreads traffic.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0214","card":"q-docker-025-swarm-concepts","sec":"docker-s026","q":"In Docker Swarm, why should you run an odd number of manager nodes?","a":"<p>Managers use the Raft consensus protocol, which needs a majority (quorum) to work. With N managers it tolerates (N-1)/2 failures, so 3 tolerate one and 5 tolerate two. An even number adds cost without more fault tolerance. Do not go beyond 7.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0215","card":"q-docker-025-swarm-concepts","sec":"docker-s026","q":"In Docker Swarm, what is the difference between a service, a task and a container?","a":"<p>A service is the desired state (image, replicas, ports). The scheduler splits it into tasks, and each task is one container slot assigned to a node. The container is the running instance of a task. If it dies, Swarm creates a new task.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0216","card":"q-docker-025-swarm-concepts","sec":"docker-s026","q":"What does it mean that Swarm reconciles desired state?","a":"<p>You declare that a service should have 3 replicas. The manager continuously compares the real state with it and starts or replaces tasks to match, for example when a node fails. This gives self-healing without manual action.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0217","card":"q-docker-025-swarm-concepts","sec":"docker-s026","q":"How does the Swarm routing mesh work?","a":"<p>Every node listens on a service's published port. A request to any node is forwarded over the ingress overlay network to a node that runs a task of that service, and tasks are load balanced round robin. Clients can use any node's address.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0218","card":"q-docker-025-swarm-concepts","sec":"docker-s026","q":"Which ports must be open between Swarm nodes?","a":"<p>TCP 2377 for cluster management, TCP and UDP 7946 for node communication, and UDP 4789 for overlay network traffic.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0219","card":"q-docker-025-swarm-concepts","sec":"docker-s026","q":"In Docker Swarm, do manager nodes run service tasks?","a":"<p>By default yes. To keep managers for control only, drain them with <code>docker node update --availability drain &lt;node&gt;</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0220","card":"q-docker-025-swarm-concepts","sec":"docker-s026","q":"When would you choose Swarm over Kubernetes?","a":"<p>For small teams and simple clusters that already use Docker and Compose files, where low operational cost matters. Kubernetes is better when you need a larger ecosystem, custom resources, advanced scheduling, autoscaling and broad cloud support.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0221","card":"q-docker-025-swarm-concepts","sec":"docker-s026","q":"In Docker Swarm, what is the difference between <code>docker config</code> and <code>docker secret</code>?","a":"<p>Both distribute data to service tasks through the Swarm Raft store. Secrets are for sensitive values: they are encrypted at rest and mounted in memory at <code>/run/secrets/&lt;name&gt;</code>. Configs are for non-sensitive files, such as an nginx.conf, are not encrypted, and are mounted as files in the container. Both let you change content without rebuilding the image.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0222","card":"q-docker-025-swarm-concepts","sec":"docker-s026","q":"In Docker Swarm, how do the VIP and <code>dnsrr</code> endpoint modes of a service differ?","a":"<p>In the default VIP mode, the service name resolves to one virtual IP and Swarm load balances across the tasks behind it. In <code>dnsrr</code> mode (<code>--endpoint-mode dnsrr</code>) the service name resolves to the individual task IPs and the client does the balancing, which suits clients that need real addresses or their own balancer. <code>dnsrr</code> cannot be used with ingress routing-mesh published ports, so use host-mode ports.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0223","card":"q-docker-025-swarm-concepts","sec":"docker-s026","q":"How do you protect and rotate join tokens in Docker Swarm?","a":"<p>Join tokens decide who can add worker or manager nodes, so treat them as secrets. Show one with <code>docker swarm join-token worker</code> (or <code>manager</code>). If one leaks, run <code>docker swarm join-token --rotate worker</code> to issue a new token. Existing nodes stay in the cluster, and the old token stops working for new joins.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0224","card":"q-docker-026-creating-and-scaling-services","sec":"docker-s027","q":"How do you initialize Swarm?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker swarm init</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0225","card":"q-docker-026-creating-and-scaling-services","sec":"docker-s027","q":"How do you deploy a Swarm service?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker service create --name web -p 80:80 nginx</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0226","card":"q-docker-026-creating-and-scaling-services","sec":"docker-s027","q":"What is a stack in Swarm?","a":"<p>A multi-service YAML file.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker stack deploy -c stack.yml mystack</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0227","card":"q-docker-026-creating-and-scaling-services","sec":"docker-s027","q":"How do you scale in Swarm?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker service scale web=5</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0228","card":"q-docker-026-creating-and-scaling-services","sec":"docker-s027","q":"In Docker Swarm, what is the difference between replicated and global services?","a":"<p>A replicated service runs a number of replicas that you set. A global service runs exactly one task on every node, which suits agents such as log collectors and monitoring.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0229","card":"q-docker-026-creating-and-scaling-services","sec":"docker-s027","q":"When does <code>docker swarm init</code> need <code>--advertise-addr</code>?","a":"<p>When the host has several network interfaces or IPs. It tells other nodes which address to use to reach this manager. Without it the command can fail or choose the wrong address.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0230","card":"q-docker-026-creating-and-scaling-services","sec":"docker-s027","q":"In Docker Swarm, what is the difference between <code>docker stack deploy</code> and <code>docker compose up</code>?","a":"<p><code>stack deploy</code> sends a Compose-format file to a Swarm cluster and creates services using the <code>deploy:</code> section (replicas, update and placement settings). <code>build:</code> is ignored, so images must be in a registry that all nodes can pull from. <code>compose up</code> runs plain containers on one host.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0231","card":"q-docker-026-creating-and-scaling-services","sec":"docker-s027","q":"In Docker Swarm, you scale a service to 5 replicas but some tasks stay <code>Pending</code>. What do you check?","a":"<p>Run <code>docker service ps &lt;service&gt;</code> for the error. Typical causes are not enough CPU or memory on nodes, placement constraints that no node satisfies, a missing image on workers (no registry access), or a published port conflict.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0232","card":"q-docker-026-creating-and-scaling-services","sec":"docker-s027","q":"How do you restrict a Swarm service to certain nodes?","a":"<p>Use placement constraints, such as <code>--constraint node.role==worker</code> or a label like <code>node.labels.disk==ssd</code>. In a stack file put them under <code>deploy.placement.constraints</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0233","card":"q-docker-026-creating-and-scaling-services","sec":"docker-s027","q":"How does a worker join a Swarm?","a":"<p>Run <code>docker swarm join --token &lt;worker-token&gt; &lt;manager-ip&gt;:2377</code>. Get the token with <code>docker swarm join-token worker</code> on a manager. Treat the token as a secret.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0234","card":"q-docker-027-updates-rollback-and-node-removal","sec":"docker-s028","q":"How do you update a service with no downtime?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker service update --image nginx:1.19 --update-delay 10s web</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0235","card":"q-docker-027-updates-rollback-and-node-removal","sec":"docker-s028","q":"How do you roll back a Swarm service?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker service rollback web</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0236","card":"q-docker-027-updates-rollback-and-node-removal","sec":"docker-s028","q":"How do you remove a Swarm node?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker swarm leave  # Worker\ndocker node rm &lt;id&gt;  # Manager</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0237","card":"q-docker-027-updates-rollback-and-node-removal","sec":"docker-s028","q":"How do you deploy a multi-container app with Swarm?","a":"<p>Write a stack file, run <code>docker stack deploy</code>, scale, and monitor.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0238","card":"q-docker-027-updates-rollback-and-node-removal","sec":"docker-s028","q":"In Docker Swarm, which options does a rolling update control?","a":"<p><code>--update-delay</code> is the wait between update batches. <code>--update-parallelism</code> sets how many tasks update at once (default 1). <code>--update-failure-action</code> chooses pause, continue or rollback on failure. <code>--update-order start-first</code> starts the new task before stopping the old one, which avoids a capacity dip.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker service update --image nginx:1.19 --update-delay 10s web</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"docker-f0239","card":"q-docker-027-updates-rollback-and-node-removal","sec":"docker-s028","q":"What makes a Swarm rolling update really zero-downtime?","a":"<p>A healthcheck on the image so Swarm knows when a new task is ready, <code>start-first</code> ordering, enough replicas, parallelism low enough to keep capacity, and an app that shuts down gracefully. Without a healthcheck, a task counts as ready when its process starts.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0240","card":"q-docker-027-updates-rollback-and-node-removal","sec":"docker-s028","q":"How can a Swarm update roll back automatically if the new version fails?","a":"<p>Set <code>--update-failure-action rollback</code> (and optionally <code>--rollback-*</code> options). Swarm reverts to the previous service spec when the update fails. <code>docker service rollback</code> does it manually.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0241","card":"q-docker-027-updates-rollback-and-node-removal","sec":"docker-s028","q":"How do you safely take a node out of a Docker Swarm?","a":"<p>Drain it with <code>docker node update --availability drain &lt;node&gt;</code> so tasks move elsewhere. A worker then runs <code>docker swarm leave</code>, and you remove it from a manager with <code>docker node rm</code>. Demote a manager to a worker first so quorum is not hurt.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0242","card":"q-docker-027-updates-rollback-and-node-removal","sec":"docker-s028","q":"A Swarm lost its quorum because most managers failed. How do you recover?","a":"<p>On a surviving manager run <code>docker swarm init --force-new-cluster --advertise-addr &lt;ip&gt;</code>. It makes that node a single-manager cluster with the existing state. Then add managers again.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0243","card":"q-docker-028-securing-containers","sec":"docker-s029","q":"What are the best practices for securing containers?","a":"<p>Run as non-root, use minimal images, scan images, limit resources, and use secrets.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0244","card":"q-docker-028-securing-containers","sec":"docker-s029","q":"How do you run a container as non-root?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Dockerfile</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>RUN useradd -m appuser\nUSER appuser</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0245","card":"q-docker-028-securing-containers","sec":"docker-s029","q":"What are the risks of <code>--privileged</code>?","a":"<p>It gives full host access, a huge security hole.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0246","card":"q-docker-028-securing-containers","sec":"docker-s029","q":"What is the role of namespaces and cgroups?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Namespaces</strong>: isolate.</li>\n  <li><strong>Cgroups</strong>: limit resources.</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0247","card":"q-docker-028-securing-containers","sec":"docker-s029","q":"How do you set resource limits?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker run --cpus=&quot;1&quot; -m 256m</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0248","card":"q-docker-028-securing-containers","sec":"docker-s029","q":"Why is running as root inside a container risky even though it is isolated?","a":"<p>Root in the container is UID 0 on the host unless user namespaces are used. A breakout or a mounted host path gives the attacker root access to the host. Run as a non-root user so a compromise has fewer rights.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0249","card":"q-docker-028-securing-containers","sec":"docker-s029","q":"In this Dockerfile, where should the <code>USER</code> instruction go and why?","a":"<p>After the steps that need root (installing packages, creating directories), and before <code>CMD</code>. Every later instruction runs as that user, so <code>apt-get install</code> after <code>USER</code> would fail. Use <code>COPY --chown=appuser</code> for files the user must own.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Dockerfile</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>RUN useradd -m appuser\nUSER appuser</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"docker-f0250","card":"q-docker-028-securing-containers","sec":"docker-s029","q":"Which options reduce a container's privileges in Docker?","a":"<p><code>--cap-drop ALL</code> and then <code>--cap-add</code> only what is needed, <code>--security-opt no-new-privileges</code>, <code>--read-only</code> root file system, the default seccomp profile, and AppArmor or SELinux profiles. Avoid <code>--privileged</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0251","card":"q-docker-028-securing-containers","sec":"docker-s029","q":"Instead of <code>--privileged</code>, how do you give a container one extra permission in Docker?","a":"<p>Add only the capability it needs, for example <code>--cap-add NET_ADMIN</code>, or pass a specific device with <code>--device</code>. <code>--privileged</code> grants all capabilities and device access.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0252","card":"q-docker-028-securing-containers","sec":"docker-s029","q":"What do rootless mode and user namespace remapping (<code>userns-remap</code>) give you in Docker?","a":"<p>Both map root inside containers to an unprivileged user on the host. Rootless mode also runs the daemon without root. A container breakout then lands with limited rights. Some features such as low ports and certain network modes are restricted.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0253","card":"q-docker-028-securing-containers","sec":"docker-s029","q":"How does pinning base images improve security?","a":"<p>A digest pin (<code>image@sha256:...</code>) guarantees the same content on every build, so a changed upstream tag cannot slip in unreviewed code. Pair it with regular rebuilds and scanning so you still receive patches.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0254","card":"q-docker-028-securing-containers","sec":"docker-s029","q":"What is a seccomp profile in Docker, and when would you customise it?","a":"<p>Seccomp is a Linux kernel feature that filters which system calls a process may make. Docker applies a default profile that allows most syscalls and blocks dozens of dangerous ones, for example <code>mount</code> and <code>reboot</code>-style calls. Customise it with <code>--security-opt seccomp=profile.json</code> when you want a stricter allowlist for a known workload or need one blocked syscall. <code>seccomp=unconfined</code> removes the filter and should be avoided.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0255","card":"q-docker-029-content-trust-scanning-and-secrets","sec":"docker-s030","q":"What is Docker Content Trust?","a":"<p>It ensures images are signed.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>DOCKER_CONTENT_TRUST=1</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0256","card":"q-docker-029-content-trust-scanning-and-secrets","sec":"docker-s030","q":"How do you scan an image for vulnerabilities?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker scan nginx:latest</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0257","card":"q-docker-029-content-trust-scanning-and-secrets","sec":"docker-s030","q":"What is a Docker secret?","a":"<p>A way to store sensitive data securely.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker secret create mysecret data</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0258","card":"q-docker-029-content-trust-scanning-and-secrets","sec":"docker-s030","q":"What does Docker Content Trust protect against?","a":"<p>When <code>DOCKER_CONTENT_TRUST=1</code>, pulls and runs accept only images with a valid signature (built on Notary). It protects against tampered images or a compromised registry. Many teams now use Sigstore cosign for signing and verification.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0259","card":"q-docker-029-content-trust-scanning-and-secrets","sec":"docker-s030","q":"Why scan images in CI and also keep scanning images that are already deployed?","a":"<p>A scan only knows vulnerabilities published up to that time. An image that was clean at build can become vulnerable when new CVEs appear. Scan during CI to block bad builds and re-scan registries and running images regularly.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0260","card":"q-docker-029-content-trust-scanning-and-secrets","sec":"docker-s030","q":"Which Docker command replaced the Snyk-backed <code>docker scan</code>, and from roughly when?","a":"<p>Around 2023 and later, <code>docker scan</code> was deprecated and replaced by Docker Scout (<code>docker scout cves &lt;image&gt;</code>). Open-source scanners such as Trivy and Grype are also common in pipelines.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0261","card":"q-docker-029-content-trust-scanning-and-secrets","sec":"docker-s030","q":"In Docker Swarm, what does the last argument of this command mean?","a":"<p>It is the source of the secret value: a file path, or <code>-</code> to read standard input. The secret is stored encrypted in the Swarm Raft log.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker secret create mysecret data</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"docker-f0262","card":"q-docker-029-content-trust-scanning-and-secrets","sec":"docker-s030","q":"How does a Swarm service read a Docker secret, and why is that better than an environment variable?","a":"<p>Swarm mounts the secret as an in-memory file at <code>/run/secrets/&lt;name&gt;</code> only in the containers of services that were granted it. Environment variables show up in <code>docker inspect</code>, child processes and crash dumps, and secrets are not stored in the image.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0263","card":"q-docker-029-content-trust-scanning-and-secrets","sec":"docker-s030","q":"Why must you not put a secret in an <code>ENV</code> or <code>ARG</code> in a Dockerfile?","a":"<p>Both stay readable in the image metadata and <code>docker history</code>. Use build secrets with BuildKit (<code>RUN --mount=type=secret,id=token ...</code>), which are not stored in a layer, and supply runtime secrets from a secrets manager or Docker secrets.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0264","card":"q-docker-029-content-trust-scanning-and-secrets","sec":"docker-s030","q":"In Docker, how do you pass a build-time secret with BuildKit so it does not end up in an image layer?","a":"<p>Build with <code>docker build --secret id=token,src=token.txt .</code>. The secret is mounted only during that <code>RUN</code> at <code>/run/secrets/token</code> and is not saved in a layer or in <code>docker history</code>, unlike <code>ARG</code> or <code>ENV</code>. This needs BuildKit, which is the default builder in Docker Engine 23.0 and later.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Dockerfile</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code># syntax=docker/dockerfile:1\nFROM alpine\nRUN --mount=type=secret,id=token cat /run/secrets/token &gt; /dev/null</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"docker-f0265","card":"q-docker-030-restricting-traffic-and-host-access","sec":"docker-s031","q":"How do you restrict container traffic?","a":"<p>Use custom networks, <code>--icc=false</code> and firewall rules.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0266","card":"q-docker-030-restricting-traffic-and-host-access","sec":"docker-s031","q":"How do you prevent host file system access?","a":"<p>No binds to the root, read-only mounts, and dropped capabilities.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0267","card":"q-docker-030-restricting-traffic-and-host-access","sec":"docker-s031","q":"What does <code>--icc=false</code> do in Docker, and why is it limited?","a":"<p>It is a daemon option for the default bridge that disables container-to-container traffic unless links are used. It is legacy. The better way is separate user-defined networks, so only containers that need each other share a network.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0268","card":"q-docker-030-restricting-traffic-and-host-access","sec":"docker-s031","q":"How do you create a Docker network with no outbound internet access?","a":"<p>Use <code>docker network create --internal backend</code>. Containers on it can talk to each other but are not routed to the outside. This suits databases that only the application tier needs to reach.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0269","card":"q-docker-030-restricting-traffic-and-host-access","sec":"docker-s031","q":"Which host paths should you never mount into a container in Docker?","a":"<p><code>/</code>, <code>/etc</code>, <code>/proc</code>, <code>/sys</code>, <code>/var/run/docker.sock</code> and other sensitive paths. If mounting is needed, use a narrow directory and <code>:ro</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0270","card":"q-docker-030-restricting-traffic-and-host-access","sec":"docker-s031","q":"How do you limit what a container can send out on the network in Docker?","a":"<p>Use internal networks, firewall rules in the <code>DOCKER-USER</code> iptables chain, or egress controls at the cloud layer (security groups, network policies). Docker does not offer an egress filter on its own.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0271","card":"q-docker-031-podman-logging-and-plugins","sec":"docker-s032","q":"Docker vs Podman?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Docker</strong>: daemon-based.</li>\n  <li><strong>Podman</strong>: daemonless and rootless.</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0272","card":"q-docker-031-podman-logging-and-plugins","sec":"docker-s032","q":"How does Docker logging work?","a":"<p>It captures stdout. Drivers include <code>json-file</code>, <code>syslog</code> and <code>none</code>.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0273","card":"q-docker-031-podman-logging-and-plugins","sec":"docker-s032","q":"What are Docker plugins?","a":"<p>They extend Docker's features.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker plugin install rexray</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0274","card":"q-docker-031-podman-logging-and-plugins","sec":"docker-s032","q":"What is the main architectural difference between Podman and Docker, and why does it matter?","a":"<p>Podman has no central daemon. Each container is started by a normal process (fork/exec), and rootless is the default. There is no privileged background service as a single point of failure or attack. The CLI is mostly compatible with Docker's, and Podman integrates well with systemd.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0275","card":"q-docker-031-podman-logging-and-plugins","sec":"docker-s032","q":"When would you pick a logging driver other than <code>json-file</code> in Docker?","a":"<p>When logs must leave the host: <code>awslogs</code> for CloudWatch, <code>fluentd</code>, <code>gelf</code> or <code>syslog</code> for central systems, <code>journald</code> on systemd hosts. The <code>local</code> driver is a rotated, compact local option. The trade-off is that remote drivers add a dependency on the log service.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0276","card":"q-docker-031-podman-logging-and-plugins","sec":"docker-s032","q":"What kinds of Docker plugins exist and why would you use one?","a":"<p>Volume plugins (external storage such as NFS or cloud disks), network plugins, log drivers and authorization plugins. They let Docker use storage or networking that is not built in. Install and manage them with <code>docker plugin</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0277","card":"q-docker-032-registries","sec":"docker-s033","q":"What is a Docker registry and how do you set up a private one?","a":"<p>A registry stores images.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker run -d -p 5000:5000 registry:2</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0278","card":"q-docker-032-registries","sec":"docker-s033","q":"How do you push to Docker Hub?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker push username/myapp:tag</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0279","card":"q-docker-032-registries","sec":"docker-s033","q":"How is a Docker image name structured, and where does it pull from when no registry is given?","a":"<p><code>registry-host/namespace/repository:tag</code>, for example <code>ghcr.io/acme/app:1.2</code>. With no host (<code>nginx</code> or <code>acme/app</code>) Docker uses Docker Hub (<code>docker.io</code>).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0280","card":"q-docker-032-registries","sec":"docker-s033","q":"To push <code>myapp</code> to your own registry in Docker, why do you tag it first?","a":"<p>The registry host and repository are part of the image name. <code>docker tag myapp registry.example.com/team/myapp:1.0</code> creates that name, and <code>docker push</code> uses the name to decide where to send the image.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0281","card":"q-docker-032-registries","sec":"docker-s033","q":"What do you need to run a private registry safely in production with <code>registry:2</code>?","a":"<p>TLS and authentication, since Docker clients refuse plain HTTP registries other than localhost unless listed under <code>insecure-registries</code>. Add persistent storage with a volume or object storage and a garbage-collection plan. Managed options such as ECR, GHCR and Harbor handle much of this.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0282","card":"q-docker-032-registries","sec":"docker-s033","q":"How do Docker clients store registry credentials and how do you secure them?","a":"<p><code>docker login</code> saves them in <code>~/.docker/config.json</code>, base64 encoded and not encrypted unless a credential helper is set. Use a credential helper (keychain, <code>pass</code>) or short-lived tokens in CI.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0283","card":"q-docker-032-registries","sec":"docker-s033","q":"Why use a pull-through cache or your own registry in front of Docker Hub?","a":"<p>Docker Hub limits pulls, and the limits have changed over time, especially for anonymous users. A cache or mirror avoids failures from rate limiting, speeds up pulls and keeps builds working if the public registry is down.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0284","card":"q-docker-033-health-checks","sec":"docker-s034","q":"What is the purpose of HEALTHCHECK?","a":"<p>It monitors container health.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Dockerfile</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>HEALTHCHECK --interval=30s --timeout=3s --retries=3 CMD curl -f http://localhost/ || exit 1</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0285","card":"q-docker-033-health-checks","sec":"docker-s034","q":"In this Dockerfile line, what do the options and the command do?","a":"<p>Every 30 seconds Docker runs the command inside the container. If it takes over 3 seconds or fails, it counts as a failure. After 3 consecutive failures the status becomes <code>unhealthy</code>. <code>curl -f</code> returns non-zero on HTTP errors, and <code>|| exit 1</code> turns any failure into exit code 1.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Dockerfile</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>HEALTHCHECK --interval=30s --timeout=3s --retries=3 CMD curl -f http://localhost/ || exit 1</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"docker-f0286","card":"q-docker-033-health-checks","sec":"docker-s034","q":"What are the health states of a Docker container and what do the exit codes of a healthcheck mean?","a":"<p>States are <code>starting</code>, <code>healthy</code> and <code>unhealthy</code>. The command exit code 0 means healthy and 1 means unhealthy (2 is reserved). <code>--start-period</code> gives a slow app time to start before failures count.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0287","card":"q-docker-033-health-checks","sec":"docker-s034","q":"What is a common mistake when writing a <code>HEALTHCHECK</code> with <code>curl</code> in a slim image?","a":"<p>The image may not contain <code>curl</code>, so the check always fails. Install it, use another tool already in the image such as <code>wget</code>, or write the check in the app's own language.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0288","card":"q-docker-033-health-checks","sec":"docker-s034","q":"In Docker, why is a running container not the same as a healthy application?","a":"<p>Docker only knows the main process is alive. The app can be deadlocked, out of connections or failing every request while the process runs. A healthcheck tests the application behavior itself.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0289","card":"q-docker-034-containerizing-apps","sec":"docker-s035","q":"How do you containerize a Node.js app?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Dockerfile</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>FROM node:16\nCOPY . /app\nRUN npm install\nCMD [&quot;node&quot;, &quot;index.js&quot;]</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0290","card":"q-docker-034-containerizing-apps","sec":"docker-s035","q":"How do you write a Dockerfile for a Flask app?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Dockerfile</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>FROM python:3.9-slim\nCOPY requirements.txt .\nRUN pip install -r requirements.txt\nCOPY . .\nCMD [&quot;flask&quot;, &quot;run&quot;, &quot;--host=0.0.0.0&quot;]</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0291","card":"q-docker-034-containerizing-apps","sec":"docker-s035","q":"How do you migrate an app to Docker?","a":"<p>Write the Dockerfile, build and test, add volumes, then deploy.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0292","card":"q-docker-034-containerizing-apps","sec":"docker-s035","q":"What is wrong with this Dockerfile for a Node.js app?","a":"<p>There is no <code>WORKDIR</code>, so <code>npm install</code> runs in <code>/</code> where there is no <code>package.json</code>, and <code>CMD</code> also runs from <code>/</code>. Copying everything before the install breaks caching, and a host <code>node_modules</code> is copied in unless <code>.dockerignore</code> excludes it. It also runs as root, and <code>node:16</code> is end of life. Better: <code>WORKDIR /app</code>, copy <code>package*.json</code>, <code>RUN npm ci</code>, copy the rest, <code>USER node</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Dockerfile</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>FROM node:16\nCOPY . /app\nRUN npm install\nCMD [&quot;node&quot;, &quot;index.js&quot;]</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"docker-f0293","card":"q-docker-034-containerizing-apps","sec":"docker-s035","q":"In this Flask Dockerfile, why is <code>requirements.txt</code> copied before the rest of the code?","a":"<p>So the <code>pip install</code> layer is cached and only rebuilt when requirements change, not on every code edit.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Dockerfile</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>FROM python:3.9-slim\nCOPY requirements.txt .\nRUN pip install -r requirements.txt\nCOPY . .\nCMD [&quot;flask&quot;, &quot;run&quot;, &quot;--host=0.0.0.0&quot;]</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"docker-f0294","card":"q-docker-034-containerizing-apps","sec":"docker-s035","q":"Why does <code>flask run</code> in a container need <code>--host=0.0.0.0</code>?","a":"<p>Flask listens on 127.0.0.1 by default, which inside a container is reachable only from the container itself, so published ports do not work. <code>0.0.0.0</code> listens on all interfaces. For production use a WSGI server such as gunicorn instead of the Flask development server.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0295","card":"q-docker-034-containerizing-apps","sec":"docker-s035","q":"What should an application satisfy to run well in a container?","a":"<p>Read config from environment variables, write logs to stdout/stderr, keep no state on the local disk (use volumes or external services), handle SIGTERM for graceful shutdown, and expose a health endpoint. These are twelve-factor practices.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0296","card":"q-docker-034-containerizing-apps","sec":"docker-s035","q":"What does <code>.dockerignore</code> exclude in a typical Node or Python image build and why?","a":"<p>Entries such as <code>.git</code>, <code>node_modules</code>, <code>__pycache__</code>, <code>.env</code> and local build output. This keeps the build context small, avoids leaking secrets into the image and avoids copying host-built dependencies over the container's own.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0297","card":"q-docker-034-containerizing-apps","sec":"docker-s035","q":"Why does PID 1 matter for a Node.js or Python process in a Docker container?","a":"<p>The main process runs as PID 1, which gets no default signal handlers, so SIGTERM is ignored unless the app handles it, and PID 1 must also reap orphaned child processes or zombies pile up. Fix it with <code>docker run --init</code> (or <code>init: true</code> in Compose), which runs a tiny init such as tini, and use exec-form <code>CMD</code> or <code>exec \"$@\"</code> in an entrypoint script so the app, not a shell, is PID 1.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0298","card":"q-docker-035-compose-for-microservices","sec":"docker-s036","q":"How do you use Compose for microservices?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>services:\n    frontend:\n        image: node:16\n        ports: [&quot;3000:3000&quot;]\n    backend:\n        image: python:3.9\n        ports: [&quot;5000:5000&quot;]\n    db:\n        image: postgres:13\n        volumes: [db-data:/var/lib/postgresql/data]\nvolumes:\n    db-data:</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0299","card":"q-docker-035-compose-for-microservices","sec":"docker-s036","q":"In this Compose file, why is <code>db-data</code> listed again at the bottom?","a":"<p>Named volumes used by services must be declared in the top-level <code>volumes:</code> section. Compose creates the volume and keeps it when containers are recreated. <code>/var/lib/postgresql/data</code> is where Postgres keeps its data, so the data survives container removal.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>    db:\n        image: postgres:13\n        volumes: [db-data:/var/lib/postgresql/data]\nvolumes:\n    db-data:</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"docker-f0300","card":"q-docker-035-compose-for-microservices","sec":"docker-s036","q":"In a Compose app with <code>frontend</code>, <code>backend</code> and <code>db</code> services, how does the backend reach the database?","a":"<p>By service name on the default network: host <code>db</code>, port 5432. It does not need a published port. Publishing <code>db</code> ports would expose the database to the host network without reason.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0301","card":"q-docker-035-compose-for-microservices","sec":"docker-s036","q":"Why will the <code>postgres</code> service in a Compose file fail without more config?","a":"<p>The official <code>postgres</code> image requires <code>POSTGRES_PASSWORD</code> (or an explicit trust setting) and exits otherwise. Set it through a secret or an env file.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0302","card":"q-docker-035-compose-for-microservices","sec":"docker-s036","q":"How would you stop the frontend container from talking to the database directly in Compose?","a":"<p>Define two networks, <code>frontend</code> and <code>backend</code>. Attach the frontend to <code>frontend</code>, the database to <code>backend</code> only, and the backend service to both.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0303","card":"q-docker-036-ci-cd-and-zero-downtime","sec":"docker-s037","q":"How do you do CI/CD with Docker?","a":"<p>Build and push in CI (for example GitHub Actions), then deploy via Compose or Swarm.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0304","card":"q-docker-036-ci-cd-and-zero-downtime","sec":"docker-s037","q":"How do you do a zero-downtime update?","a":"<p>Use a Swarm rolling update with <code>docker service update</code>.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0305","card":"q-docker-036-ci-cd-and-zero-downtime","sec":"docker-s037","q":"What are the usual stages of a Docker image CI/CD pipeline?","a":"<p>Check out code, build the image, run tests (often in the container), scan for vulnerabilities, tag, push to a registry, then deploy and run smoke checks. Registry credentials come from the CI secret store.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0306","card":"q-docker-036-ci-cd-and-zero-downtime","sec":"docker-s037","q":"Why is <code>docker compose up -d</code> not truly zero-downtime for an updated service?","a":"<p>Compose recreates changed containers by stopping the old one and starting a new one, so there is a gap. For zero downtime run multiple replicas behind a load balancer, update them one at a time with healthchecks, or use Swarm rolling updates, blue-green or canary deployments.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0307","card":"q-docker-036-ci-cd-and-zero-downtime","sec":"docker-s037","q":"What must be true for a zero-downtime Docker deployment to work?","a":"<p>Several replicas behind a load balancer, healthchecks so traffic goes only to ready instances, graceful shutdown, and backward-compatible database changes so old and new versions can run together.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0308","card":"q-docker-036-ci-cd-and-zero-downtime","sec":"docker-s037","q":"How do you speed up Docker image builds in CI where each job starts on a clean runner?","a":"<p>Use BuildKit cache export and import (<code>--cache-from</code>, <code>--cache-to</code>, registry or CI cache backends), order the Dockerfile for caching and use multi-stage builds.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0309","card":"q-docker-036-ci-cd-and-zero-downtime","sec":"docker-s037","q":"In a CI job that must build Docker images, what is the difference between Docker-in-Docker and mounting the host's Docker socket?","a":"<p>Docker-in-Docker runs a separate daemon inside the job container. It needs a privileged container but keeps builds isolated from the host's containers. Mounting <code>/var/run/docker.sock</code> reuses the host daemon: it is faster and shares the layer cache, but the job can control every container on that host, which is effectively root access. Daemonless builders such as Kaniko or rootless BuildKit avoid both problems.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0310","card":"q-docker-038-running-containers-on-aws-compute","sec":"docker-s039","q":"How do you create a Docker container on an EC2 instance?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code># Run on EC2 instance after SSH\ndocker run -d -p 80:80 nginx</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0311","card":"q-docker-038-running-containers-on-aws-compute","sec":"docker-s039","q":"How do you attach a container to an EBS volume on EC2?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code># Run on EC2 instance with EBS volume mounted at /mnt/ebs\ndocker run -d -v /mnt/ebs:/data nginx</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0312","card":"q-docker-038-running-containers-on-aws-compute","sec":"docker-s039","q":"How do you attach a container to an ECS cluster?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code># Assumes ECS task definition is created via AWS CLI or console\ndocker build -t my-app .\ndocker tag my-app &lt;ECR_REGISTRY&gt;/my-app:latest\ndocker push &lt;ECR_REGISTRY&gt;/my-app:latest\n# Use AWS CLI to run task on ECS\naws ecs run-task --cluster my-ecs-cluster --task-definition my-task --launch-type FARGATE --network-configuration &quot;awsvpcConfiguration={subnets=[subnet-12345678],securityGroups=[sg-12345678]}&quot;</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0313","card":"q-docker-038-running-containers-on-aws-compute","sec":"docker-s039","q":"How do you create a Docker image and push it to Amazon ECR?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker build -t my-app .\ndocker tag my-app &lt;ECR_REGISTRY&gt;/my-app:latest\naws ecr get-login-password --region us-east-1 | docker login --username AWS --password-stdin &lt;ECR_REGISTRY&gt;\ndocker push &lt;ECR_REGISTRY&gt;/my-app:latest</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0314","card":"q-docker-038-running-containers-on-aws-compute","sec":"docker-s039","q":"How do you choose between Docker on EC2, ECS and EKS on AWS?","a":"<p>Docker on EC2 is simple but you manage the host, scaling and recovery. ECS is AWS's own orchestrator with simpler concepts and deep AWS integration. EKS runs Kubernetes, which fits teams that need its ecosystem or portability but costs more to operate.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0315","card":"q-docker-038-running-containers-on-aws-compute","sec":"docker-s039","q":"In Amazon ECS, what is the difference between a task definition, a task and a service?","a":"<p>A task definition is the blueprint (images, CPU, memory, ports, roles). A task is a running instance of it. A service keeps a desired number of tasks running, replaces failed ones and can register them with a load balancer. <code>run-task</code> starts one-off tasks, and services are for long-running apps.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0316","card":"q-docker-038-running-containers-on-aws-compute","sec":"docker-s039","q":"What is the difference between the Fargate and EC2 launch types in ECS?","a":"<p>With Fargate AWS runs the compute, so you have no servers to patch and pay per task resources. With EC2 you manage the instances, which gives more control (GPUs, custom AMIs, daemon tasks, cheaper at steady high utilization) but more work.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0317","card":"q-docker-038-running-containers-on-aws-compute","sec":"docker-s039","q":"Why does <code>aws ecs run-task</code> for Fargate need an <code>awsvpcConfiguration</code> with subnets and security groups?","a":"<p>Fargate tasks use the <code>awsvpc</code> network mode, so each task gets its own network interface in your VPC. You must say which subnets and security groups it uses. A task in a public subnet needs a public IP, or a NAT gateway, to pull images.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0318","card":"q-docker-038-running-containers-on-aws-compute","sec":"docker-s039","q":"Why is <code>docker login</code> done through <code>aws ecr get-login-password | docker login --username AWS --password-stdin</code> for ECR?","a":"<p>ECR uses a temporary authorization token (valid 12 hours) instead of a fixed password. <code>--password-stdin</code> keeps the token out of shell history and the process list.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0319","card":"q-docker-038-running-containers-on-aws-compute","sec":"docker-s039","q":"What is the difference between using an EBS volume and EFS for container data on AWS?","a":"<p>An EBS volume attaches to one instance in one Availability Zone, so it suits single-host data. EFS is a shared network file system across AZs and many tasks, so it fits data shared between containers or tasks that move between hosts.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0320","card":"q-docker-038-running-containers-on-aws-compute","sec":"docker-s039","q":"How should a container on EC2 or ECS get AWS permissions?","a":"<p>Through an IAM role (instance profile or ECS task role) with least privilege. The SDK gets temporary credentials automatically. Do not bake access keys into images or pass them as environment variables.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0321","card":"q-docker-039-load-balancer-dns-and-lambda","sec":"docker-s040","q":"How do you attach a container to a load balancer?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code># Assumes ALB is set up with a target group in AWS\ndocker run -d -p 80:80 --name nginx nginx\n# Register container&#x27;s EC2 instance with ALB target group via AWS CLI\naws elbv2 register-targets --target-group-arn &lt;TARGET_GROUP_ARN&gt; --targets Id=&lt;EC2_INSTANCE_ID&gt;,Port=80</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0322","card":"q-docker-039-load-balancer-dns-and-lambda","sec":"docker-s040","q":"How do you attach a container to a Route 53 domain?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker run -d -p 80:80 --name nginx nginx\n# Update Route 53 record via AWS CLI (run separately)\naws route53 change-resource-record-sets --hosted-zone-id &lt;ZONE_ID&gt; --change-batch &#x27;{&quot;Changes&quot;:[{&quot;Action&quot;:&quot;UPSERT&quot;,&quot;ResourceRecordSet&quot;:{&quot;Name&quot;:&quot;nginx.example.com&quot;,&quot;Type&quot;:&quot;A&quot;,&quot;TTL&quot;:300,&quot;ResourceRecords&quot;:[{&quot;Value&quot;:&quot;&lt;EC2_PUBLIC_IP&gt;&quot;}]}}]}&#x27;</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0323","card":"q-docker-039-load-balancer-dns-and-lambda","sec":"docker-s040","q":"How do you attach a container to a Lambda function trigger?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code># Lambda trigger typically set up via AWS console/CLI; container runs app\ndocker run -d -p 8080:8080 my-app\n# Configure Lambda to invoke via API Gateway or event source separately</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0324","card":"q-docker-039-load-balancer-dns-and-lambda","sec":"docker-s040","q":"What does <code>aws elbv2 register-targets</code> do, and what are its limits for containers?","a":"<p>It adds a target (an EC2 instance and port) to an ALB target group so the load balancer sends traffic to it after health checks pass. Doing this by hand does not scale. ECS services register and deregister tasks automatically, and dynamic port mapping lets several tasks share an instance.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0325","card":"q-docker-039-load-balancer-dns-and-lambda","sec":"docker-s040","q":"What is the difference between ALB and NLB for container workloads?","a":"<p>ALB works at layer 7 (HTTP/HTTPS) with path and host routing, so it fits web apps and microservices. NLB works at layer 4 (TCP/UDP/TLS) with very high throughput, low latency and static IPs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0326","card":"q-docker-039-load-balancer-dns-and-lambda","sec":"docker-s040","q":"For a containerized site on AWS, why use a Route 53 alias record to a load balancer instead of an A record to an instance IP?","a":"<p>An instance IP changes when the instance is replaced and gives no failover. An alias record to an ALB follows the load balancer, supports health-based routing and has no query charge for AWS targets. With <code>change-resource-record-sets</code>, <code>UPSERT</code> creates or updates the record.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0327","card":"q-docker-039-load-balancer-dns-and-lambda","sec":"docker-s040","q":"Can you run any Docker image on AWS Lambda?","a":"<p>No. A Lambda container image must implement the Lambda Runtime API (use an AWS base image or add the Runtime Interface Client), is limited to 10 GB, and each invocation runs up to 15 minutes. Lambda calls your handler per event. It does not run a long-lived web server started by <code>docker run</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0328","card":"q-docker-039-load-balancer-dns-and-lambda","sec":"docker-s040","q":"When do you choose Lambda container images over ECS Fargate?","a":"<p>Lambda suits short, event-driven work with variable or low traffic and no idle cost. Fargate suits long-running services, steady traffic, custom ports and processes that need more than 15 minutes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0329","card":"q-docker-040-databases-and-caches","sec":"docker-s041","q":"How do you connect a container to an RDS instance?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker run -d \\\n  -e MYSQL_HOST=&lt;RDS_ENDPOINT&gt; \\\n  -e MYSQL_USER=admin \\\n  -e MYSQL_PASSWORD=&lt;DB_PASSWORD&gt; \\\n  -e MYSQL_DATABASE=mydb \\\n  mysql:5.7</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0330","card":"q-docker-040-databases-and-caches","sec":"docker-s041","q":"How do you connect a container to a DynamoDB table?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker run -d \\\n    -e AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=&lt;AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID&gt; \\\n    -e AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=&lt;AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY&gt; \\\n    -e AWS_REGION=us-east-1 \\\n    amazon/aws-cli dynamodb list-tables</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0331","card":"q-docker-040-databases-and-caches","sec":"docker-s041","q":"How do you connect a container to a Redis instance?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker run -d \\\n    -e REDIS_HOST=&lt;REDIS_ENDPOINT&gt; \\\n    -e REDIS_PORT=6379 \\\n    redis redis-cli -h &lt;REDIS_ENDPOINT&gt; PING</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0332","card":"q-docker-040-databases-and-caches","sec":"docker-s041","q":"A containerized app on AWS times out (no authentication error) when it connects to an RDS endpoint. What do you check?","a":"<p>A timeout means packets never arrive, so it is a network problem and not a login problem. Check in this order:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>The RDS security group allows the DB port (3306, 5432) from the container host's security group or CIDR (with <code>awsvpc</code> mode on ECS, the task's own security group).</li>\n  <li>The container and the database are in the same VPC, or routing/peering exists. An RDS instance with no public access cannot be reached from outside the VPC.</li>\n  <li>The subnet network ACLs and route tables.</li>\n  <li>DNS resolution of the endpoint from inside the container. A wrong password gives an authentication error instead, not a timeout.</li>\n</ul>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0333","card":"q-docker-040-databases-and-caches","sec":"docker-s041","q":"In Docker on AWS, why should an app use the RDS endpoint DNS name and not an IP address?","a":"<p>The IP behind an RDS endpoint can change, for example after a Multi-AZ failover, a reboot or a replacement. The endpoint name is a DNS record that is updated to point to the new primary. The app must also re-resolve DNS and reconnect after a failover, so connection pools should have a maximum connection lifetime and retry logic.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0334","card":"q-docker-040-databases-and-caches","sec":"docker-s041","q":"Should a production database run in a Docker container or be a managed service such as RDS?","a":"<p>Managed service is the safer default. It gives backups, patching, Multi-AZ failover and monitoring without you building them. A database in a container needs persistent volumes, backup jobs, a plan for host failure and careful upgrades. A container database is good for local development and tests, where it starts fast and can be thrown away. Containers suit stateless workloads best.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0335","card":"q-docker-040-databases-and-caches","sec":"docker-s041","q":"What is <code>amazon/dynamodb-local</code> used for when you work with Docker and DynamoDB?","a":"<p>It is a local DynamoDB-compatible service in a container. You use it for development and tests without an AWS account or cost. The app points to it by overriding the endpoint (e.g. <code>--endpoint-url http://localhost:8000</code> in the AWS CLI, or the SDK endpoint setting). It is for testing only. Behaviour such as throughput limits and some features differ from the real service.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0336","card":"q-docker-040-databases-and-caches","sec":"docker-s041","q":"In AWS, why can a container not reach an ElastiCache Redis endpoint from your laptop?","a":"<p>ElastiCache nodes live inside a VPC and have no public endpoint. Only resources in the same VPC (or connected by peering, VPN or Direct Connect) and allowed by the cache's security group on port 6379 can reach them. A container on EC2, ECS or EKS in that VPC can; a container on a laptop cannot, unless you tunnel through a bastion host or VPN.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0337","card":"q-docker-040-databases-and-caches","sec":"docker-s041","q":"In Docker, how can you quickly test from a container host that a Redis endpoint is reachable, without installing a client on the host?","a":"<p>Run a throwaway client container in the foreground: <code>docker run --rm redis redis-cli -h &lt;endpoint&gt; PING</code>. A reply of <code>PONG</code> proves DNS, routing, the security group and the port all work from that host's network. A timeout points to a network or security group problem, and <code>NOAUTH</code> or an authentication error means the network is fine and only credentials are missing. <code>--rm</code> removes the container afterwards.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0338","card":"q-docker-040-databases-and-caches","sec":"docker-s041","q":"A fleet of containers is scaled out and the RDS database starts refusing connections with \"too many connections\". What is happening and how do you fix it?","a":"<p>Each container instance opens its own connection pool, so total connections grow with the replica count and can exceed the database <code>max_connections</code>. Fix it by lowering the per-container pool size, using a connection proxy such as RDS Proxy or PgBouncer to multiplex connections, or sizing the database for the maximum fleet. Plan the pool size as <code>max_connections</code> divided by the maximum number of replicas.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0339","card":"q-docker-041-s3-sns-and-cloudwatch-logs","sec":"docker-s042","q":"How do you attach a container to an S3 bucket?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker run -d \\\n  -e AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=&lt;AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID&gt; \\\n  -e AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=&lt;AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY&gt; \\\n  -e AWS_REGION=us-east-1 \\\n  amazon/aws-cli s3 ls s3://my-bucket</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0340","card":"q-docker-041-s3-sns-and-cloudwatch-logs","sec":"docker-s042","q":"How do you connect a container to an SNS topic?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker run -d \\\n    -e AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=&lt;AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID&gt; \\\n    -e AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=&lt;AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY&gt; \\\n    -e AWS_REGION=us-east-1 \\\n    amazon/aws-cli sns publish --topic-arn &lt;SNS_TOPIC_ARN&gt; --message &quot;Hello from Docker&quot;</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0341","card":"q-docker-041-s3-sns-and-cloudwatch-logs","sec":"docker-s042","q":"How do you attach a container to a CloudWatch log group?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker run -d \\\n    --log-driver=awslogs \\\n    --log-opt awslogs-region=us-east-1 \\\n    --log-opt awslogs-group=my-log-group \\\n    --log-opt awslogs-create-group=true \\\n    nginx</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0342","card":"q-docker-041-s3-sns-and-cloudwatch-logs","sec":"docker-s042","q":"In Docker, why does <code>amazon/aws-cli s3 ls s3://my-bucket</code> work when you put only the arguments after the image name?","a":"<p>The <code>amazon/aws-cli</code> image defines <code>aws</code> as its <code>ENTRYPOINT</code>. Everything after the image name in <code>docker run</code> becomes arguments to that entrypoint, so <code>s3 ls s3://my-bucket</code> runs as <code>aws s3 ls s3://my-bucket</code>. To run something else in the image, override it with <code>--entrypoint</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0343","card":"q-docker-041-s3-sns-and-cloudwatch-logs","sec":"docker-s042","q":"Why can you not mount an S3 bucket into a Docker container as a normal volume?","a":"<p>S3 is an object store and has no POSIX file system semantics (no in-place edits, no real directories, no file locking). A Docker volume expects a file system. Tools such as Mountpoint for Amazon S3 or s3fs can expose a bucket as a file system, but with limits and extra privileges. The usual approach is to access S3 through the AWS SDK or CLI.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0344","card":"q-docker-041-s3-sns-and-cloudwatch-logs","sec":"docker-s042","q":"A container in a private subnet with no NAT gateway must read from S3. How do you make that work?","a":"<p>Add an S3 gateway VPC endpoint and associate it with the subnet's route table. S3 traffic then stays on the AWS network and needs no internet route or NAT. You can limit it with an endpoint policy and a bucket policy. A gateway endpoint for S3 has no hourly cost.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0345","card":"q-docker-041-s3-sns-and-cloudwatch-logs","sec":"docker-s042","q":"When the Docker <code>awslogs</code> log driver is used, whose AWS credentials send the logs?","a":"<p>The Docker daemon's, not the container's. The daemon sends the log events, so the credentials come from the daemon's environment or the instance role of the host. That role needs <code>logs:CreateLogStream</code> and <code>logs:PutLogEvents</code>, plus <code>logs:CreateLogGroup</code> if <code>awslogs-create-group=true</code> is used. Credentials given to the container with <code>-e</code> do not help.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0346","card":"q-docker-041-s3-sns-and-cloudwatch-logs","sec":"docker-s042","q":"What is the downside of <code>--log-opt awslogs-create-group=true</code> for Docker containers on AWS?","a":"<p>It needs the extra <code>logs:CreateLogGroup</code> permission on every host, and the group is created with no retention, so logs are kept forever and cost grows. It is better to create the log group ahead of time with infrastructure as code, set a retention period, and drop that option.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0347","card":"q-docker-041-s3-sns-and-cloudwatch-logs","sec":"docker-s042","q":"In Docker Engine 20.10 and later, can you still run <code>docker logs</code> for a container that uses the <code>awslogs</code> driver?","a":"<p>Yes. Since Engine 20.10, \"dual logging\" keeps a local cache of the logs for remote drivers, so <code>docker logs</code> works. In earlier versions <code>docker logs</code> only worked for <code>json-file</code>, <code>local</code> and <code>journald</code>, and gave an error for remote drivers.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0348","card":"q-docker-041-s3-sns-and-cloudwatch-logs","sec":"docker-s042","q":"In Docker, what is the difference between blocking and non-blocking mode for a log driver such as <code>awslogs</code>?","a":"<p>In the default blocking mode, the container's writes to stdout wait until the driver has taken the message. If CloudWatch is slow or unreachable, the application can stall. With <code>--log-opt mode=non-blocking --log-opt max-buffer-size=4m</code>, messages go to an in-memory ring buffer and the app keeps running. The cost is that messages can be lost if the buffer fills.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0349","card":"q-docker-041-s3-sns-and-cloudwatch-logs","sec":"docker-s042","q":"Why would an application publish to SNS instead of sending to SQS directly, and when would you use both?","a":"<p>SNS is publish/subscribe. One message fans out to many subscribers (SQS queues, Lambda, HTTP, email). SQS is a queue where each message is processed by one consumer group. Use SNS alone to notify several systems. Use SNS with an SQS queue per consumer when each consumer needs its own durable buffer and retry handling.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0350","card":"q-docker-042-container-fails-to-start","sec":"docker-s043","q":"Container fails to start: \"OCI runtime create failed: no such file or directory: /bin/bash\"?","a":"<p>The image lacks <code>/bin/bash</code> or the entrypoint is wrong.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker inspect &lt;image_name&gt; | grep -i entrypoint\ndocker run -it &lt;image_name&gt; /bin/sh -c &quot;ls /bin/bash&quot;</code></pre></div>\n<ul>\n  <li>Install bash: <code>RUN apt-get update &amp;&amp; apt-get install -y bash</code></li>\n  <li>Or use <code>/bin/sh</code>: <code>docker run -it --entrypoint /bin/sh &lt;image_name&gt;</code></li>\n  <li>Ensure the base image has the required shell. Minimal images (for example alpine) may only have <code>sh</code>.</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0351","card":"q-docker-042-container-fails-to-start","sec":"docker-s043","q":"Container stuck in \"Created\" state?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker ps -a\ndocker inspect &lt;container_id&gt; | jq &#x27;.State.Error&#x27;\njournalctl -u docker.service\ndocker start &lt;container_id&gt;</code></pre></div>\n<ul>\n  <li>Recreate if needed: <code>docker rm &lt;container_id&gt; &amp;&amp; docker run &lt;image_name&gt;</code></li>\n  <li>Verify the Docker daemon (<code>systemctl status docker</code>), resources and architecture compatibility.</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0352","card":"q-docker-042-container-fails-to-start","sec":"docker-s043","q":"In Docker, a container fails with <code>no such file or directory</code> for a shell script entrypoint, although the script exists in the image. What could be wrong?","a":"<p>The error often names the interpreter, not the script. Two common causes:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>The script starts with <code>#!/bin/bash</code> but the image has no bash (e.g. Alpine has only <code>sh</code>).</li>\n  <li>The script was saved with Windows (CRLF) line endings, so the interpreter is looked up as <code>/bin/bash\\r</code>. Check the first line with <code>head -1 script.sh | cat -A</code>, use <code>#!/bin/sh</code> or install bash, and convert line endings with <code>dos2unix</code> or a <code>.gitattributes</code> rule.</li>\n</ul>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0353","card":"q-docker-042-container-fails-to-start","sec":"docker-s043","q":"In Docker, what is the difference between the states <code>Created</code> and <code>Exited</code>?","a":"<p><code>Created</code> means Docker prepared the container (file system, network, config) but the main process never started, either because you used <code>docker create</code> and did not start it, or because start failed (a missing bind mount source, a port conflict, a runtime error). <code>Exited</code> means the process did run and then ended. The exit code and <code>docker logs</code> tell you why.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0354","card":"q-docker-042-container-fails-to-start","sec":"docker-s043","q":"In Docker, an image's entrypoint crashes at once so you cannot <code>docker exec</code> into it. How do you inspect the image?","a":"<p>Start a new container from the image with the entrypoint replaced, for example <code>docker run --rm -it --entrypoint sh &lt;image&gt;</code>. That gives you a shell in the same file system to check files, permissions and the binary. If the image has no shell, copy files out with <code>docker create</code> plus <code>docker cp</code>, or save the file system with <code>docker export</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0355","card":"q-docker-043-memory-and-exit-codes","sec":"docker-s044","q":"Container exits with code 137?","a":"<p>It is an out-of-memory (OOM) kill.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker logs &lt;container_id&gt;\ndocker inspect &lt;container_id&gt; | grep -i memory\ndocker run --memory=&quot;512m&quot; &lt;image_name&gt;</code></pre></div>\n<p>Check host memory (<code>free -m</code>), application leaks, or adjust <code>--memory-swap</code>.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0356","card":"q-docker-043-memory-and-exit-codes","sec":"docker-s044","q":"Container fails: \"cannot allocate memory\"?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>free -m\ndocker inspect &lt;container_id&gt; | grep -i memory\ndocker run --memory=&quot;1g&quot; &lt;image_name&gt;\ndocker system prune</code></pre></div>\n<p>Check for memory leaks, make sure the host has memory, and adjust swap settings.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0357","card":"q-docker-043-memory-and-exit-codes","sec":"docker-s044","q":"How do you tell whether a container was killed by its own memory limit or by the host running out of memory?","a":"<p>Check <code>docker inspect -f '{{.State.OOMKilled}}' &lt;container&gt;</code>; it is <code>true</code> when the kernel killed the container's process for exceeding its cgroup limit. Also read <code>dmesg</code> or <code>journalctl -k</code>: \"Memory cgroup out of memory\" lines mean the container limit, while \"Out of memory: Killed process\" for the global case means the host itself had no memory. The fix differs: raise or tune the limit, or add host memory and set limits on all containers.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0358","card":"q-docker-043-memory-and-exit-codes","sec":"docker-s044","q":"A Java service in a Docker container with <code>--memory=512m</code> keeps getting OOM-killed although the heap looks small. What is going on?","a":"<p>The container limit covers the whole process, not only the heap: metaspace, thread stacks, direct buffers and native memory also count. If the JVM heap is set near the limit, the total goes over and the kernel kills it. Use a container-aware JVM (Java 10+, and 8u191+, have <code>UseContainerSupport</code>), set <code>-XX:MaxRAMPercentage</code> to about 50-75 percent, and leave headroom. Node.js has the same issue; keep <code>--max-old-space-size</code> lower than the limit.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0359","card":"q-docker-043-memory-and-exit-codes","sec":"docker-s044","q":"In Docker, why does <code>free -m</code> inside a container show the host's memory and not the container's limit?","a":"<p><code>free</code> and <code>top</code> read <code>/proc/meminfo</code> and <code>/proc/stat</code>, which are not namespaced, so they report host totals. The real limit is in the cgroup files (e.g. <code>/sys/fs/cgroup/memory.max</code> on cgroup v2) and in <code>docker stats</code>. This also explains why old runtimes that read <code>/proc</code> sized themselves to the host and then got OOM-killed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0360","card":"q-docker-043-memory-and-exit-codes","sec":"docker-s044","q":"In Docker, will <code>docker system prune</code> fix a container that fails with \"cannot allocate memory\"?","a":"<p>No. Pruning removes stopped containers, unused networks, dangling images and build cache, which frees disk space and not RAM. Stopped containers hold no memory. To fix memory pressure, find what is using it (<code>docker stats</code>, <code>free -m</code>), fix leaks, set sensible <code>--memory</code> limits, add swap or RAM, or reduce the number of containers on the host.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0361","card":"q-docker-043-memory-and-exit-codes","sec":"docker-s044","q":"In Docker, what is the difference between <code>--memory</code> and <code>--memory-reservation</code>?","a":"<p><code>--memory</code> is a hard limit: going over it triggers the OOM killer. <code>--memory-reservation</code> is a soft limit that applies only when the host is short of memory; Docker then tries to push the container back toward that value. It must be lower than <code>--memory</code>. Use a reservation for scheduling fairness and a hard limit for safety.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0362","card":"q-docker-043-memory-and-exit-codes","sec":"docker-s044","q":"How do you choose a memory limit for a container in Docker?","a":"<p>Measure first: watch <code>docker stats</code> under realistic and peak load, then set the limit at the observed peak plus headroom (often 20-30 percent). Too low causes OOM kills; too high or unlimited lets one container starve the others on the host. Revisit after releases, and alert on usage near the limit.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0363","card":"q-docker-044-unresponsive-or-unreachable-containers","sec":"docker-s045","q":"Application in the container is not responding?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker exec -it &lt;container_id&gt; /bin/sh\nps aux\ndocker logs &lt;container_id&gt;\ncurl http://localhost:&lt;port&gt;\ndocker stats &lt;container_id&gt;</code></pre></div>\n<p>Check application logs and port binding. Avoid stopping the container while debugging.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0364","card":"q-docker-044-unresponsive-or-unreachable-containers","sec":"docker-s045","q":"Container not accessible, possibly not running?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker ps -a | grep &lt;container_name&gt;\ndocker start &lt;container_id&gt;\ndocker logs &lt;container_id&gt;</code></pre></div>\n<ul>\n  <li>Recreate: <code>docker rm &lt;container_id&gt; &amp;&amp; docker run &lt;image_name&gt;</code></li>\n  <li>Verify configuration, the Docker daemon and host resources (<code>df -h</code>, <code>free -m</code>).</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0365","card":"q-docker-044-unresponsive-or-unreachable-containers","sec":"docker-s045","q":"Container uses excessive CPU?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker stats &lt;container_id&gt;\ndocker exec &lt;container_id&gt; top\ndocker update --cpus=&quot;0.5&quot; &lt;container_id&gt;</code></pre></div>\n<p>Set CPU limits (<code>--cpus</code>), check for application loops, and monitor host CPU (<code>htop</code>).</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0366","card":"q-docker-044-unresponsive-or-unreachable-containers","sec":"docker-s045","q":"In Docker, how do you preserve evidence from a misbehaving container before you restart it?","a":"<p>Restarting destroys in-memory state, so capture first:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><code>docker logs</code> and <code>docker inspect</code> output saved to files.</li>\n  <li><code>docker stats</code>, <code>docker top</code>, and thread or heap dumps from inside the app.</li>\n  <li><code>docker cp</code> for files, or <code>docker commit</code> / <code>docker export</code> to save the file system. Then restart or recreate. If you must stop activity but keep the process, use <code>docker pause</code>.</li>\n</ul>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0367","card":"q-docker-044-unresponsive-or-unreachable-containers","sec":"docker-s045","q":"In Docker, what does <code>docker pause</code> do and how does it differ from <code>docker stop</code>?","a":"<p><code>pause</code> freezes all processes of the container using the cgroup freezer. They keep their memory and state and are not signalled, and the container can be resumed with <code>docker unpause</code>. <code>stop</code> sends SIGTERM (then SIGKILL after a timeout) and ends the processes. Pause is useful to stop a runaway container while you investigate.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0368","card":"q-docker-044-unresponsive-or-unreachable-containers","sec":"docker-s045","q":"In Docker, <code>docker stats</code> shows 250% CPU for a container. What does that mean?","a":"<p>The percentage is relative to one core, so 250% means about 2.5 cores are in use. It can exceed 100% on a multi-core host unless the container has a <code>--cpus</code> limit, in which case it is capped near that limit.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0369","card":"q-docker-044-unresponsive-or-unreachable-containers","sec":"docker-s045","q":"In Docker, why does <code>top</code> inside a container show total CPU figures for the host and not only for the container?","a":"<p>The summary lines come from <code>/proc/stat</code>, which is not namespaced, so they show the host. The process list is limited to the container's PID namespace. For per-container usage, use <code>docker stats</code> or the cgroup files on the host.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0370","card":"q-docker-045-recreating-scaling-and-updating","sec":"docker-s046","q":"How do you recreate an accidentally deleted container?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker inspect &lt;image_name&gt; | grep -i cmd\ndocker run -d --name &lt;container_name&gt; -p &lt;host_port&gt;:&lt;container_port&gt; -v &lt;volume_path&gt; &lt;image_name&gt;\ndocker-compose up -d</code></pre></div>\n<p>Store configuration in Compose files or scripts, verify volumes, and check environment variables.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0371","card":"q-docker-045-recreating-scaling-and-updating","sec":"docker-s046","q":"How do you scale a Dockerized application?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker-compose up -d --scale &lt;service_name&gt;=3\ndocker swarm init\ndocker service create --replicas 3 --name &lt;service_name&gt; &lt;image_name&gt;\ndocker run -d -p 80:80 nginx</code></pre></div>\n<p>NGINX config:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Nginx</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>upstream app {\n    server container1:8080;\n    server container2:8080;\n    server container3:8080;\n}\nserver { listen 80; location / { proxy_pass http://app; } }</code></pre></div>\n<p>The app must be stateless. Monitor resources (<code>docker stats</code>) and use an external load balancer in production.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0372","card":"q-docker-045-recreating-scaling-and-updating","sec":"docker-s046","q":"How do you update a container to the latest application version?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker pull &lt;image_name&gt;:latest\ndocker-compose up -d\ndocker service update --image &lt;image_name&gt;:latest &lt;service_name&gt;\ndocker exec &lt;container_id&gt; &lt;app_version_command&gt;</code></pre></div>\n<p>Test in staging, ensure data compatibility, and use health checks.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0373","card":"q-docker-045-recreating-scaling-and-updating","sec":"docker-s046","q":"In Docker, why is it safe to delete and recreate a container, and what must be true for that to work?","a":"<p>A container is meant to be disposable. Recreating works without data loss when its state is outside the container: data in volumes or external services, configuration in Compose files or environment variables, and the image in a registry. Keep run options in a Compose file or script in version control, not in someone's shell history, so a lost container can be rebuilt the same way.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0374","card":"q-docker-045-recreating-scaling-and-updating","sec":"docker-s046","q":"Why must an application be stateless before you scale it to several containers?","a":"<p>Requests are spread over replicas, so a request can land on a different container each time. Anything stored in one container's memory or local disk (sessions, uploads, caches that must be consistent) is missing on the others and lost when a container is replaced. Move state to a shared store such as a database, Redis or object storage, or use sticky sessions as a weaker workaround.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0375","card":"q-docker-045-recreating-scaling-and-updating","sec":"docker-s046","q":"In Docker Compose, when you scale a service to three replicas, how does another service reach them?","a":"<p>The service name resolves through Docker's embedded DNS to the IP addresses of all replicas, and the client usually gets them in rotating order. This is DNS round robin, not a real load balancer: it has no health checks, and clients that cache the first address will not spread load. For real balancing, put a reverse proxy or load balancer in front.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0376","card":"q-docker-045-recreating-scaling-and-updating","sec":"docker-s046","q":"In Docker, after <code>docker pull myapp:latest</code>, why is the running container still on the old version?","a":"<p>A container is bound to the image ID it was created from. Pulling only updates the local tag. You must recreate the container (<code>docker compose up -d</code>, <code>docker service update</code>, or stop, remove and run again) to use the new image. For rollback, redeploy a previous immutable tag or digest.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0377","card":"q-docker-046-connectivity-failures","sec":"docker-s047","q":"Container cannot connect to an external database?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker inspect &lt;container_id&gt; | grep -i network\ndocker exec &lt;container_id&gt; ping &lt;db_host&gt;\ndocker exec &lt;container_id&gt; nslookup &lt;db_host&gt;\nsudo iptables -L -n\nsudo iptables -A INPUT -p tcp --dport &lt;db_port&gt; -j ACCEPT</code></pre></div>\n<p>Verify the database host and port, the network mode, and firewall or security groups.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0378","card":"q-docker-046-connectivity-failures","sec":"docker-s047","q":"Containers in the same network cannot communicate?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker network ls\ndocker inspect &lt;container_id&gt; | grep -i network\ndocker exec &lt;container1_id&gt; ping &lt;container2_name&gt;\ndocker network create mynet\ndocker network connect mynet &lt;container_id&gt;</code></pre></div>\n<p>Docker Compose:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>version: &#x27;3&#x27;\nservices:\n  app1: { image: &lt;image1&gt;, networks: [mynet] }\n  app2: { image: &lt;image2&gt;, networks: [mynet] }\nnetworks: { mynet: { driver: bridge } }</code></pre></div>\n<p>Use container names for DNS, check network policies, and restart after changes.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0379","card":"q-docker-046-connectivity-failures","sec":"docker-s047","q":"Container blocked from an external API by a firewall?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>sudo iptables -L -n -v\ndocker exec &lt;container_id&gt; curl &lt;api_url&gt;\nsudo iptables -A OUTPUT -p tcp --dport &lt;api_port&gt; -j ACCEPT\nip route</code></pre></div>\n<p>Verify firewall rules and cloud security groups, then test connectivity.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0380","card":"q-docker-046-connectivity-failures","sec":"docker-s047","q":"A Docker container cannot reach the internet, but the host can. What do you check?","a":"<p>In order:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>DNS inside the container (<code>nslookup</code>, <code>/etc/resolv.conf</code>), which separates DNS failures from routing failures.</li>\n  <li>IP forwarding on the host (<code>sysctl net.ipv4.ip_forward</code> must be 1).</li>\n  <li>The iptables <code>FORWARD</code> policy and the <code>MASQUERADE</code> rule for the Docker subnet (a firewall tool such as firewalld can break Docker's rules).</li>\n  <li>Overlapping subnets between the Docker bridge and your corporate or VPN network.</li>\n  <li>Proxy settings if the host uses a proxy.</li>\n</ul>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0381","card":"q-docker-046-connectivity-failures","sec":"docker-s047","q":"In a Docker container, <code>ping</code> to a host works but the application still cannot connect. Why?","a":"<p>Ping uses ICMP and only shows basic reachability. The application uses TCP or UDP on a specific port, which a firewall or security group may block, or nothing may be listening. Test the actual port with <code>nc -zv host port</code> or <code>curl -v</code>. Many minimal images also lack <code>ping</code>, so a missing tool does not mean a network fault.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0382","card":"q-docker-046-connectivity-failures","sec":"docker-s047","q":"A Docker image has no <code>ping</code>, <code>curl</code> or <code>nslookup</code>. How do you troubleshoot its network without changing the image?","a":"<p>Start a debug container that shares the target's network namespace and has the tools, for example <code>docker run --rm -it --network container:&lt;name&gt; nicolaka/netshoot</code>. It sees the same interfaces, IP and DNS as the target. Add <code>--pid container:&lt;name&gt;</code> to also see its processes. This keeps the production image small and unchanged.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0383","card":"q-docker-046-connectivity-failures","sec":"docker-s047","q":"In Docker, why does adding <code>iptables -A INPUT -p tcp --dport 8080 -j ACCEPT</code> often not fix a blocked container port?","a":"<p>The <code>INPUT</code> chain filters traffic addressed to the host itself. Traffic to a published container port is translated by NAT and then routed to the container, so it passes through the <code>FORWARD</code> chain (and Docker's own chains). Rules for container traffic belong in <code>FORWARD</code> or Docker's <code>DOCKER-USER</code> chain. Also check cloud security groups, which are separate from host iptables.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0384","card":"q-docker-046-connectivity-failures","sec":"docker-s047","q":"In Docker, two Compose projects need to talk to each other. How do you connect them?","a":"<p>Each project gets its own default network, so containers cannot see each other by default. Create a shared network (<code>docker network create shared</code>), and attach services of both projects to it, declared <code>external: true</code> in the Compose files. Then they resolve each other by service name or alias. Join only the services that need it, to keep isolation.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0385","card":"q-docker-047-ports-and-dns","sec":"docker-s048","q":"Containerized app not accessible via the mapped port?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker inspect &lt;container_id&gt; | grep -i port\ndocker exec &lt;container_id&gt; netstat -tuln\ncurl http://localhost:&lt;host_port&gt;\nsudo iptables -L -n\nsudo iptables -A INPUT -p tcp --dport &lt;host_port&gt; -j ACCEPT</code></pre></div>\n<p>Verify the port mapping, that the app binds to <code>0.0.0.0</code>, and that no host service conflicts.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0386","card":"q-docker-047-ports-and-dns","sec":"docker-s048","q":"Port conflict: \"Bind for 0.0.0.0:80 failed\"?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>sudo netstat -tulnp | grep :80\nsudo kill &lt;pid&gt;\ndocker run -p 8080:80 &lt;image_name&gt;</code></pre></div>\n<p>Check for conflicting containers (<code>docker ps</code>) and avoid duplicate port usage.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0387","card":"q-docker-047-ports-and-dns","sec":"docker-s048","q":"Container cannot resolve DNS names?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker exec &lt;container_id&gt; nslookup google.com\ndocker inspect &lt;container_id&gt; | grep -i dns\ndocker run --dns 8.8.8.8 &lt;image_name&gt;\ncat /etc/resolv.conf</code></pre></div>\n<p>Verify host DNS, check network policies (port 53), and restart after DNS changes.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0388","card":"q-docker-047-ports-and-dns","sec":"docker-s048","q":"In Docker, what is the difference between <code>EXPOSE</code> in a Dockerfile and <code>-p</code> in <code>docker run</code>?","a":"<p><code>EXPOSE</code> only documents which port the app listens on; it does not open anything. <code>-p host:container</code> actually publishes the port on the host. <code>docker run -P</code> publishes the <code>EXPOSE</code>d ports to random host ports. Containers on the same user-defined network can reach each other's ports without either.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0389","card":"q-docker-047-ports-and-dns","sec":"docker-s048","q":"Where does a Docker container get its DNS settings, and what happens if the host uses <code>127.0.0.53</code> (systemd-resolved)?","a":"<p>On the default bridge a container gets a copy of the host's <code>/etc/resolv.conf</code>. A loopback nameserver such as <code>127.0.0.53</code> would not work inside the container, so Docker skips it and, if none remains, falls back to public DNS servers (8.8.8.8 and 8.8.4.4). That fallback breaks names that only your internal DNS knows. Set <code>--dns</code> per container, or <code>\"dns\": [...]</code> in <code>daemon.json</code>, to use your resolver. User-defined networks use the embedded DNS at 127.0.0.11, which forwards to the host's configured servers.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0390","card":"q-docker-047-ports-and-dns","sec":"docker-s048","q":"How do you set DNS servers and search domains for all containers on a Docker host?","a":"<p>Put <code>\"dns\": [\"10.0.0.2\"]</code> and <code>\"dns-search\": [\"corp.example.com\"]</code> in <code>/etc/docker/daemon.json</code>, then restart the daemon. Containers created afterwards use them. Per container, use <code>--dns</code> and <code>--dns-search</code>. Existing containers keep their old settings until recreated.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0391","card":"q-docker-047-ports-and-dns","sec":"docker-s048","q":"Why must a firewall allow both UDP and TCP port 53 for container DNS to work reliably?","a":"<p>DNS normally uses UDP, but it switches to TCP when answers are large (truncated responses) and for zone transfers. If TCP 53 is blocked, some lookups fail only for big responses, which looks like random DNS failures. Allow both, from the container networks to the resolvers.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0392","card":"q-docker-047-ports-and-dns","sec":"docker-s048","q":"In Docker, how do you see which host ports a container has published?","a":"<p>Run <code>docker port &lt;container&gt;</code>, or read <code>docker ps</code> in the PORTS column, or <code>docker inspect -f '{{json .NetworkSettings.Ports}}' &lt;container&gt;</code>. To find which container holds a host port, use <code>docker ps --filter publish=80</code>; for a host process outside Docker, use <code>ss -ltnp</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0393","card":"q-docker-048-packet-loss-502-errors-and-slowness","sec":"docker-s049","q":"Container dropping packets intermittently?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker inspect &lt;container_id&gt; | grep -i network\ndocker exec &lt;container_id&gt; tcpdump -i eth0\nsudo tcpdump -i docker0\ndocker stats &lt;container_id&gt;</code></pre></div>\n<p>Check for congestion, MTU settings or network driver issues (<code>ifconfig</code>).</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0394","card":"q-docker-048-packet-loss-502-errors-and-slowness","sec":"docker-s049","q":"Containerized web server returns 502 Bad Gateway?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker logs &lt;container_id&gt;\ndocker exec &lt;web_container_id&gt; curl http://&lt;backend_service&gt;:&lt;port&gt;\ndocker inspect &lt;container_id&gt; | grep -i network</code></pre></div>\n<p>NGINX config:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Nginx</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>server { listen 80; location / { proxy_pass http://&lt;backend_service&gt;:&lt;port&gt;; } }</code></pre></div>\n<p>Verify backend health, network configuration and proxy settings.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0395","card":"q-docker-048-packet-loss-502-errors-and-slowness","sec":"docker-s049","q":"Slow container network performance?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker stats &lt;container_id&gt;\ndocker run -it --rm &lt;image_name&gt; iperf -c &lt;target_ip&gt;\nsudo iftop -i docker0\ndocker network inspect &lt;network_name&gt; | grep -i mtu</code></pre></div>\n<p>Compare with other containers, check congestion or QoS, and verify the network driver.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0396","card":"q-docker-048-packet-loss-502-errors-and-slowness","sec":"docker-s049","q":"In Docker, small requests work but large downloads between containers hang or stall. What is a likely cause?","a":"<p>An MTU mismatch. If the container network MTU is larger than the path allows (e.g. overlay networks add VXLAN overhead of about 50 bytes, VPNs and some cloud networks have smaller MTUs) and fragmentation is blocked, big packets are dropped while small ones pass. Check with <code>ping -M do -s &lt;size&gt;</code> and set the network MTU with <code>--opt com.docker.network.driver.mtu=1450</code> (or the matching <code>daemon.json</code> setting).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0397","card":"q-docker-048-packet-loss-502-errors-and-slowness","sec":"docker-s049","q":"What do HTTP 502, 503 and 504 usually mean when they come from a reverse proxy in front of containers?","a":"<p>502 Bad Gateway: the proxy got no valid response from the backend (connection refused, reset, backend crashed). 503 Service Unavailable: no healthy backend is available or the service is overloaded. 504 Gateway Timeout: the backend did not answer within the proxy's timeout. Use the code to decide whether to check if the backend is up, healthy, or too slow.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0398","card":"q-docker-048-packet-loss-502-errors-and-slowness","sec":"docker-s049","q":"A Nginx container proxies to <code>http://backend:8080</code>. After the backend container is recreated, Nginx returns 502 until it is restarted. Why?","a":"<p>Nginx resolves hostnames in <code>proxy_pass</code> once at start-up and keeps the IP. A recreated container gets a new IP, so Nginx keeps sending to the old address. Fix it with <code>resolver 127.0.0.11 valid=10s;</code> and a variable in <code>proxy_pass</code> (e.g. <code>set $upstream http://backend:8080; proxy_pass $upstream;</code>), which makes Nginx resolve at request time. Or reload Nginx after a deploy.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0399","card":"q-docker-048-packet-loss-502-errors-and-slowness","sec":"docker-s049","q":"In Docker, how do you capture packets of a container that has no <code>tcpdump</code> installed?","a":"<p>Run <code>tcpdump</code> from the host inside the container's network namespace: <code>nsenter -t $(docker inspect -f '{{.State.Pid}}' &lt;container&gt;) -n tcpdump -i eth0</code>. A debug container sharing the namespace (<code>--network container:&lt;name&gt;</code>) also works. Nothing is installed in the target.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0400","card":"q-docker-048-packet-loss-502-errors-and-slowness","sec":"docker-s049","q":"In Docker, intermittent connection drops and \"nf_conntrack: table full, dropping packet\" appear in the host log. What is happening?","a":"<p>The kernel's connection tracking table is full. NAT for container traffic needs a tracking entry per connection, and a busy host with many short connections can fill the table. New connections are dropped. Raise <code>net.netfilter.nf_conntrack_max</code>, shorten timeouts, reuse connections (keep-alive), or reduce connection churn.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0401","card":"q-docker-048-packet-loss-502-errors-and-slowness","sec":"docker-s049","q":"A service in Docker is slow. How do you find out whether the network, the app or the database is responsible?","a":"<p>Narrow it by layers. Measure from the client with <code>curl -w</code> timing (DNS, connect, first byte). Run <code>iperf</code> between two containers on the same host, then between hosts, to separate bridge and physical network issues. Test the database from the app container directly. Compare latency container-to-container with <code>--network host</code> to rule out NAT overhead. Check <code>docker stats</code> and host CPU for saturation.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0402","card":"q-docker-048-packet-loss-502-errors-and-slowness","sec":"docker-s049","q":"What does the <code>userland-proxy</code> setting in Docker's <code>daemon.json</code> control?","a":"<p>With it on (the default), Docker runs a <code>docker-proxy</code> process per published port to handle some cases, such as a host process connecting to a published port through <code>localhost</code>. Setting <code>\"userland-proxy\": false</code> relies on iptables only, which uses fewer processes and is a little faster. Some hairpin cases behave differently, so test before changing it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0403","card":"q-docker-049-restricting-traffic-between-containers","sec":"docker-s050","q":"How do you restrict network traffic between containers?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker network create --driver bridge --opt com.docker.network.bridge.enable_icc=false mynet\ndocker network connect mynet &lt;container_id&gt;\nsudo iptables -A FORWARD -s &lt;container1_ip&gt; -d &lt;container2_ip&gt; -p tcp --dport &lt;port&gt; -j ACCEPT</code></pre></div>\n<p>Test connectivity, use Compose networks, and avoid blocking critical traffic.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0404","card":"q-docker-049-restricting-traffic-between-containers","sec":"docker-s050","q":"In Docker, how do you encrypt traffic between containers on different Swarm nodes?","a":"<p>Create the overlay network with encryption: <code>docker network create -d overlay --opt encrypted mynet</code>. Docker then encrypts the VXLAN data plane with IPsec between nodes. It costs some CPU and throughput, and IP protocol 50 (ESP) must be allowed between nodes in addition to the Swarm ports. Control-plane traffic between managers is always encrypted.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0405","card":"q-docker-050-permissions-and-missing-paths","sec":"docker-s051","q":"Container cannot write to a mounted volume?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>ls -ld /path/to/volume\nchmod -R 775 /path/to/volume\nchown -R user:group /path/to/volume\ndocker inspect &lt;container_id&gt; | jq &#x27;.Mounts&#x27;\nsetenforce 0\ndocker restart &lt;container_id&gt;</code></pre></div>\n<p>Align host and container permissions, and check SELinux/AppArmor.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0406","card":"q-docker-050-permissions-and-missing-paths","sec":"docker-s051","q":"Bind mount changes not reflected on the host?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker inspect &lt;container_id&gt; | jq &#x27;.Mounts&#x27;\nls -ld /path/to/host/mount\nchmod -R 775 /path/to/host/mount\ndocker exec &lt;container_id&gt; id\ndocker run -v /path/to/host/mount:/app:rw &lt;image_name&gt;\nchcon -R -t container_file_t /path/to/host/mount</code></pre></div>\n<p>Confirm the <code>rw</code> flag, align permissions, and check SELinux.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0407","card":"q-docker-050-permissions-and-missing-paths","sec":"docker-s051","q":"Volume error: \"no such file or directory\"?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker volume ls\nls -ld /path/to/host/mount\nmkdir -p /path/to/host/mount\ndocker run -v /path/to/host/mount:/app &lt;image_name&gt;\ncat Dockerfile | grep VOLUME</code></pre></div>\n<p>Ensure the volume or path exists, check path spelling, and verify the Docker commands.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0408","card":"q-docker-050-permissions-and-missing-paths","sec":"docker-s051","q":"A container gets \"permission denied\" on a bind mount. Why is <code>chmod 777</code> a bad fix and what is better?","a":"<p>It gives every user on the host write access to the data, which is a security problem, and it hides the real mismatch: the container's UID/GID differs from the owner of the host directory. Better: run the container as the owning user (<code>--user $(id -u):$(id -g)</code>), <code>chown</code> the directory to the container's UID, or use a named volume, which Docker initialises with the image's ownership.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0409","card":"q-docker-050-permissions-and-missing-paths","sec":"docker-s051","q":"In Docker on SELinux hosts (e.g. RHEL, Fedora), what do the <code>:z</code> and <code>:Z</code> suffixes on a bind mount do?","a":"<p>They relabel the host path so the container may access it. <code>:z</code> gives a shared label, usable by several containers. <code>:Z</code> gives a private label for this container only. Use them instead of <code>setenforce 0</code>, which turns SELinux protection off for the whole host. Do not use <code>:Z</code> on shared system directories such as <code>/home</code> or <code>/etc</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0410","card":"q-docker-050-permissions-and-missing-paths","sec":"docker-s051","q":"In Docker, what is the difference between <code>-v data:/app</code> and <code>-v $(pwd)/data:/app</code> in <code>docker run</code>?","a":"<p><code>-v data:/app</code> uses a named volume: Docker creates and manages it under its own storage area, and initialises it from the image's content at <code>/app</code>. <code>-v $(pwd)/data:/app</code> is a bind mount of that host directory, so the container sees and changes the host files directly. Forgetting the path form is a common mistake: with a bare name the data ends up in a Docker volume and not in the folder you expected. Prefer the absolute form (<code>$(pwd)/data</code>) for bind mounts in <code>docker run</code>. A relative <code>./data</code> is accepted by Docker Compose, and some newer Docker Engine versions accept it too, so check your version before relying on it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0411","card":"q-docker-051-disk-usage","sec":"docker-s052","q":"Volume filling up the host disk?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>du -sh /var/lib/docker/volumes/*\ndocker stop &lt;container_id&gt;\ndocker run --rm -v &lt;volume_name&gt;:/data busybox sh -c &quot;rm -rf /data/unneeded_files&quot;\ndocker volume prune</code></pre></div>\n<p>Stop containers before cleanup, verify files are safe to delete, and use pruning cautiously.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0412","card":"q-docker-051-disk-usage","sec":"docker-s052","q":"Unused volumes consuming disk space?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker volume ls\ndocker volume ls -f dangling=true\ndocker volume prune\ndf -h</code></pre></div>\n<p>Verify volume dependencies and avoid accidental data loss.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0413","card":"q-docker-051-disk-usage","sec":"docker-s052","q":"In Docker, how do you find out what is using disk space?","a":"<p>Run <code>docker system df</code> for totals of images, containers, local volumes and build cache, and <code>docker system df -v</code> for each item. Then check <code>/var/lib/docker</code> with <code>du -sh /var/lib/docker/*</code>. Typical culprits are unrotated <code>json-file</code> logs under <code>containers/</code>, many old images, large writable layers, anonymous volumes and build cache.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0414","card":"q-docker-051-disk-usage","sec":"docker-s052","q":"On a Linux host, you deleted a huge log file but <code>df</code> still shows the disk full. Why?","a":"<p>A process still has the deleted file open, so the file system keeps the blocks until the process closes it or exits. Find these with <code>lsof +L1</code> (open files with link count 0). Restart or signal the process to reopen its log (or truncate with <code>: &gt; file</code> instead of <code>rm</code>). Containers with large logs show this often.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0415","card":"q-docker-051-disk-usage","sec":"docker-s052","q":"In Docker, what does <code>docker system prune -a --volumes</code> remove, and when is it risky?","a":"<p>It removes stopped containers, unused networks, all images not used by a container, build cache and unused volumes. It is risky on a host with data volumes: a volume of a stopped database container counts as unused and can be deleted, and removing images forces re-downloads. Run it without <code>--volumes</code> and review <code>docker system df</code> first.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0416","card":"q-docker-051-disk-usage","sec":"docker-s052","q":"How do you move Docker's data to a bigger disk on a Linux host?","a":"<p>Stop Docker, copy <code>/var/lib/docker</code> to the new mount with <code>rsync -aH</code> (keeps permissions and hard links), set <code>\"data-root\": \"/mnt/docker\"</code> in <code>/etc/docker/daemon.json</code>, and start Docker. Check containers and volumes, then remove the old directory. Putting <code>/var/lib/docker</code> on its own partition from the start stops Docker from filling the root disk.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0417","card":"q-docker-052-recovery-migration-and-expansion","sec":"docker-s053","q":"How do you recover a corrupted volume?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker volume inspect &lt;volume_name&gt;\ndocker run --rm -v &lt;volume_name&gt;:/data -it busybox sh\nfsck /path/to/volume\ntar -xzf /backup/volume_backup.tar.gz -C /var/lib/docker/volumes/&lt;volume_name&gt;/_data\ndocker volume create new_volume\ndocker run -v new_volume:/app &lt;image_name&gt;</code></pre></div>\n<p>Maintain backups, test restored data, and use recovery containers.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0418","card":"q-docker-052-recovery-migration-and-expansion","sec":"docker-s053","q":"How do you migrate a volume to a new host?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker stop &lt;container_id&gt;\ntar -czf volume_backup.tar.gz -C /var/lib/docker/volumes/&lt;volume_name&gt;/_data .\nscp volume_backup.tar.gz user@new_host:/tmp\ndocker volume create &lt;volume_name&gt;\ntar -xzf /tmp/volume_backup.tar.gz -C /var/lib/docker/volumes/&lt;volume_name&gt;/_data\ndocker run -v &lt;volume_name&gt;:/app &lt;image_name&gt;</code></pre></div>\n<p>Ensure Docker version compatibility, verify permissions, and test after migration.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0419","card":"q-docker-052-recovery-migration-and-expansion","sec":"docker-s053","q":"How do you expand database container storage?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker exec &lt;container_id&gt; df -h\ndocker volume create new_volume\ndocker run --rm -v &lt;old_volume&gt;:/old -v new_volume:/new busybox sh -c &quot;cp -a /old/. /new/&quot;\ndocker stop &lt;container_id&gt;\ndocker run -v new_volume:/data &lt;image_name&gt;</code></pre></div>\n<p>Minimize downtime, verify data integrity, and ensure host disk space.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0420","card":"q-docker-052-recovery-migration-and-expansion","sec":"docker-s053","q":"Why does <code>fsck</code> not help to repair a Docker volume directory?","a":"<p><code>fsck</code> checks file systems on block devices (a partition or disk, normally unmounted), not directories. A volume directory is only a folder on the host file system, so you run <code>fsck</code> on the underlying device, with Docker stopped and the file system unmounted. For corrupted application data inside a volume, use the application's own recovery tools or restore a backup.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0421","card":"q-docker-052-recovery-migration-and-expansion","sec":"docker-s053","q":"When you copy data between Docker volumes with a helper container, why use <code>cp -a</code> and not <code>cp -r</code>?","a":"<p><code>-a</code> is archive mode: it keeps ownership, permissions, timestamps, symlinks and special files, and copies recursively. <code>cp -r</code> loses ownership and times (the copy becomes owned by the user running <code>cp</code>), so a database or app running as another user may fail to start on the copied data.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0422","card":"q-docker-052-recovery-migration-and-expansion","sec":"docker-s053","q":"A database container on an EC2 host with an EBS volume has run out of space. What are the steps to grow it?","a":"<p>Increase the EBS volume size in AWS (it can be done online), then grow the partition if needed (<code>growpart</code>) and the file system (<code>resize2fs</code> for ext4, <code>xfs_growfs</code> for XFS). The Docker volume then sees the space with no data copy. Take a snapshot first. If Docker data is on the root disk, the same applies to the root volume.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0423","card":"q-docker-053-nfs-and-persistence","sec":"docker-s054","q":"Container cannot access an NFS volume?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>ping &lt;nfs_server_ip&gt;\nmount | grep nfs\napt-get install -y nfs-common\nmount -t nfs &lt;nfs_server_ip&gt;:/share /mnt\ndocker inspect &lt;container_id&gt; | jq &#x27;.Mounts&#x27;\ndocker run -v &lt;nfs_server_ip&gt;:/share:/mnt &lt;image_name&gt;</code></pre></div>\n<p>Verify the NFS server, firewall and client tools.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0424","card":"q-docker-053-nfs-and-persistence","sec":"docker-s054","q":"How do you ensure data persistence across restarts?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker volume create persistent_volume\ndocker run -v persistent_volume:/app/data &lt;image_name&gt;\ndocker inspect persistent_volume</code></pre></div>\n<p>Use named volumes, back up critical data, and verify mounts.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0425","card":"q-docker-053-nfs-and-persistence","sec":"docker-s054","q":"In Docker, how do you create a volume backed by an NFS share?","a":"<p>Use the <code>local</code> driver with NFS options:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker volume create --driver local \\\n  --opt type=nfs \\\n  --opt o=addr=10.0.0.5,rw \\\n  --opt device=:/exports/share \\\n  nfsvol</code></pre></div>\n<p>Then mount it with <code>-v nfsvol:/data</code>. The host needs the NFS client package (<code>nfs-common</code> on Debian/Ubuntu, <code>nfs-utils</code> on RHEL). Docker mounts the share on the host when the container starts.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0426","card":"q-docker-053-nfs-and-persistence","sec":"docker-s054","q":"What problems should you expect when a database stores its files on NFS?","a":"<p>Network latency slows every I/O, file locking and caching semantics are weaker so databases (and SQLite) can corrupt data, and the NFS server becomes a single point of failure. Permission issues also appear from UID mismatches and root squashing. NFS is fine for shared files and media; for databases use local block storage (e.g. EBS) or the database's own replication.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0427","card":"q-docker-053-nfs-and-persistence","sec":"docker-s054","q":"In Docker, which data survives <code>docker restart</code>, <code>docker stop</code> plus <code>docker start</code>, and <code>docker rm</code>?","a":"<p>The container's writable layer survives restart and stop/start, but is deleted by <code>docker rm</code>. Data in named volumes and bind mounts survives all three (anonymous volumes survive <code>rm</code> unless you use <code>rm -v</code>). So any data that must outlive the container belongs in a volume or an external service.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0428","card":"q-docker-053-nfs-and-persistence","sec":"docker-s054","q":"In Docker Swarm, why is a local volume not enough for a stateful service?","a":"<p>A local volume exists only on the node where it was created. If Swarm reschedules the task to another node, the task starts with an empty volume. Pin the service to one node with a placement constraint (weak for availability), or use shared storage (NFS, EFS, or a cloud volume plugin) so any node can mount the data.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0429","card":"q-docker-054-build-failures","sec":"docker-s055","q":"Build fails: \"COPY failed: no such file or directory\"?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>cat Dockerfile | grep COPY\nls -l ./xyz</code></pre></div>\n<p>Fix: <code>COPY ./xyz /app/xyz</code></p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker build -t my_image .</code></pre></div>\n<p>Verify file paths and that the files are in the build context.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0430","card":"q-docker-054-build-failures","sec":"docker-s055","q":"Missing dependency in a custom image?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker logs &lt;container_id&gt;\ncat Dockerfile</code></pre></div>\n<p>Add: <code>RUN apt-get update &amp;&amp; apt-get install -y &lt;dependency&gt;</code></p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker build -t my_image .\ndocker run my_image</code></pre></div>\n<p>Check logs, update the Dockerfile, and test after the rebuild.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0431","card":"q-docker-054-build-failures","sec":"docker-s055","q":"Build fails due to a network timeout?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>ping archive.ubuntu.com</code></pre></div>\n<p>Use a mirror: <code>RUN echo \"deb http://mirror.example.com/ubuntu focal main\" &gt; /etc/apt/sources.list</code></p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker build --build-arg APT_TIMEOUT=300 -t my_image .\ndocker build --no-cache -t my_image .</code></pre></div>\n<p>Verify the network, use reliable mirrors, and cache packages locally.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0432","card":"q-docker-054-build-failures","sec":"docker-s055","q":"Inconsistent Dockerfile builds?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>cat Dockerfile</code></pre></div>\n<p>Pin versions: <code>FROM node:16.13.2; RUN npm install express@4.17.1</code></p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker build --no-cache -t my_image .\ndocker run -it &lt;builder_image&gt; bash</code></pre></div>\n<p>Pin package versions, avoid variable outputs, and standardize the build environment.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0433","card":"q-docker-054-build-failures","sec":"docker-s055","q":"In Docker, what error do you get with <code>COPY ../config.json /app/</code> and why?","a":"<p>The build fails with an error like \"forbidden path outside the build context\". The build context is the directory you pass to <code>docker build</code>, and only files inside it are sent to the daemon. Paths with <code>..</code> cannot leave it. Fix it by building from a parent directory (<code>docker build -f app/Dockerfile .</code>) or by moving the file inside the context.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0434","card":"q-docker-054-build-failures","sec":"docker-s055","q":"In Docker, <code>COPY package.json .</code> fails with \"not found\" although the file exists next to the Dockerfile. What do you check?","a":"<p>Check that the file is not excluded by <code>.dockerignore</code> (excluded files are not in the context), that the build context is the right directory (the last argument of <code>docker build</code>), that the path and its upper/lower case match exactly (Linux is case-sensitive, a Mac or Windows build may hide this), and that a multi-stage <code>COPY --from</code> points to the right stage.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0435","card":"q-docker-054-build-failures","sec":"docker-s055","q":"In Docker, why use <code>npm ci</code> instead of <code>npm install</code> in a Dockerfile?","a":"<p><code>npm ci</code> installs exactly what <code>package-lock.json</code> lists, fails if the lock file and <code>package.json</code> disagree, and starts from a clean <code>node_modules</code>. That makes builds repeatable. <code>npm install</code> can pick newer versions and rewrite the lock file. Python equivalents are pinned requirements or lock files with hashes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0436","card":"q-docker-054-build-failures","sec":"docker-s055","q":"In Docker, how do you pass a corporate proxy to a <code>docker build</code> so <code>apt-get</code> and <code>npm</code> can download packages?","a":"<p>Docker has predefined build args <code>HTTP_PROXY</code>, <code>HTTPS_PROXY</code> and <code>NO_PROXY</code> that need no <code>ARG</code> line: <code>docker build --build-arg HTTPS_PROXY=http://proxy:3128 .</code>. Docker excludes them from <code>docker history</code> and from the image's environment by default, so they do not leak into the final image. If you declare one with an explicit <code>ARG HTTPS_PROXY</code> in the Dockerfile, it becomes visible in the history. Do not put credentials in the proxy URL anyway.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0437","card":"q-docker-054-build-failures","sec":"docker-s055","q":"A multi-stage Docker build succeeds, but the final container fails with \"module not found\". What is the likely cause?","a":"<p>The dependency exists in the builder stage but was not copied into the final stage, or it was a dev dependency that the final stage's install skipped. Check which paths <code>COPY --from=builder</code> copies (e.g. <code>node_modules</code>, or a virtual env) and that production dependencies are installed in the final stage. Test by running the image, not only by building it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0438","card":"q-docker-055-faster-builds","sec":"docker-s056","q":"How do you optimize a Dockerfile for faster builds?","a":"<p>Cache dependencies by copying the dependency file first, use multi-stage builds, and minimize copied files.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Dockerfile</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>COPY requirements.txt .\nRUN pip install -r requirements.txt\nCOPY . .</code></pre></div>\n<p>Multi-stage:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Dockerfile</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>FROM node:16 AS builder\nWORKDIR /app\nCOPY package.json .\nRUN npm install\nFROM node:16-slim\nCOPY --from=builder /app /app</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker build --cache-from my_image:latest -t my_image .</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0439","card":"q-docker-055-faster-builds","sec":"docker-s056","q":"In a Dockerfile, what does <code>RUN --mount=type=cache,target=/root/.cache/pip pip install -r requirements.txt</code> do?","a":"<p>It is a BuildKit cache mount. The directory <code>/root/.cache/pip</code> persists between builds on the builder and is not stored in any image layer. When the dependency list changes and the layer must rerun, pip reuses downloaded packages and does not fetch them again. It needs BuildKit (the default builder in Docker Engine 23.0 and later).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0440","card":"q-docker-055-faster-builds","sec":"docker-s056","q":"How do multi-stage builds help build speed in BuildKit and not only image size?","a":"<p>BuildKit builds stages that do not depend on each other in parallel, and skips stages the target does not need (<code>--target</code> for a stage). Separate stages for dependencies, tests and the final image also keep each stage's cache independent, so a change in application code does not rebuild the dependency stage.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0441","card":"q-docker-056-registry-and-base-image-problems","sec":"docker-s057","q":"Pulling an outdated image from the registry?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker pull my_image:latest\ndocker image rm my_image:latest\ndocker images my_image\ndocker run my_image:latest</code></pre></div>\n<p>Avoid <code>latest</code> in production, clear the cache, and verify registry credentials.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0442","card":"q-docker-056-registry-and-base-image-problems","sec":"docker-s057","q":"Base image unavailable in the registry?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker images\ndocker tag my_image:backup my_image:latest\ndocker search &lt;image_name&gt;</code></pre></div>\n<p>Update: <code>FROM new_image:latest</code></p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker build -t my_image .</code></pre></div>\n<p>Maintain local backups, use specific tags, and test alternatives.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0443","card":"q-docker-056-registry-and-base-image-problems","sec":"docker-s057","q":"In Docker, what is the difference between <code>docker run image:latest</code> and <code>docker run --pull=always image:latest</code>?","a":"<p>By default <code>docker run</code> uses a local image with that tag if it exists and only pulls when it is missing, so an old local <code>latest</code> can run even though the registry has a newer one. <code>--pull=always</code> checks the registry first and pulls if the digest changed. Better still, deploy by immutable version tag or digest.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0444","card":"q-docker-056-registry-and-base-image-problems","sec":"docker-s057","q":"In Docker, what do the errors <code>manifest unknown</code>, <code>unauthorized</code> and <code>toomanyrequests</code> mean on <code>docker pull</code>?","a":"<p><code>manifest unknown</code>: the repository exists but the tag (or platform) does not. <code>unauthorized</code> or <code>denied</code>: wrong or missing credentials, or no permission on a private repository (a wrong name can also look like this on Docker Hub). <code>toomanyrequests</code>: Docker Hub's pull rate limit was reached; sign in or wait.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0445","card":"q-docker-056-registry-and-base-image-problems","sec":"docker-s057","q":"How do you protect your builds from an upstream base image being deleted, changed or retired?","a":"<p>Copy the base images you depend on into your own registry (for example ECR or a private Harbor) and build <code>FROM</code> that copy, pinned by version or digest. You keep control of updates, you avoid rate limits and outages of the public registry, and you can scan the images you mirror. Rebuild on a schedule so you still receive security patches.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0446","card":"q-docker-057-scanning-and-multi-arch","sec":"docker-s058","q":"How do you scan for image vulnerabilities?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>trivy image my_image:latest</code></pre></div>\n<p>Update: <code>RUN apt-get update &amp;&amp; apt-get upgrade -y</code></p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker build -t my_image:latest .\ntrivy image my_image:latest</code></pre></div>\n<p>Use scanners (Trivy/Clair), keep images updated, and retest after updates.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0447","card":"q-docker-057-scanning-and-multi-arch","sec":"docker-s058","q":"How do you create a multi-architecture image?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker buildx create --use\ndocker buildx build --platform linux/amd64,linux/arm64 -t my_image:multiarch --push .\ndocker buildx imagetools inspect my_image:multiarch</code></pre></div>\n<p>Ensure buildx is installed, specify the architectures, and push to a registry.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0448","card":"q-docker-057-scanning-and-multi-arch","sec":"docker-s058","q":"How do you make a CI pipeline fail when an image has serious vulnerabilities, using Trivy?","a":"<p>Run <code>trivy image --severity HIGH,CRITICAL --exit-code 1 &lt;image&gt;</code>. Trivy exits with 1 if it finds vulnerabilities at those levels, which fails the job. <code>--ignore-unfixed</code> hides issues with no available fix, so the gate only blocks what you can act on. Keep a reviewed ignore file for accepted risks, with expiry dates.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0449","card":"q-docker-057-scanning-and-multi-arch","sec":"docker-s058","q":"A scan reports a vulnerability in a base image package and no fixed version exists. What do you do?","a":"<p>Check if it is exploitable in your use (is the package or code path used, is it reachable, is a patch pending). Options: move to a smaller base image that does not contain the package (slim, distroless), wait for the patch and rebuild, add a documented exception, or add compensating controls. Running <code>apt-get upgrade</code> in your Dockerfile cannot install a fix that does not exist yet.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0450","card":"q-docker-057-scanning-and-multi-arch","sec":"docker-s058","q":"In Docker, how do you check that a pushed image really contains both amd64 and arm64?","a":"<p>Run <code>docker buildx imagetools inspect &lt;image:tag&gt;</code>. It lists the manifest list entries, one per platform (e.g. <code>linux/amd64</code>, <code>linux/arm64</code>) with their digests. <code>docker manifest inspect</code> shows the same. If a platform is missing, a host of that architecture fails with an \"exec format error\" or \"no matching manifest\".</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0451","card":"q-docker-058-non-root-and-filesystem-restrictions","sec":"docker-s059","q":"How do you run a container as a non-root user?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Dockerfile</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>FROM node:16\nRUN useradd -m appuser\nUSER appuser\nCOPY . /app\nWORKDIR /app\nCMD [&quot;node&quot;, &quot;app.js&quot;]</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker run --user appuser &lt;image_name&gt;\ndocker build -t &lt;image_name&gt; .</code></pre></div>\n<p>Verify the app supports non-root and check permissions.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0452","card":"q-docker-058-non-root-and-filesystem-restrictions","sec":"docker-s059","q":"How do you restrict container filesystem access?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker run --read-only &lt;image_name&gt;\ndocker run -v /host/path:/container/path:ro &lt;image_name&gt;\ndocker run --tmpfs /tmp &lt;image_name&gt;</code></pre></div>\n<p>Avoid sensitive mounts, use read-only, and combine with user namespaces.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0453","card":"q-docker-058-non-root-and-filesystem-restrictions","sec":"docker-s059","q":"In a Dockerfile, why use a numeric UID (<code>USER 10001</code>) rather than a name (<code>USER appuser</code>)?","a":"<p>Orchestrators such as Kubernetes can check a numeric UID is not 0 (<code>runAsNonRoot</code>), but cannot verify a user name from the image metadata, and a name only exists if you created it in the image. A fixed numeric UID also makes volume ownership predictable across images and hosts.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0454","card":"q-docker-058-non-root-and-filesystem-restrictions","sec":"docker-s059","q":"A non-root container fails with \"permission denied\" when it binds to port 80. How do you solve it?","a":"<p>Ports under 1024 are privileged. Options: have the app listen on a high port (8080) and map it with <code>-p 80:8080</code> or a load balancer (the best choice), add only the <code>NET_BIND_SERVICE</code> capability, or lower <code>net.ipv4.ip_unprivileged_port_start</code> for the container's network namespace. Do not fall back to running as root.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0455","card":"q-docker-058-non-root-and-filesystem-restrictions","sec":"docker-s059","q":"In Docker, why does <code>COPY --chown=appuser:appuser . /app</code> matter when you also set <code>USER appuser</code>?","a":"<p><code>COPY</code> creates files owned by root by default, whatever <code>USER</code> is set. A non-root process would then be unable to write to them. <code>--chown</code> sets the right owner at copy time without an extra <code>RUN chown</code> layer, which would duplicate the data in a new layer.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0456","card":"q-docker-058-non-root-and-filesystem-restrictions","sec":"docker-s059","q":"In Docker, after you set <code>--read-only</code> the app crashes. How do you find which paths it needs to write?","a":"<p>Run the container without <code>--read-only</code> under normal load and then <code>docker diff &lt;container&gt;</code>, which lists files added (A), changed (C) or deleted (D) in the writable layer. Those paths (often <code>/tmp</code>, <code>/var/run</code>, cache or log directories) become <code>--tmpfs</code> mounts or volumes, and everything else stays read-only.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0457","card":"q-docker-059-capabilities-and-security-policies","sec":"docker-s060","q":"A container bypasses security policies?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker inspect &lt;container_id&gt; | grep CapAdd\ndocker inspect &lt;container_id&gt; | grep Privileged\ndocker run --cap-drop=ALL --cap-add=NET_BIND_SERVICE &lt;image_name&gt;\ndocker run --security-opt apparmor=docker-default &lt;image_name&gt;</code></pre></div>\n<p>Avoid privileged mode, apply AppArmor/SELinux, and audit configurations.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0458","card":"q-docker-059-capabilities-and-security-policies","sec":"docker-s060","q":"How do you reduce container capabilities?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker inspect &lt;container_id&gt; | grep CapAdd\ndocker run --cap-drop=ALL --cap-add=CHOWN --cap-add=SETUID &lt;image_name&gt;\ndocker exec &lt;container_id&gt; capsh --print</code></pre></div>\n<p>Drop unnecessary capabilities and test functionality.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0459","card":"q-docker-059-capabilities-and-security-policies","sec":"docker-s060","q":"What are Linux capabilities and why does Docker use them?","a":"<p>Capabilities split the all-powerful root privilege into separate rights, e.g. <code>NET_ADMIN</code> for network configuration, <code>CHOWN</code> to change ownership, <code>SYS_ADMIN</code> (very broad). Docker starts containers with a small default set, so even root inside a container cannot do most privileged operations on the host. You add rights with <code>--cap-add</code> or remove them with <code>--cap-drop</code> to follow least privilege.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0460","card":"q-docker-059-capabilities-and-security-policies","sec":"docker-s060","q":"What does <code>docker run --privileged</code> actually change and why is it dangerous?","a":"<p>It gives the container all capabilities, access to all host devices, and relaxes the default seccomp and AppArmor restrictions. Root in such a container can mount host disks, load kernel modules and easily escape to the host. It should only be used for special cases, such as Docker-in-Docker for trusted CI, and in isolated hosts.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0461","card":"q-docker-059-capabilities-and-security-policies","sec":"docker-s060","q":"In Docker, which hosts use AppArmor and which use SELinux for container confinement?","a":"<p>Debian and Ubuntu hosts use AppArmor (Docker loads a default <code>docker-default</code> profile). RHEL, CentOS Stream and Fedora use SELinux (with <code>container_t</code> labels and the <code>:z</code>/<code>:Z</code> mount options). Pick the one your distribution supports. Do not disable it to solve a permission problem; adjust the profile or labels.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0462","card":"q-docker-060-investigating-a-compromise","sec":"docker-s061","q":"How do you investigate a compromised container?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker top &lt;container_id&gt;\ndocker logs &lt;container_id&gt;\ndocker run --rm -v /var/run/docker.sock:/var/run/docker.sock aquasec/trivy image &lt;image_name&gt;\ndocker pause &lt;container_id&gt;\ndocker rm -f &lt;container_id&gt;</code></pre></div>\n<p>Quarantine the container, check the network, and rebuild from a trusted source.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0463","card":"q-docker-060-investigating-a-compromise","sec":"docker-s061","q":"How do you block unauthorized container network requests?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker network inspect &lt;network_name&gt;\ntcpdump -i docker0\niptables -A OUTPUT -s &lt;container_ip&gt; -j DROP\ndocker run --network none &lt;image_name&gt;</code></pre></div>\n<p>Use network namespaces, firewall rules, and audit ports.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0464","card":"q-docker-060-investigating-a-compromise","sec":"docker-s061","q":"When a container may be compromised, why is <code>docker rm -f</code> the wrong first step?","a":"<p>It destroys the evidence (file changes, running processes, in-memory state) and does not tell you how the attacker got in. First contain: disconnect it from the network or <code>docker pause</code> it, block its egress. Then preserve: <code>docker diff</code>, <code>docker logs</code>, <code>docker inspect</code>, <code>docker export</code>, and host logs. Then eradicate, rebuild from a trusted image, rotate credentials and fix the cause.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0465","card":"q-docker-060-investigating-a-compromise","sec":"docker-s061","q":"What does <code>docker diff &lt;container&gt;</code> show and why is it useful in an incident?","a":"<p>It lists the files that changed in the container's writable layer compared with the image: <code>A</code> added, <code>C</code> changed, <code>D</code> deleted. Since images are immutable, unexpected new binaries, changed config or scripts in <code>/tmp</code> stand out immediately and show what an attacker modified.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0466","card":"q-docker-060-investigating-a-compromise","sec":"docker-s061","q":"Which signs suggest an attacker escaped from a container to the host?","a":"<p>Check if the container ran privileged, had <code>/var/run/docker.sock</code> or host paths such as <code>/</code> or <code>/etc</code> mounted, had dangerous capabilities (<code>SYS_ADMIN</code>), or ran on a host with an unpatched kernel or runtime. Then look on the host for new users, SSH keys, cron jobs, unknown processes, and containers you did not start. If any is true, treat the whole host as compromised.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0467","card":"q-docker-060-investigating-a-compromise","sec":"docker-s061","q":"Why do teams rebuild and redeploy a compromised container from a trusted image instead of cleaning it up in place?","a":"<p>You cannot be sure what an attacker changed, so cleaning is unreliable. Images are meant to be immutable and containers disposable, so redeploying from a known-good, scanned image is fast and certain. Before that, find and close the entry point (a vulnerable library, exposed secret, open port), or the new container will be hit the same way.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0468","card":"q-docker-061-credentials-and-vulnerable-libraries","sec":"docker-s062","q":"How do you remove sensitive credentials from an image?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker history &lt;image_name&gt;\ndocker run --rm -v /var/run/docker.sock:/var/run/docker.sock aquasec/trivy image &lt;image_name&gt;</code></pre></div>\n<p>Rebuild without credentials:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Dockerfile</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>FROM node:16\nCOPY . /app\nWORKDIR /app\nCMD [&quot;node&quot;, &quot;app.js&quot;]</code></pre></div>\n<p>Use secrets or environment variables, and squash layers.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0469","card":"q-docker-061-credentials-and-vulnerable-libraries","sec":"docker-s062","q":"How do you update a vulnerable library in a container?","a":"<p>Scan with the same Trivy command, then update: <code>FROM node:16; RUN apt-get update &amp;&amp; apt-get upgrade -y</code></p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker build -t &lt;image_name&gt;:latest .\ndocker push &lt;image_name&gt;:latest</code></pre></div>\n<p>Update base images, automate scans, and test compatibility.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0470","card":"q-docker-061-credentials-and-vulnerable-libraries","sec":"docker-s062","q":"A Dockerfile runs <code>COPY .env /app/.env</code> and a later <code>RUN rm /app/.env</code>. Is the secret gone from the image?","a":"<p>No. Each instruction creates a layer, and the layer with the <code>COPY</code> still contains the file. Anyone who pulls the image can extract that layer, for example with <code>docker save</code>. Deleting in a later layer only hides the file. Keep secrets out of the build with BuildKit secret mounts, and use runtime injection.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0471","card":"q-docker-061-credentials-and-vulnerable-libraries","sec":"docker-s062","q":"A secret was baked into an image that was pushed to a registry. What do you do?","a":"<p>Assume it is compromised. Rotate or revoke the credential first, since deleting the image does not undo copies that others pulled or caches. Then rebuild the image without the secret, push it, remove the old tags and digests from the registry, and check access logs for use of the credential. Add secret scanning (e.g. Trivy or gitleaks) to CI.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0472","card":"q-docker-061-credentials-and-vulnerable-libraries","sec":"docker-s062","q":"What is an SBOM and why produce one for a Docker image?","a":"<p>A software bill of materials lists the packages and libraries inside an image (and their versions). When a new vulnerability such as a critical library bug is announced, you can query SBOMs to find which images are affected without rescanning everything. BuildKit can attach one at build time with <code>docker buildx build --sbom=true</code>, and tools such as Syft or Trivy can create them.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0473","card":"q-docker-062-access-control-and-registry-trust","sec":"docker-s063","q":"How do you implement Docker role-based access control?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>useradd -m dockeruser\ngroupadd docker\nusermod -aG docker dockeruser\nchown root:docker /var/run/docker.sock\nchmod 660 /var/run/docker.sock\ndocker context create restricted --docker host=unix:///var/run/docker.sock</code></pre></div>\n<p>Limit the <code>docker</code> group, use TLS, and audit permissions.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0474","card":"q-docker-062-access-control-and-registry-trust","sec":"docker-s063","q":"How do you verify a registry for malicious images?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker run --rm -v /var/run/docker.sock:/var/run/docker.sock aquasec/trivy image &lt;registry&gt;/&lt;image_name&gt;\ndocker trust inspect &lt;registry&gt;/&lt;image_name&gt;\ndocker pull --disable-content-trust=false &lt;registry&gt;/&lt;image_name&gt;\ndocker login &lt;registry&gt;</code></pre></div>\n<p>Use trusted registries, enable Docker Content Trust, and scan regularly.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0475","card":"q-docker-062-access-control-and-registry-trust","sec":"docker-s063","q":"Why is membership of the <code>docker</code> group effectively root access on the host?","a":"<p>Anyone who can talk to the Docker daemon can start a container that mounts the host file system, e.g. <code>docker run -v /:/host --privileged alpine chroot /host</code>, and read or change anything as root. So add only trusted administrators to the group, or use rootless Docker, and treat the socket as a root credential.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0476","card":"q-docker-062-access-control-and-registry-trust","sec":"docker-s063","q":"Does Docker Engine have built-in role-based access control?","a":"<p>No. The Engine either lets a client use the full API or not. Options: Unix socket permissions, TLS client certificates for a remote daemon, authorization plugins, rootless mode, or an orchestrator or platform with real RBAC (for example Kubernetes RBAC, or ECS with IAM). Registry access control is separate and lives in the registry.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0477","card":"q-docker-062-access-control-and-registry-trust","sec":"docker-s063","q":"In Docker, why is exposing the daemon on TCP port 2375 dangerous, and what are the safe options for remote access?","a":"<p>Port 2375 is unencrypted and unauthenticated, so anyone who can reach it controls the host as root; attackers scan for this to run crypto miners. Safe options: use SSH (<code>docker context create remote --docker host=ssh://user@host</code>), or TLS with client certificates on port 2376 and firewall rules limiting which client addresses may connect.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0478","card":"q-docker-062-access-control-and-registry-trust","sec":"docker-s063","q":"What does signing images with cosign add compared with only pulling by tag?","a":"<p>A tag is mutable and says nothing about who built the image. A signature binds an image digest to a key or an identity (keyless signing uses OIDC identities), and <code>cosign verify</code> proves the image came from your pipeline and was not changed. Combined with pulling by digest and a policy check at deploy time, it protects against tampered or substituted images.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0479","card":"q-docker-063-compose-problems","sec":"docker-s064","q":"Docker Compose \"service not found\" error?","a":"<p>Check <code>docker-compose.yml</code> for the service definition, validate syntax (<code>docker-compose config</code>), and restart (<code>docker-compose up -d</code>). Verify service names, file paths and the Compose version.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0480","card":"q-docker-063-compose-problems","sec":"docker-s064","q":"Compose service communication failure?","a":"<p>Verify the network (<code>docker network inspect &lt;network&gt;</code>), test connectivity (<code>docker exec &lt;container&gt; ping &lt;service&gt;</code>), and ensure service names resolve. Check DNS, firewall and network configuration.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0481","card":"q-docker-063-compose-problems","sec":"docker-s064","q":"Compose ignores environment variables?","a":"<p>Verify <code>docker-compose.yml</code> (<code>env_file</code> or <code>environment</code>), check the container (<code>docker inspect &lt;container&gt; | grep Env</code>), and restart (<code>docker-compose up -d</code>). Validate variable syntax and avoid overrides.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0482","card":"q-docker-063-compose-problems","sec":"docker-s064","q":"Compose crashes due to resource limits?","a":"<p>Check limits (<code>docker inspect &lt;container&gt;</code>), adjust <code>docker-compose.yml</code> (<code>mem_limit: 512m</code>, <code>cpus: 0.5</code>), and restart (<code>docker-compose up -d</code>). Monitor resource usage and balance limits.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0483","card":"q-docker-063-compose-problems","sec":"docker-s064","q":"Compose file startup errors?","a":"<p>Validate syntax (<code>docker-compose config</code>), check logs (<code>docker-compose logs</code>), fix errors, and restart (<code>docker-compose up -d</code>). Verify service dependencies and image availability.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0484","card":"q-docker-063-compose-problems","sec":"docker-s064","q":"In Docker Compose, <code>docker compose logs web</code> says \"no such service\" while <code>docker logs web</code> works. Why?","a":"<p>Compose commands use service names from the Compose file (e.g. <code>web</code>), while plain <code>docker</code> commands use container names, which Compose builds as <code>&lt;project&gt;-&lt;service&gt;-&lt;number&gt;</code> (for example <code>myapp-web-1</code>) unless <code>container_name</code> is set. A mix-up of the two is a common cause of \"not found\" errors, as is running the command in a directory with a different project.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0485","card":"q-docker-063-compose-problems","sec":"docker-s064","q":"In Docker Compose, how does Compose find its file, and how do you choose another file or project name?","a":"<p>It looks in the current directory for <code>compose.yaml</code>, <code>compose.yml</code>, <code>docker-compose.yaml</code> or <code>docker-compose.yml</code> (the first form is preferred in the Compose Specification). Use <code>-f other.yml</code> (repeatable to merge files) to choose a file and <code>-p name</code> or <code>COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME</code> for the project name. The project name defaults to the directory name, and it prefixes resource names.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0486","card":"q-docker-063-compose-problems","sec":"docker-s064","q":"In Docker Compose, a <code>${DB_HOST}</code> in <code>docker-compose.yml</code> is empty at runtime. Why?","a":"<p><code>${VAR}</code> in the Compose file is interpolated by Compose from the shell environment or the <code>.env</code> file in the project directory. Variables in an <code>env_file:</code> are only passed to the container and are not used for interpolation. Check with <code>docker compose config</code>, which prints the resolved file, and set a default with <code>${DB_HOST:-localhost}</code> or a required check with <code>${DB_HOST:?missing}</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0487","card":"q-docker-063-compose-problems","sec":"docker-s064","q":"In Docker Compose V2 (Compose Specification), how do you set memory and CPU limits for a service?","a":"<p>Use <code>deploy.resources.limits</code> (e.g. <code>cpus: \"0.5\"</code>, <code>memory: 512M</code>), which Compose V2 applies on a single host. The older <code>mem_limit</code> and <code>cpus</code> service keys also still work. Legacy <code>docker-compose</code> 1.x applied <code>deploy</code> limits only with <code>--compatibility</code>. A container that hits the memory limit is OOM-killed, so test with real load.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0488","card":"q-docker-063-compose-problems","sec":"docker-s064","q":"In Docker Compose, what is the warning about orphan containers and how do you clear it?","a":"<p>It appears when containers of the same project exist for services no longer in the Compose file (e.g. you renamed or removed a service). They keep running and holding resources. Use <code>docker compose up -d --remove-orphans</code> or <code>docker compose down --remove-orphans</code> to remove them.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0489","card":"q-docker-063-compose-problems","sec":"docker-s064","q":"In Docker Compose, what is the risk of <code>docker compose down -v</code>?","a":"<p><code>-v</code> also removes the named volumes declared in the Compose file, so a database's data is deleted with the containers. Plain <code>down</code> keeps volumes. Use <code>-v</code> only to reset a development environment on purpose, and never on stacks whose volumes hold data you need.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0490","card":"q-docker-064-swarm-problems","sec":"docker-s065","q":"Swarm service stuck in \"pending\"?","a":"<p>Check node resources (<code>docker node ls</code>), logs (<code>docker service logs &lt;service&gt;</code>) and constraints. Scale or adjust (<code>docker service update</code>). Ensure enough nodes and check placement constraints.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0491","card":"q-docker-064-swarm-problems","sec":"docker-s065","q":"Swarm service not scaling?","a":"<p>Check resources (<code>docker node ls</code>), update replicas (<code>docker service scale &lt;service&gt;=3</code>), and review logs (<code>docker service logs &lt;service&gt;</code>). Ensure node capacity and check constraints.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0492","card":"q-docker-064-swarm-problems","sec":"docker-s065","q":"Swarm node marked \"Down\"?","a":"<p>Check status (<code>docker node ls</code>) and logs (<code>journalctl -u docker</code>), then restart the node (<code>docker node update --availability active &lt;node&gt;</code>). Verify the network, the Docker daemon and node health.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0493","card":"q-docker-064-swarm-problems","sec":"docker-s065","q":"Swarm service not load balancing?","a":"<p>Check the routing mesh (<code>docker network inspect ingress</code>), verify replicas (<code>docker service ps &lt;service&gt;</code>), and test endpoints (<code>curl &lt;node_ip&gt;</code>). Ensure correct ports and check the load balancer config.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0494","card":"q-docker-064-swarm-problems","sec":"docker-s065","q":"In Docker Swarm, what is the difference between node availability <code>active</code>, <code>pause</code> and <code>drain</code>?","a":"<p><code>active</code>: the node can receive new tasks. <code>pause</code>: it keeps its running tasks but receives no new ones. <code>drain</code>: it receives no tasks and Swarm moves its running tasks to other nodes. Use drain for maintenance. A node's status (<code>ready</code> or <code>down</code>) is different: it reports whether the manager can reach it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0495","card":"q-docker-064-swarm-problems","sec":"docker-s065","q":"In Docker Swarm, how do you see why a service's tasks keep failing and restarting?","a":"<p>Run <code>docker service ps --no-trunc &lt;service&gt;</code>. It lists each task, including failed earlier ones, with the full error message (e.g. image not found, no suitable node, task exited with code 1). Then use <code>docker service logs &lt;service&gt;</code> for the application output, and <code>docker service inspect</code> for constraints and resources.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0496","card":"q-docker-064-swarm-problems","sec":"docker-s065","q":"In Docker Swarm, why does a service fail to start on worker nodes when it uses an image from a private registry?","a":"<p>The worker nodes have no credentials for the registry. Deploy with <code>docker service create --with-registry-auth</code> or <code>docker stack deploy --with-registry-auth</code>; the manager then sends your login credentials to the workers that run tasks. Without it, the tasks stay <code>Rejected</code> with a pull error.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0497","card":"q-docker-064-swarm-problems","sec":"docker-s065","q":"In Docker Swarm, a node comes back after downtime but sits idle while other nodes are busy. Why?","a":"<p>Swarm does not move running tasks back to rebalance. Tasks stay where they were rescheduled, and only new tasks go to the empty node. To spread them again, run <code>docker service update --force &lt;service&gt;</code>, which recreates tasks (use a rolling update setting so it does not cause downtime).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0498","card":"q-docker-064-swarm-problems","sec":"docker-s065","q":"In Docker Swarm, what is the difference between publishing a port in <code>ingress</code> mode and in <code>host</code> mode?","a":"<p><code>ingress</code> (default) uses the routing mesh: the port is open on every node and traffic is forwarded to any task, even on another node. <code>host</code> mode publishes the port only on nodes that run a task and goes straight to that container, with no routing mesh. Host mode keeps the client's source IP and avoids an extra hop, but needs a load balancer that knows which nodes run the tasks.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0499","card":"q-docker-065-rolling-updates","sec":"docker-s066","q":"How do you do a rolling update for a Swarm service?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker service update --image &lt;image&gt;:latest &lt;service&gt; --update-parallelism 1 --update-delay 10s</code></pre></div>\n<p>Test updates, ensure health checks, and monitor with <code>docker service ps &lt;service&gt;</code>.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0500","card":"q-docker-065-rolling-updates","sec":"docker-s066","q":"In Docker Swarm, what does <code>--update-monitor</code> do in a rolling update?","a":"<p>It sets how long Swarm watches each updated task after it starts (default 5 seconds) to decide if the update worked. If a task fails during that window, <code>--update-failure-action</code> (pause, continue or rollback) applies. Use a longer monitor period when the app needs time before it fails, and combine it with health checks.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0501","card":"q-docker-066-finding-bottlenecks","sec":"docker-s067","q":"High latency in a Dockerized app?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker stats\ntop -p $(pidof dockerd)\niostat -x 1\nnetstat -i</code></pre></div>\n<p>Check CPU, memory, disk and network bottlenecks. Correlate container and host metrics.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0502","card":"q-docker-066-finding-bottlenecks","sec":"docker-s067","q":"A slow containerized app?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker stats &lt;container_name&gt;\ndocker top &lt;container_name&gt;\niostat -x 1\nnetstat -tunap</code></pre></div>\n<p>Check resources, processes, disk and network. Correlate with logs and identify contention.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0503","card":"q-docker-066-finding-bottlenecks","sec":"docker-s067","q":"How do you analyze high load with Linux tools?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>top\nhtop\nsar -u 1 5\nsar -r 1 5</code></pre></div>\n<p>Identify high-CPU and high-memory processes and check trends. Look for Docker processes and monitor historical data.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0504","card":"q-docker-066-finding-bottlenecks","sec":"docker-s067","q":"What is the USE method and how does it apply to a Docker host?","a":"<p>For every resource (CPU, memory, disk, network) check Utilization (how busy), Saturation (queued work, e.g. run queue, swap, I/O wait) and Errors (drops, failed I/O, OOM kills). It gives a fast, systematic way to find the bottleneck without guessing. Apply it to both the host and each container's cgroup limits.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0505","card":"q-docker-066-finding-bottlenecks","sec":"docker-s067","q":"What does load average mean and why can it be high while CPU use is low?","a":"<p>Load average is the average number of tasks that are running, runnable or in uninterruptible sleep. On Linux, tasks waiting for disk or NFS I/O count too. So high load with low CPU usually means I/O wait. Check <code>iostat -x</code> and <code>top</code>'s <code>wa</code> value to confirm.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0506","card":"q-docker-066-finding-bottlenecks","sec":"docker-s067","q":"A container has a <code>--cpus</code> limit and shows modest CPU use in <code>docker stats</code>, yet the app is slow. What can explain this?","a":"<p>CPU throttling. The CFS quota gives the container a limited CPU time per 100 ms period, and when it is used up the processes pause until the next period, adding latency even when the average looks low. Check <code>nr_throttled</code> and <code>throttled_time</code> in the cgroup's <code>cpu.stat</code>. Raise the limit, or use <code>--cpu-shares</code> (a relative weight with no hard cap).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0507","card":"q-docker-066-finding-bottlenecks","sec":"docker-s067","q":"In Linux, what do high <code>%iowait</code> and high <code>await</code> in <code>iostat -x</code> tell you?","a":"<p>The CPU is waiting on disk. High <code>await</code> (average time per I/O request) and <code>%util</code> near 100 on a device show the disk is saturated or slow. Look for the process responsible (<code>iotop</code>), check whether a container writes heavily to its writable layer or logs, and consider faster storage, volumes on a dedicated disk, or I/O limits.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0508","card":"q-docker-067-memory-disk-and-network","sec":"docker-s068","q":"Periodic memory spikes?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker stats &lt;container_name&gt;\ndocker inspect &lt;container_name&gt; | grep Memory\nsmem -k -p -c pss,uss</code></pre></div>\n<p>Monitor spikes, check limits, and analyze processes. Verify application activity and check for leaks.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0509","card":"q-docker-067-memory-disk-and-network","sec":"docker-s068","q":"Excessive container disk I/O?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>iostat -x 1\niotop -o -P\ndocker inspect &lt;container_name&gt; | grep Mounts</code></pre></div>\n<p>Identify high-I/O processes and check mounts. Optimize app I/O and check host issues.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0510","card":"q-docker-067-memory-disk-and-network","sec":"docker-s068","q":"Network bottlenecks on the Docker host?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>iftop -i eth0\nnload eth0\ndocker network inspect bridge</code></pre></div>\n<p>Monitor traffic and check container network config. Identify high-traffic containers and check policies.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0511","card":"q-docker-067-memory-disk-and-network","sec":"docker-s068","q":"In Linux, why can high memory use in a container be normal and not a leak?","a":"<p>A container's memory counter includes page cache (file data the kernel caches), which is reclaimable and is released when memory is needed. A real leak shows resident memory that keeps growing under steady load and never levels off, and ends in an OOM kill. Compare resident set size over time, not one reading.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0512","card":"q-docker-067-memory-disk-and-network","sec":"docker-s068","q":"What does PSS mean in <code>smem</code> output and why is it more useful than RSS?","a":"<p>PSS (proportional set size) counts a process's private memory plus its share of memory shared with other processes (shared libraries are divided between the processes using them). RSS counts shared pages fully for each process, so adding RSS over many processes overstates total use. PSS adds up to the real total.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0513","card":"q-docker-067-memory-disk-and-network","sec":"docker-s068","q":"How do you limit a container's disk I/O in Docker?","a":"<p>Use <code>--device-read-bps</code> and <code>--device-write-bps</code> (bytes per second) or <code>--device-read-iops</code> and <code>--device-write-iops</code> on a specific device, for example <code>--device-write-bps /dev/sda:10mb</code>. <code>--blkio-weight</code> sets a relative weight. These apply to block devices, not to network file systems, and need a supported I/O scheduler setup.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0514","card":"q-docker-067-memory-disk-and-network","sec":"docker-s068","q":"On a busy Docker host, <code>iftop</code> shows heavy traffic. How do you find which container causes it?","a":"<p><code>iftop</code> shows IP addresses, so map them to containers with <code>docker network inspect</code> or <code>docker inspect -f '{{.NetworkSettings.IPAddress}}'</code>. Better, read per-container counters with <code>docker stats</code> (NET I/O) or cAdvisor, and capture a container's traffic by running <code>tcpdump</code> in its network namespace.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0515","card":"q-docker-068-monitoring-setup-benchmarking-and-profil","sec":"docker-s069","q":"How do you set up monitoring?","a":"<p>Monitor CPU, memory, I/O and network with Prometheus and Grafana.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker run -d --name prometheus -p 9090:9090 -v /path/to/prometheus.yml:/etc/prometheus/prometheus.yml prom/prometheus\ndocker run -d --name grafana -p 3000:3000 grafana/grafana</code></pre></div>\n<p>Configure Prometheus scraping and set alerts.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0516","card":"q-docker-068-monitoring-setup-benchmarking-and-profil","sec":"docker-s069","q":"How do you benchmark container performance?","a":"<p>Simulate load and monitor metrics.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker run -it &lt;container_name&gt; stress --cpu 2 --io 1 --vm 1 --vm-bytes 128M --timeout 60s\ndocker stats &lt;container_name&gt;\nperf stat -p $(docker top &lt;container_name&gt; | awk &#x27;NR&gt;1 {print $2}&#x27;)</code></pre></div>\n<p>Use realistic workloads and compare against baselines.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0517","card":"q-docker-068-monitoring-setup-benchmarking-and-profil","sec":"docker-s069","q":"How do you profile container CPU usage?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker stats &lt;container_name&gt;\ndocker top &lt;container_name&gt;\nperf stat -p $(docker top &lt;container_name&gt; | awk &#x27;NR&gt;1 {print $2}&#x27;)\ndocker update --cpus=&quot;0.5&quot; &lt;container_name&gt;</code></pre></div>\n<p>Monitor and limit CPU usage. Identify intensive processes and balance limits.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0518","card":"q-docker-068-monitoring-setup-benchmarking-and-profil","sec":"docker-s069","q":"How do you capture intermittent container crashes?","a":"<p>Stream logs, monitor events, and use Promtail/Loki.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker logs &lt;container_name&gt; --follow\ndocker events --filter &#x27;container=&lt;container_name&gt;&#x27;\ndocker run -d --name promtail -v /var/log:/var/log loki/promtail -config.file=/etc/promtail/promtail.yml</code></pre></div>\n<p>Correlate crashes with resources and ensure log capture.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0519","card":"q-docker-068-monitoring-setup-benchmarking-and-profil","sec":"docker-s069","q":"In a Prometheus and Grafana stack for Docker, what role does cAdvisor play?","a":"<p>cAdvisor reads the cgroup and runtime data on each host and exposes per-container CPU, memory, network and disk metrics at an HTTP endpoint. Prometheus scrapes that endpoint, stores the time series and evaluates alert rules, and Grafana draws dashboards from Prometheus. Add node exporter for host metrics. Docker's own <code>metrics-addr</code> setting in <code>daemon.json</code> exposes metrics of the daemon, not of each container.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0520","card":"q-docker-068-monitoring-setup-benchmarking-and-profil","sec":"docker-s069","q":"What makes a good alert for containers and why not alert on every CPU spike?","a":"<p>Alert on symptoms users feel and on saturation that persists: sustained high latency or error rate, memory close to the limit, OOM kills, restart loops, disk filling, and failing health checks, each over a time window. Short spikes are normal, and alerting on them produces noise that teams learn to ignore. Link each alert to a runbook.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0521","card":"q-docker-068-monitoring-setup-benchmarking-and-profil","sec":"docker-s069","q":"What makes a container benchmark trustworthy?","a":"<p>Use a realistic workload and data size (a synthetic CPU burner such as <code>stress</code> only shows limits). Warm up first, repeat runs, and use the same hardware, limits and image versions. Record a baseline and compare percentiles (p95, p99) and not only the average. Stop other noisy workloads on the host, and keep a load generator on a separate machine.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0522","card":"q-docker-069-missing-or-verbose-logs","sec":"docker-s070","q":"No logs from the containerized app?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker inspect &lt;container_name&gt; | grep LogConfig\ndocker run --log-driver=json-file --log-opt max-size=10m &lt;container_name&gt;\ndocker logs &lt;container_name&gt;</code></pre></div>\n<p>Configure the <code>json-file</code> driver and verify logs. Ensure the app writes to stdout/stderr and check driver compatibility.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0523","card":"q-docker-069-missing-or-verbose-logs","sec":"docker-s070","q":"How do you increase log verbosity for crashes?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker inspect &lt;container_name&gt; | grep LogConfig\ndocker run --log-driver=json-file --log-opt max-size=50m &lt;container_name&gt; --verbose\ndocker logs &lt;container_name&gt;</code></pre></div>\n<p>Enable verbose mode and increase log size. Verify the app supports verbose and monitor storage.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0524","card":"q-docker-069-missing-or-verbose-logs","sec":"docker-s070","q":"How do you enable debugging for silent failures?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker run --log-driver=json-file --log-opt max-size=10m &lt;container_name&gt; --debug\ndocker events --filter &#x27;container=&lt;container_name&gt;&#x27;\ndocker logs &lt;container_name&gt;</code></pre></div>\n<p>Enable debug and monitor events and logs. Verify debug support and check the config for suppressed logs.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0525","card":"q-docker-069-missing-or-verbose-logs","sec":"docker-s070","q":"How do you redirect container logs to a file?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker run --log-driver=json-file --log-opt max-size=10m -v /host/path:/logs &lt;container_name&gt;\ndocker exec &lt;container_name&gt; sh -c &#x27;echo &quot;log output&quot; &gt; /logs/app.log&#x27;</code></pre></div>\n<p>Use a volume for log files. Check permissions and monitor log growth.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0526","card":"q-docker-069-missing-or-verbose-logs","sec":"docker-s070","q":"After you change the log options in <code>daemon.json</code>, why do existing containers keep logging the old way?","a":"<p>The logging driver and its options are fixed when a container is created. A daemon default applies only to containers created afterwards, and the daemon must be restarted to load it. Recreate the containers, or set <code>--log-driver</code> and <code>--log-opt</code> per container.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0527","card":"q-docker-069-missing-or-verbose-logs","sec":"docker-s070","q":"How do you turn on debug logging for the Docker daemon without restarting your containers?","a":"<p>Set <code>\"debug\": true</code> in <code>/etc/docker/daemon.json</code> and send SIGHUP to <code>dockerd</code> (<code>kill -SIGHUP $(pidof dockerd)</code>), which reloads the setting without restart. Daemon logs then show more detail in <code>journalctl -u docker</code>. Turn it off after troubleshooting, since it is verbose.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0528","card":"q-docker-069-missing-or-verbose-logs","sec":"docker-s070","q":"How do you change an application's log level in a container without rebuilding the image?","a":"<p>Read the level from an environment variable (e.g. <code>LOG_LEVEL=debug</code>) or a mounted config file, and set it with <code>-e</code> or in the Compose file, then recreate the container. For some apps a signal or admin endpoint changes the level at runtime. Do not ship debug as the default in production because of the volume and the risk of logging secrets.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0529","card":"q-docker-070-centralized-logging","sec":"docker-s071","q":"How do you collect logs from multiple containers, or centralize logs from multiple hosts?","a":"<p>Use Loki and Promtail for aggregation and query via Grafana.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker run -d --name loki -p 3100:3100 grafana/loki\ndocker run -d --name promtail -v /var/lib/docker/containers:/var/lib/docker/containers loki/promtail -config.file=/etc/promtail/promtail.yml</code></pre></div>\n<p>Ensure Promtail has access, configure retention, ensure connectivity between hosts, and secure with TLS.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0530","card":"q-docker-070-centralized-logging","sec":"docker-s071","q":"In Docker logging, how does Loki differ from Elasticsearch in the ELK stack?","a":"<p>Loki indexes only labels (container, service, host) and stores the log text compressed in chunks, so it is cheaper and simpler to run, and you query with LogQL. Elasticsearch indexes the full text of each entry, which gives fast free-text search and analytics but needs more storage and operations effort. Loki fits Grafana-centred setups; Elasticsearch fits heavy search needs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0531","card":"q-docker-070-centralized-logging","sec":"docker-s071","q":"What are the two common ways to ship Docker container logs to a central system?","a":"<p>A logging driver in Docker (<code>fluentd</code>, <code>gelf</code>, <code>syslog</code>, <code>awslogs</code>) sends logs straight from the daemon, with little setup but a dependency on the endpoint. An agent (Promtail, Fluent Bit, Filebeat, or a sidecar) tails the log files or the Docker socket and forwards them, which gives buffering, parsing and enrichment, and keeps <code>docker logs</code> working. Agents are the more flexible choice.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0532","card":"q-docker-071-debugging-inside-containers","sec":"docker-s072","q":"How do you debug a container process with <code>strace</code> or <code>gdb</code>?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker exec -it &lt;container_name&gt; apt-get update &amp;&amp; apt-get install -y strace gdb\ndocker exec -it &lt;container_name&gt; strace -p $(pgrep process_name)\ndocker exec -it &lt;container_name&gt; gdb --pid=$(pgrep process_name)</code></pre></div>\n<p>Ensure the tools are available and avoid impacting production.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0533","card":"q-docker-071-debugging-inside-containers","sec":"docker-s072","q":"How do you confirm a container memory leak?","a":"<p>Use <code>valgrind</code> to detect leaks.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker exec -it &lt;container_name&gt; apt-get update &amp;&amp; apt-get install -y valgrind\ndocker exec -it &lt;container_name&gt; valgrind --leak-check=full --log-file=/tmp/valgrind.log process_name</code></pre></div>\n<p>Ensure debug symbols and account for overhead.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0534","card":"q-docker-071-debugging-inside-containers","sec":"docker-s072","q":"How do you extract context from cryptic errors?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker logs &lt;container_name&gt; --tail 100\ndocker inspect &lt;container_name&gt; | grep Env\ndocker exec &lt;container_name&gt; cat /path/to/config</code></pre></div>\n<p>Review logs, environment and configs. Correlate errors with settings and enable debug mode.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0535","card":"q-docker-071-debugging-inside-containers","sec":"docker-s072","q":"In Docker, why does <code>strace -p</code> often fail with \"Operation not permitted\" inside a container?","a":"<p>Containers drop the <code>SYS_PTRACE</code> capability by default, so tracing another process is blocked. Start the container with <code>--cap-add=SYS_PTRACE</code> (and for old Docker versions with a restrictive seccomp profile, adjust it). Use this on a test or a copy of the container, not as a permanent setting in production, since it weakens isolation.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0536","card":"q-docker-071-debugging-inside-containers","sec":"docker-s072","q":"In Docker, why is installing debug tools with <code>docker exec</code> into a running container a poor habit?","a":"<p>The tools exist only in that container and disappear when it is recreated. They enlarge its attack surface and change the state you are trying to debug. Better: keep a separate debug image with the tools and attach it to the target's namespaces (<code>--pid container:&lt;name&gt; --network container:&lt;name&gt;</code>), or reproduce the problem in a throwaway container with the tools added.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0537","card":"q-docker-071-debugging-inside-containers","sec":"docker-s072","q":"In Docker, how do you run a command as root inside a container whose image sets a non-root <code>USER</code>?","a":"<p>Use <code>docker exec -u 0 -it &lt;container&gt; sh</code> (or <code>-u root</code>). It starts the process as root in the same container, which is useful for installing a tool or reading a file during a debug session. The container itself still runs as the image's user.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0538","card":"q-docker-071-debugging-inside-containers","sec":"docker-s072","q":"How can you tell if a containerized service has a memory leak without special tools?","a":"<p>Run a constant load and watch the container's memory in <code>docker stats</code> or in Prometheus over time. A steady climb that never levels off and ends in an OOM kill suggests a leak; usage that rises then plateaus is a normal cache. Then confirm with the language's heap profiler (e.g. <code>pprof</code>, <code>tracemalloc</code>, a JVM heap dump) to see which objects grow.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0539","card":"q-docker-072-host-journal-logs","sec":"docker-s073","q":"How do you manage excessive journald logs?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>journalctl --disk-usage\njournalctl --vacuum-size=500M\nsudo vi /etc/systemd/journald.conf\n# Set: SystemMaxUse=500M\nsudo systemctl restart systemd-journald</code></pre></div>\n<p>Reduce log size and set limits. Balance retention and monitor after changes.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0540","card":"q-docker-072-host-journal-logs","sec":"docker-s073","q":"On a systemd host, where do the Docker daemon's own logs go and how do you read them?","a":"<p>The daemon's logs go to the journal. Read them with <code>journalctl -u docker</code> (add <code>-f</code> to follow, <code>--since \"1 hour ago\"</code> to narrow). They show daemon start-up, image pulls, network setup and container start failures, so they help when a container never starts and <code>docker logs</code> is empty.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0541","card":"q-docker-072-host-journal-logs","sec":"docker-s073","q":"In Docker, when the <code>journald</code> logging driver is used, how do you read one container's logs?","a":"<p>Docker stores container logs in the journal with fields such as <code>CONTAINER_NAME</code> and <code>CONTAINER_ID</code>. Filter with <code>journalctl CONTAINER_NAME=web</code>, or use <code>docker logs web</code>, which also works with this driver. The trade-off is that container logs then share the journal's size limits and retention with system logs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0542","card":"q-docker-073-restarting-and-restoring","sec":"docker-s074","q":"How do you auto-restart containers after a host crash?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>sudo systemctl enable docker\ndocker run --restart=always -d &lt;image_name&gt;\ndocker update --restart=always &lt;container_id&gt;</code></pre></div>\n<p>Use an appropriate restart policy, verify resources, and check logs.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0543","card":"q-docker-073-restarting-and-restoring","sec":"docker-s074","q":"How do you restore a critical containerized app?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker-compose -f docker-compose.yml up -d\ndocker run -d --name &lt;container_name&gt; -p &lt;port&gt;:&lt;port&gt; -v &lt;volume&gt; &lt;image_name&gt;\ndocker ps -a</code></pre></div>\n<p>Store configs, ensure the image and volume are available, and test restoration.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0544","card":"q-docker-073-restarting-and-restoring","sec":"docker-s074","q":"What does the <code>live-restore</code> option in Docker's <code>daemon.json</code> do?","a":"<p>With <code>\"live-restore\": true</code>, containers keep running while the Docker daemon is stopped or upgraded, and the daemon reconnects to them when it starts again. It reduces downtime for daemon upgrades and restarts. It is not compatible with Swarm mode, and some changes (such as network options) still need containers to be recreated.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0545","card":"q-docker-073-restarting-and-restoring","sec":"docker-s074","q":"What do RTO and RPO mean when planning recovery for Dockerized applications?","a":"<p>RTO (recovery time objective) is how long the application may be down. RPO (recovery point objective) is how much data you can afford to lose, measured in time since the last good backup. A tight RPO needs frequent backups or replication, and a tight RTO needs automation and standby capacity. These targets decide the backup and failover design.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0546","card":"q-docker-073-restarting-and-restoring","sec":"docker-s074","q":"To rebuild a Docker-based application on a new host after a failure, which things must exist outside the failed host?","a":"<p>Four things: the images (in a registry, not only on the host), the configuration (Compose files or deployment code in version control), the data (volume backups, database dumps or replicas), and the secrets (a secrets manager). With these, recovery is a scripted deployment and a restore. Containers themselves are not backed up.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0547","card":"q-docker-074-backups-and-restores","sec":"docker-s075","q":"How do you back up a Dockerized database?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker exec &lt;mysql_container&gt; mysqldump -u root -p&lt;password&gt; --all-databases &gt; backup.sql\nscp backup.sql user@remote_host:/backups/\necho &quot;0 2 * * * docker exec &lt;mysql_container&gt; mysqldump -u root -p&lt;password&gt; --all-databases &gt; /backups/backup_$(date +\\%F).sql&quot; | crontab -</code></pre></div>\n<p>Use app-specific tools, encrypt backups, and test restoration.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0548","card":"q-docker-074-backups-and-restores","sec":"docker-s075","q":"How do you restore a corrupted volume?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker stop &lt;container_id&gt;\ntar -xzf /backups/volume_backup.tar.gz -C /var/lib/docker/volumes/&lt;volume_name&gt;\ndocker start &lt;container_id&gt;\ndocker exec &lt;container_id&gt; ls /data</code></pre></div>\n<p>Back up regularly, verify integrity, and test the app after the restore.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0549","card":"q-docker-074-backups-and-restores","sec":"docker-s075","q":"How do you recover from filesystem corruption?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>sudo fsck /dev/sdX\nsudo apt remove docker.io\nsudo apt install docker.io\ndocker-compose -f docker-compose.yml up -d\ntar -xzf /backups/volume_backup.tar.gz -C /var/lib/docker/volumes/</code></pre></div>\n<p>Back up <code>/var/lib/docker</code>, verify the filesystem, and test containers.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0550","card":"q-docker-074-backups-and-restores","sec":"docker-s075","q":"Why use <code>mysqldump --single-transaction</code> when backing up a MySQL container?","a":"<p>For InnoDB tables, it takes the dump inside one consistent transaction snapshot without locking tables, so the application keeps working and the dump is consistent. It does not give a consistent copy for MyISAM tables, which need locks. For large databases, physical backup tools (Percona XtraBackup, snapshots) are faster.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0551","card":"q-docker-074-backups-and-restores","sec":"docker-s075","q":"What is wrong with <code>mysqldump -u root -p&lt;password&gt;</code> in a backup script?","a":"<p>The password appears on the command line, so any user on the host can read it in the process list, and it can end up in shell history and logs. Pass it through an option file or the <code>MYSQL_PWD</code> environment variable, or a secrets mechanism, and restrict who can read the script.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0552","card":"q-docker-074-backups-and-restores","sec":"docker-s075","q":"Why does a cron job that runs <code>docker exec -it db mysqldump ... &gt; backup.sql</code> produce a broken or failing backup?","a":"<p><code>-t</code> allocates a pseudo-TTY, which cron does not have (it fails with \"the input device is not a TTY\"), and when it does exist the TTY adds carriage returns that corrupt the dump. In scripts use <code>docker exec</code> without <code>-t</code> (and <code>-i</code> only when feeding input).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0553","card":"q-docker-074-backups-and-restores","sec":"docker-s075","q":"How do you back up a PostgreSQL database that runs in a container?","a":"<p>Use the database's own tools: <code>docker exec db pg_dump -U user dbname &gt; dump.sql</code> for a logical dump (or <code>pg_dumpall</code> for the whole server), or <code>pg_basebackup</code> for a physical backup with WAL archiving for point-in-time recovery. Copying the data directory of a running database gives an inconsistent backup. Store backups off the host and test restoring them.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0554","card":"q-docker-074-backups-and-restores","sec":"docker-s075","q":"For a Dockerized database, why is a backup not proven until you have tested a restore?","a":"<p>A backup can be empty, truncated, encrypted with a lost key, incompatible with the new version or missing parts, and you only find out when you need it. Schedule regular restore tests into a separate environment, check that the app starts and the data is correct, and measure the time against your recovery time objective. Keep copies in more than one place (the 3-2-1 rule).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0555","card":"q-docker-075-swarm-recovery","sec":"docker-s076","q":"How do you recover a Swarm cluster after manager loss?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker node ls\ndocker node promote &lt;worker_node_id&gt;\ndocker swarm init --force-new-cluster --advertise-addr &lt;manager_ip&gt;:2377\ndocker swarm join --token &lt;worker_token&gt; &lt;manager_ip&gt;:2377</code></pre></div>\n<p>Maintain an odd number of managers, back up Swarm state, and verify health.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0556","card":"q-docker-075-swarm-recovery","sec":"docker-s076","q":"How do you simulate a Docker host failure?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>sudo systemctl stop docker\ndocker node update --availability drain &lt;node_id&gt;\ndocker service ps &lt;service_name&gt;\nsudo systemctl start docker\ndocker node update --availability active &lt;node_id&gt;</code></pre></div>\n<p>Ensure replicas exist, test backups, monitor availability, and document the steps.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0557","card":"q-docker-075-swarm-recovery","sec":"docker-s076","q":"What does <code>docker swarm init --autolock</code> protect against and what must you keep?","a":"<p>With autolock, the keys that encrypt the manager's Raft logs on disk (and the TLS keys) are themselves encrypted, so someone who steals a manager's disk cannot read the cluster secrets. After a manager restarts it stays locked until you run <code>docker swarm unlock</code> and give the unlock key. Store that key safely outside the cluster, because losing it can mean you cannot recover the manager.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0558","card":"q-docker-075-swarm-recovery","sec":"docker-s076","q":"How do you back up the state of a Docker Swarm manager?","a":"<p>Stop Docker on a manager (to get a consistent copy; with three or more managers the cluster keeps running), then archive <code>/var/lib/docker/swarm</code> (Raft data, certificates, secrets), and start Docker again. Keep the autolock key if enabled. To restore, put the files on a new node, then run <code>docker swarm init --force-new-cluster</code>. Back up application volumes separately.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0559","card":"q-docker-075-swarm-recovery","sec":"docker-s076","q":"When you test failure in Docker Swarm, how is stopping Docker on a node different from draining it?","a":"<p>Draining is a planned action: Swarm moves tasks away and the node stays reachable. Stopping Docker (or powering the node off) simulates a real failure: Swarm notices the node is down after missed heartbeats and then reschedules its tasks, so you see detection time and the real recovery behaviour. Run both when you test.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0560","card":"q-docker-076-malware-recovery","sec":"docker-s077","q":"How do you recover a Docker host from malware?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>sudo ip link set eth0 down\ndocker stop $(docker ps -q)\nsudo apt install clamav\nclamscan -r / --remove\nsudo apt update &amp;&amp; sudo apt upgrade -y\nsudo systemctl restart docker\ndocker start $(docker ps -a -q)</code></pre></div>\n<p>Block malicious traffic, scan images, and restore from clean backups.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0561","card":"q-docker-076-malware-recovery","sec":"docker-s077","q":"Why is it better to rebuild a malware-infected Docker host from a known-good image than to clean it with an antivirus scan?","a":"<p>Once an attacker has root on a host, you cannot trust the tools or the kernel on it: rootkits can hide and survive a scan, and you cannot be sure everything was removed. Rebuild from a trusted image or a golden AMI, redeploy containers from clean images, restore data from a backup taken before the infection, and rotate all credentials the host could access.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0562","card":"q-docker-077-high-availability-and-failover","sec":"docker-s078","q":"How do you ensure high availability?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker swarm init\ndocker service create --name app --replicas 3 -p 80:80 &lt;image_name&gt;\nHEALTHCHECK --interval=30s --timeout=3s CMD curl -f http://localhost/ || exit 1\ndocker service update --health-cmd=&quot;curl -f http://localhost/ || exit 1&quot; --health-interval=30s app</code></pre></div>\n<p>Use multiple nodes, a load balancer, and health monitoring.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0563","card":"q-docker-077-high-availability-and-failover","sec":"docker-s078","q":"How do you implement failover for frequent outages?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>docker swarm init\ndocker service create --name app --replicas 3 -p 80:80 &lt;image_name&gt;</code></pre></div>\n<p>NGINX config:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Nginx</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>upstream app {\n    server &lt;node1_ip&gt;:80;\n    server &lt;node2_ip&gt;:80;\n    server &lt;node3_ip&gt;:80;\n}\nserver { listen 80; location / { proxy_pass http://app; } }</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>sudo systemctl reload nginx</code></pre></div>\n<p>Spread replicas, use health checks, and test failover.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"docker-f0564","card":"q-docker-077-high-availability-and-failover","sec":"docker-s078","q":"What does high availability for a containerized app mean at each layer?","a":"<p>There must be no single point of failure at any layer: several app replicas on different nodes or availability zones, a redundant load balancer (managed, or two with a floating IP), a replicated database with failover, and shared or replicated storage. Health checks detect failures and traffic moves away on its own. One container on one host is not highly available however good its restart policy is.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0565","card":"q-docker-077-high-availability-and-failover","sec":"docker-s078","q":"What is the difference between active-active and active-passive failover?","a":"<p>In active-active, all instances serve traffic at once, so capacity is used and failure of one only reduces capacity. In active-passive, one instance serves and a standby takes over when it fails, which is simpler for stateful parts such as a primary database but wastes idle capacity and has a failover delay. Stateless web tiers are usually active-active.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0566","card":"q-docker-077-high-availability-and-failover","sec":"docker-s078","q":"An Nginx container load-balances three app containers. Why is it still a single point of failure, and how do you fix it?","a":"<p>The Nginx container itself can fail, and then nothing is reachable. Run two or more Nginx instances behind a managed load balancer (such as an AWS ALB) or share a virtual IP using keepalived (VRRP). Then the load balancing layer survives the loss of a node.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0567","card":"q-docker-077-high-availability-and-failover","sec":"docker-s078","q":"In Docker Swarm, how do you spread replicas of a service across availability zones?","a":"<p>Label nodes with their zone (<code>docker node update --label-add zone=a &lt;node&gt;</code>) and use a placement preference: <code>docker service create --placement-pref 'spread=node.labels.zone' ...</code>. Swarm distributes tasks evenly across the label values, so losing a zone loses only a share of the replicas. This is a preference and not a hard rule, and constraints are separate.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"docker-f0568","card":"q-docker-077-high-availability-and-failover","sec":"docker-s078","q":"Why should state be separated from containers when you design for failover?","a":"<p>Containers are replaceable, but data is not. If the app is stateless and its data sits in a replicated database or shared storage (e.g. RDS Multi-AZ, EFS, S3), any container on any node can take over, and failover is only starting a new container. A database in a single container on one host's local disk cannot fail over without data loss.</p>","hand":true}];
